var tafsirData_12 = {
  "1": "\"Alif-Lãm-Ra\" opens this chapter with the same three disconnected letters that began the previous one, recited individually rather than as a word. Several chapters share this feature, and the honest position taken by many early scholars is that their complete meaning rests with Allah. A frequently made observation is that these are ordinary letters of the Arabic alphabet, the same building blocks the Arabs used in their celebrated poetry, placed at the head of a Book they were challenged to imitate and could not match. The materials were entirely familiar; the result was not.\n\n\"These are the verses of the clear Book\" introduces what follows. The Arabic word \"mubin\" carries two related meanings that both apply. It describes something that is itself clear, plain, and unambiguous, and it describes something that makes other things clear. Both fit the Quran, which is clear in itself and clarifies matters that human beings could never settle on their own: the purpose of creation, the nature of God, and what happens after death.\n\nThe description is especially fitting at the start of this particular chapter. What follows is the story of Joseph, told in a single continuous narrative from beginning to end, which is unusual in the Quran. Most prophetic accounts are distributed across different chapters and told in pieces, each time emphasising what suits the context. Here the account runs unbroken through more than a hundred verses.\n\nThat clarity of storytelling is part of the point. Nothing in the chapter is obscure. A listener with no religious training can follow the events of a boy's dream, his brothers' jealousy, the well, the caravan, the house of the Egyptian minister, the prison, and the eventual reunion. The lessons emerge from the events themselves rather than from abstract argument.\n\nComing immediately after a chapter filled with warnings and destroyed nations, this opening signals a change in method. The previous chapter argued; this one narrates, and the reader is about to be taught through a story rather than through a series of confrontations.",
  "2": "\"Indeed, We have sent it down as an Arabic Quran so that you may understand\" explains why the revelation came in the language it did, and the reason given is entirely practical: comprehension.\n\nThe Arabic wording states the purpose plainly. A message intended to guide a people must reach them in words they actually use. Revelation in an unfamiliar language would have been an obstacle rather than a mercy, and Surah Fussilat makes exactly this point, noting that had it been revealed as a non-Arabic Quran, they would have objected that its verses were not made clear in their own language.\n\nThis connects with a broader principle the Quran states elsewhere, that no messenger was sent except in the language of his own people, so that he could make things clear to them. The first audience for any revelation must be able to understand it without an interpreter standing between them and their Lord.\n\nMentioning the Arabic language here also relates to the challenge running through the previous chapters. The Quran was delivered in the language its opponents had mastered, in a society that prized eloquence above almost everything, and they were invited to produce something comparable. Choosing their own field of expertise made the challenge sharper.\n\nThere is a question this verse raises for the vast majority of Muslims today, who do not speak Arabic. If the purpose of the Arabic Quran is understanding, what does that mean for a Nigerian, Indonesian, or English-speaking believer? The answer is that the verse establishes understanding as the goal, which makes the effort to understand a religious duty rather than an optional extra. Translation, study, and learning something of the original language are all ways of pursuing what this verse names as the purpose. Reciting without any attempt at comprehension falls short of what the verse itself describes as the reason for revelation.\n\nPlacing this statement just before the story of Joseph is fitting. The chapter is about to deliver a long narrative whose value depends entirely on the listener following what happens and why.",
  "3": "\"We relate to you ˹O Prophet˺ the best of stories through Our revelation of this Quran\" introduces the narrative that fills this chapter, and the description given to it is unusual. The Arabic phrase indicates the most beautiful or finest of accounts, and commentators have explained the description in several complementary ways.\n\nIt may be the best because of how completely it is told, running from a boy's dream to its fulfilment decades later without interruption. It may be the best because of the range of human experience it contains: jealousy between brothers, a father's grief, temptation, false accusation, imprisonment, the interpretation of dreams, the rise to authority, and finally forgiveness. It may also be described this way because of the lessons packed into it, since almost every moral situation a person can face appears somewhere in its course.\n\nWhat should be clear is what the description does not mean. It is not a story in the sense of something invented. The chapter is presenting real events, and the word used for relating them indicates recounting something that occurred.\n\n\"Though before this you were totally unaware ˹of them˺\" makes a claim that was open to immediate contradiction. The Prophet had never studied scripture, could not read, and had spent his life in Mecca among people equally unfamiliar with these accounts in detail. The version of Joseph's story preserved among the Jewish communities of the region differs in significant ways from what this chapter presents, which makes the suggestion of borrowing difficult to sustain.\n\nThere is also a reported context for this chapter's revelation, mentioned by commentators, that some of the Prophet's companions asked him to relate something to them, or that questioners tested him about the story of the Children of Israel who went to Egypt. Such reports vary in strength, and the chapter itself does not depend on them.\n\nWhat the verse establishes is that this narrative arrived as revelation, delivered to a man who had no access to it by ordinary means.",
  "4": "“˹Remember˺ when Joseph said to his father, “O my dear father! Indeed I dreamt of eleven stars, and the sun, and the moon—I saw them prostrating to me!”” opens the story that the Quran calls the best of narratives, and the commentators note the dream and its meaning. The verse records the words of the young Joseph to his father Jacob: “O my dear father! Indeed I dreamt of eleven stars, and the sun, and the moon—I saw them prostrating to me!” or “ya abati innee ra’aytu ahada ‘ashara kawkaban wash-shamsa wal-qamara ra’aytuhum lee sajideen,” and the commentators explain the dream, for the eleven stars are the eleven brothers of Joseph, and the sun and the moon are his parents, and the prostration of the heavenly bodies to Joseph is the vision of the honour that the Lord had destined for him, the prophecy that the family would one day bow before him in the prostration of the greeting and the honour, not the worship. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the dream is the first thread of the whole story, and the fulfillment of the dream at the end of the chapter is the proof that the vision was the prophecy, and the commentators note that the dream came to Joseph in his childhood, and the telling of the dream to the father is the first act of the story, the trust of the son in the father that the next verse will answer with the caution. Al-Sa’di observes that the dream teaches the believer the reality of the divine vision, and the prostration of the stars and the sun and the moon is the symbol of the elevation that the Lord grants to the one whom He chooses, and the story that unfolds is the story of how the vision was fulfilled through the trials that the chapters will trace.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the dream, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the visions of the divine are the signs of the future, and the dream of the young is the prophecy of the man, and the one whom the Lord chooses for the honour is the one whom the trials will test and the honour will find, and the believer who receives the sign of the future must hold to it through the trials that the future brings. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the opening of the “best of narratives,” and the believer who reads the story of Joseph reads the story of the dream that the trials could not cancel, and the fulfillment of the dream is the assurance that the promise of the Lord is the truth. The verse is the opening of the story, and the teaching of the dream that the Lord fulfills.",
  "5": "“He replied, “O my dear son! Do not relate your vision to your brothers, or they will devise a plot against you. Surely Satan is a sworn enemy to humankind.”” records the caution of Jacob, and the commentators note the two parts of the advice. The first is the caution: “Do not relate your vision to your brothers, or they will devise a plot against you,” or “ya bunayya la taqsus ru’yaka ‘ala ikhwatika fa-yakeedoo laka kaydan,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for Jacob, who knew the hearts of his sons, feared that the telling of the dream would stir the envy of the brothers, and the envy would devise the plot against Joseph, and the caution is the wisdom of the father, the protection of the son from the envy that the dream would provoke, and the word “kayd,” the plot, the scheme, is the key, for the plot of the brothers is the engine of the whole story, and the caution of the father is the first warning against it. Ibn Kathir notes the wisdom, for Jacob discerned the meaning of the dream and feared the envy, and the fear was the prophecy, for the brothers did plot, and the plot is the trial that the dream had foreseen. The second part is the warning against Satan: “Surely Satan is a sworn enemy to humankind,” or “inna ash-shaytana lil-insani ‘aduwwun mubeen,” and the commentators note the meaning, for Jacob attributed the envy and the plot to the enmity of Satan, and the warning is the teaching that the enmity of Satan is the root of the evil, and the believer who knows the enemy is the believer who guards against the envy that the enemy stirs, and the word “mubeen,” the clear, the manifest, is the description of the enmity, the declaration that the enemy is not hidden but open.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the caution, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own speech. The believer is taught that the blessing is sometimes the cause of the envy, and the telling of the blessing to the envious is the invitation of the plot, and the believer who guards the blessing from the envious is the believer who follows the wisdom of Jacob, and the enmity of Satan is the warning that the believer must always remember, for the enemy who stirs the envy is the enemy whose plots the believer must anticipate. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the wisdom of the concealment: Jacob cautioned the son, and the caution is the teaching that the blessing is guarded, not flaunted, and the believer who shares the blessing with the wrong ear has exposed the blessing to the envy, and the enmity of Satan is the ground of the caution. The verse is the caution of the father, and the teaching of the wisdom that guards the blessing.",
  "6": "“And so will your Lord choose you ˹O Joseph˺, and teach you the interpretation of dreams, and perfect His favour upon you and the descendants of Jacob—˹just˺ as He once perfected it upon your forefathers, Abraham and Isaac. Surely your Lord is All-Knowing, All-Wise.”” reveals the meaning of the dream, and the commentators note the three promises that the verse joins. The first is the choosing: “And so will your Lord choose you,” or “wa kathalika yajtabeeka rabbuka,” and the commentators explain the “ijtiba’,” the choosing, the selecting, for the Lord would choose Joseph, and the choosing is the election of the prophet, the honour that the dream had foreseen, and the word is the declaration that the trials that were coming were the path of the election. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the verse reveals to Joseph, through the words of the father, the meaning of the dream, and the meaning is the election, the choosing that the prostration of the stars had symbolized. The second is the teaching: “and teach you the interpretation of dreams,” or “wa yu‘allimuka min ta’weeli al-ahadeeth,” and the commentators explain the “ta’wil,” the interpretation, for the Lord would teach Joseph the science of the interpretation of the dreams, and the teaching is the gift that the story will display, the wisdom that would bring Joseph from the prison to the palace. The third is the completion of the favour: “and perfect His favour upon you and the descendants of Jacob—just as He once perfected it upon your forefathers, Abraham and Isaac,” or “wa yutimmu ni‘matahu ‘alayka wa ‘ala ali Ya‘qooba kama atammaha ‘ala abawayka min qablu Ibraheema wa Ishaqa,” and the commentators note the promise, for the favour of the Lord would be completed upon Joseph and upon the house of Jacob, as it had been completed upon Abraham and Isaac, and the promise is the continuity of the blessing, the line of the prophecy that ran from the fathers to the son. The verse closes with the two names: “Surely your Lord is All-Knowing, All-Wise,” or “inna rabbaka ‘aleemun hakeem,” and the commentators note the closing, for the All-Knowing knows the hearts of the brothers and the end of the story, and the All-Wise arranges the trials and the triumphs in the perfect order.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the election, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trials. The believer is taught that the choosing of the Lord is the meaning of the trials, and the one whom the Lord elects is the one whom the trials will test and the favour will crown, and the teaching of the interpretation is the gift that the patience earns, and the completion of the favour is the promise that the believer inherits, the blessing that runs from the fathers to the children. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the hidden meaning of the difficulty: the dream was the prophecy of the honour, and the trials were the path of the honour, and the believer who reads the promise of the Lord in the trial is the believer whose patience the fulfilment finds. The verse is the revelation of the meaning, and the teaching of the election that the trials serve.",
  "7": "“Indeed, in the story of Joseph and his brothers there are lessons for all who ask.”” announces the purpose of the narrative, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the purpose: “Indeed, in the story of Joseph and his brothers there are lessons for all who ask,” or “laqad kana fee Yoosufa wa ikhwatihi ayatun lis-sa’ileen,” and the commentators explain the “ayat,” the signs, the lessons, and the “sa’ileen,” the askers, the seekers, for the story of Joseph and his brothers is the storehouse of the signs, the lessons that the seeker extracts, and the word “sa’ileen” is the condition, for the lessons are for the ones who ask, the seekers of the truth who approach the story with the question, and the verse is the declaration that the narrative is not the entertainment but the teaching, the history that the believer reads for the guidance. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the story of Joseph is the most detailed of the Quran’s narratives, and the detail is the proof of the abundance of the lessons, and the lessons are for the askers, the ones who seek the guidance from the story, and the verse is the invitation to the seeking. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the manner of reading the story: the narrative is the mine, and the asker is the miner, and the one who asks the story for the lessons is the one who extracts the gold, and the one who reads it as the tale is the one who misses the treasure.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the seeking, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reading. The believer is taught that the stories of the Quran are the signs, and the signs are for the seekers, and the believer who asks the story for the lessons is the believer who finds them, and the lessons of Joseph are the lessons of the trials and the patience and the forgiveness and the trust, and the one who asks is the one whom the story guides. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the key to the whole chapter: the story is the “ayah,” the sign, and the sign is for the “sa’il,” the asker, and the believer who enters the story with the question enters the mine, and the question is the shovel. The verse is the announcement of the purpose, and the teaching of the seeking that the story rewards.",
  "8": "“˹Remember˺ when they said ˹to one another˺, “Surely Joseph and his brother ˹Benjamin˺ are more beloved to our father than we, even though we are a group of so many. Indeed, our father is clearly mistaken.”” records the first murmur of the brothers, and the commentators note the two claims and their root. The verse records the talk of the brothers: “Surely Joseph and his brother Benjamin are more beloved to our father than we, even though we are a group of so many,” or “ith qaloo la-Yoosufu wa akhoohu ahabbu ila abeena minna wa nahnu ‘usbah,” and the commentators explain the complaint, for the brothers saw that Jacob loved Joseph and his full brother Benjamin more than the rest, and the perception of the favouritism is the seed of the envy, and the word “‘usbah,” the group, the band, is the key, for the brothers argued that they were the many and the two were the two, and the many should have been the beloved, and the argument is the logic of the envy, the measurement of the love by the numbers. Ibn Kathir notes the context, for Joseph and Benjamin were the sons of the same mother, and the other brothers were the sons of the other wives, and the special love of Jacob for the two was the favouritism that the envy seized upon, and the envy is the engine of the plot that follows. The verse then records the verdict: “Indeed, our father is clearly mistaken,” or “inna abana la-fee dalalin mubeen,” and the commentators note the word “dalal,” the error, the misguidance, for the brothers judged their father to be in the clear error, and the judgment of the father is the first step of the rebellion, the accusation that justified the plot, and the word “mubeen,” the clear, is the measure of the arrogance, the declaration that the father’s error was the obvious, and the verse preserves the judgment as the diagnosis of the envy that had blinded the brothers to the truth.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the envy, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own heart. The believer is taught that the envy begins with the perception of the favouritism, and the perception is often the distortion, for the love of the father was not the injustice but the affection, and the brothers who measured the love by the numbers were the brothers whose envy had distorted the measure, and the believer who envies the blessing of another is the believer whose heart the envy has blinded to the truth, and the judgment of the father as the mistaken is the judgment that the envy pronounces upon the truth. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the anatomy of the envy: the perception, the comparison, the verdict, and the plot, and the believer who sees the four stages in the brothers is the believer who guards the heart against the first, for the envy that is not checked at the perception becomes the plot that the story will record. The verse is the record of the murmur, and the teaching of the envy that the story will trace to its end.",
  "9": "“Kill Joseph or cast him out to some ˹distant˺ land so that your father’s attention will be only yours, then after that you may ˹repent and˺ become righteous people!”” records the first proposal, and the commentators note the two options and the self-deception. The verse records the plot: “Kill Joseph or cast him out to some distant land,” or “uqtuloo Yoosufa awi itrahoohu ardan,” and the commentators explain the two options, the killing and the casting out, and the two are the two ways of removing the rival, and the purpose is stated: “so that your father’s attention will be only yours,” or “yakhlu lakum wajhu abeekum,” and the commentators note the phrase, for the removal of Joseph would free the face of the father for the brothers, and the purpose is the capture of the love, the clearing of the rival that the envy demanded. Ibn Kathir notes the gravity, for the brothers proposed the killing of the brother for the sake of the father’s love, and the proposal is the measure of the envy, the willingness to shed the blood for the affection, and the word “uqtuloo” — kill — is the first of the crimes that the story records. The verse then records the self-deception: “then after that you may repent and become righteous people!” or “wa takoonoo min ba‘dihi qawman saliheen,” and the commentators note the irony, for the brothers proposed to commit the crime and then to repent, and the proposal is the self-deception of the sinner, the illusion that the repentance after the deliberate sin would be accepted, and the commentators note the teaching, for the repentance that is planned before the sin is not the repentance but the hypocrisy, and the proposal of the brothers is the warning against the sin that the sinner hopes to repent of, and the self-deception is the second crime, the plan of the sin and the repentance together.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the self-deception, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own sin. The believer is taught that the sin that is planned with the intention of the repentance is the sin that the repentance cannot wash, and the one who says “I will sin and then repent” has deceived himself, for the repentance that is the condition of the sin is not the repentance but the calculation, and the believer who guards the heart against the planned sin is the believer whose repentance is the sincere, and the proposal of the brothers is the warning against the calculation that the falsehood loves. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the corruption of the planned sin: the brothers proposed the killing and the repentance, and the proposal is the double crime, and the believer who avoids the first has avoided the second, for the sin and the planned repentance are the two halves of the single hypocrisy. The verse is the record of the proposal, and the teaching of the self-deception that the sin plans.",
  "10": "“One of them said, “Do not kill Joseph. But if you must do something, throw him into the bottom of a well so perhaps he may be picked up by some travellers.”” records the moderating proposal, and the commentators note the two parts of the suggestion. The first is the restraint: “Do not kill Joseph,” or “la taqtuloo Yoosufa,” and the commentators note the voice, for one of the brothers, whom the commentators often identify as Judah, the eldest or the most reasonable, proposed the restraint, and the restraint is the first mercy within the plot, the suggestion that the killing be replaced by the lesser harm, and the voice of the restraint is the thread of the conscience that the envy had not entirely silenced. Ibn Kathir notes the identification, for the one who spoke was Judah, and the restraint is the proof that the hearts of the brothers were not entirely the stone, and the voice is the first step of the repentance that the end of the story will complete. The second part is the proposal: “But if you must do something, throw him into the bottom of a well so perhaps he may be picked up by some travellers,” or “wa alqoohu fee ghayabati al-jubbi yaltaqit-hu ba‘du as-sayyarati in kuntum fa‘ileen,” and the commentators explain the proposal, for the throwing of Joseph into the well, the “ghayabat al-jubb,” the depths of the pit, was the alternative to the killing, and the hope that the travellers would pick him up, and the proposal is the compromise that the brothers accepted, and the well becomes the first stage of the journey that the Lord had destined for Joseph, and the commentators note the irony, for the well that the brothers chose as the grave became the first step of the elevation, and the travellers that they hoped would remove him were the means of his journey to Egypt.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the restraint, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own mercy. The believer is taught that the voice of the restraint in the midst of the evil is the voice of the conscience, and the one who restrains the worse is the one who has kept the thread of the mercy, and the believer who is drawn into the plot of the others must speak the restraint, the word that softens the evil, and the word of the restraint is the first step of the return. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the value of the lesser harm: the brother who proposed the well instead of the killing saved the life, and the believer who can turn the evil toward the lesser is the believer whose mercy the Lord will complete, and the well of Joseph is the witness that the lesser harm is the mercy that the plot did not intend. The verse is the record of the restraint, and the teaching of the mercy that the conscience speaks.",
  "11": "“They said, “O our father! Why do you not trust us with Joseph, although we truly wish him well?”” records the first approach to the father, and the commentators note the two parts of the appeal. The verse records the appeal: “O our father! Why do you not trust us with Joseph, although we truly wish him well?” or “qaloo ya abana ma laka la ta’manna ‘ala Yoosufa wa inna lahu la-nasihoon,” and the commentators explain the appeal, for the brothers asked their father why he did not entrust Joseph to them, and the question is the first step of the plot, the request of the permission that would give them the chance to act, and the appeal is dressed in the garments of the sincerity, the claim that they wished Joseph well, and the claim is the deception, the “nasihoon,” the sincere advisors, that the plot wore. Ibn Kathir notes the irony, for the brothers who had plotted the removal of Joseph now claimed the sincerity, and the claim is the mask of the plot, the false sincerity that the verse preserves, and the word “nasihoon” is the key, for the sincere advisor is the title that the deceiver claims, and the claim is the measure of the deception. The verse is the first of the two verses of the approach, and the commentators note that the two together are the complete request, the question of the trust and the proposal of the outing, and the two are the preparation of the crime that the father’s permission would enable.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the false sincerity, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own discernment. The believer is taught that the falsehood wears the garments of the sincerity, and the claim of the well-wishing is the mask of the plot, and the believer who discerns the mask is the believer whom the deception cannot take, and the discernment is the guard that the believer must keep, for the enemy who claims the sincerity is the enemy whose plot the sincerity conceals. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the danger of the false claim: the brothers claimed the sincerity and plotted the crime, and the believer who trusts every claim of the sincerity is the believer whom the claim deceives, and the discernment of the heart is the shield that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the appeal, and the teaching of the discernment that the false sincerity demands.",
  "12": "“Send him out with us tomorrow so that he may enjoy himself and play. And we will really watch over him.”” completes the request, and the commentators note the two promises and their irony. The verse records the proposal: “Send him out with us tomorrow so that he may enjoy himself and play,” or “arsilhu ma‘ana ghadan yarta‘ wa yal‘ab,” and the commentators explain the proposal, for the brothers asked that Joseph be sent with them the next day to enjoy himself and play, and the promise of the enjoyment is the bait, the appeal to the father’s love for the son, and the word “yarta‘,” the enjoying, the playing, is the description of the outing that the brothers proposed, and the proposal is the second step of the plot, the obtaining of the permission that would deliver Joseph into their hands. Ibn Kathir notes the irony, for the brothers who proposed the enjoyment were the brothers who had proposed the killing, and the promise of the play was the mask of the plot, and the irony is the measure of the deception. The verse then records the second promise: “And we will really watch over him,” or “wa inna lahu la-hafizoon,” and the commentators note the word “hafizoon,” the keepers, the guardians, for the brothers promised to guard Joseph, and the promise of the guarding is the most bitter of the ironies, for the guardians were the plotters, and the promise is the deception that the story will expose, and the verse preserves the promise as the witness against the brothers, the declaration that the ones who swore to guard were the ones who cast the boy into the well.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the broken promise, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the promise of the guardian is the promise that the falsehood breaks, and the brothers who swore to guard were the brothers who betrayed, and the believer who is entrusted to the guardians must know that the guard of the Lord is the only guard that holds, and the promise of the creature is the promise that the creature can break. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the fragility of the creature’s promise: the brothers swore to guard and cast into the well, and the believer who trusts the promise of the creature without the guard of the Lord is the believer whom the promise betrays, and the story of Joseph is the proof that the Lord guards the one whom the creatures abandon. The verse is the completion of the request, and the teaching of the promise that the plot betrayed.",
  "13": "“He responded, “It would truly sadden me if you took him away with you, and I fear that a wolf may devour him while you are negligent of him.”” records the answer of Jacob, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The first is the sorrow: “It would truly sadden me if you took him away with you,” or “qala innee la-yahzununee an tathhaboo bihi,” and the commentators explain the sorrow, for Jacob expressed the sadness that the separation from Joseph would cause, and the sorrow is the love of the father, the attachment of the heart to the son, and the expression of the sorrow is the first sign of the reluctance, the hesitation that the love produced, and the verse preserves the sorrow as the measure of the affection that the plot would wound. Ibn Kathir notes the tenderness, for the sorrow of Jacob is the love of the father, and the love is the root of the whole story, the affection that the envy of the brothers attacked and the patience of the father endured. The second part is the fear: “and I fear that a wolf may devour him while you are negligent of him,” or “wa akhafu an ya’kulahu ath-thi’bu wa antum ‘anhu ghafiloon,” and the commentators note the fear, for Jacob feared that a wolf would devour Joseph while the brothers were heedless, and the fear is the prophecy, for the wolf would be the excuse that the brothers would invent, and the commentators note the irony, for the father’s fear of the wolf was the very lie that the brothers would tell, and the fear is the thread that the deception would seize, and the verse preserves the fear as the witness of the father’s insight and the brothers’ deceit.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the fear, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own intuition. The believer is taught that the intuition of the loving heart is often the prophecy, and the fear of Jacob was the foreboding that the events would confirm, and the believer who listens to the intuition of the love is the believer whose caution the events vindicate, and the fear of the wolf is the thread that the lie of the brothers would use, and the believer who discerns the thread is the believer whom the lie cannot deceive. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the value of the intuition: Jacob feared the wolf and the wolf was the lie, and the believer who trusts the caution of the heart is the believer whose caution the truth confirms, and the sorrow of the father is the love that the plot could not kill. The verse is the record of the answer, and the teaching of the intuition that the love supplies.",
  "14": "“They said, “If a wolf were to devour him, despite our strong group, then we would certainly be losers!”” records the reply of the brothers, and the commentators note the two parts of the argument. The verse records the reply: “If a wolf were to devour him, despite our strong group, then we would certainly be losers!” or “qaloo la-in akalahu ath-thi’bu wa nahnu ‘usbatun inna ithan la-khasiroon,” and the commentators explain the argument, for the brothers answered the fear of the father with the assurance of the strength, the claim that the wolf could not devour Joseph while the “usbah,” the strong group, was with him, and the assurance is the deception, the same group that had plotted the removal now swearing the protection, and the argument is the irony that the verse preserves, for the strength that they cited was the strength that would be used against Joseph, not for him. Ibn Kathir notes the irony, for the brothers swore that the wolf could not devour Joseph while they were the group, and the wolf was the lie that they themselves would invent, and the oath is the measure of the deceit, the swearing of the false protection that the plot would betray. The verse is the last of the approach, and the commentators note that the three verses together — the question, the proposal, and the assurance — are the complete obtaining of the permission, the plot dressed in the garments of the care, and the permission that the father granted was the permission that the plot had sought.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the false assurance, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own discernment. The believer is taught that the assurance of the group is the assurance that the falsehood gives, and the ones who swear the protection are the ones who plan the harm, and the believer who trusts the assurance of the creature is the believer whom the assurance deceives, and the discernment of the heart is the shield against the false oath, the oath that the plot swears and the act breaks. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the fragility of the creature’s oath: the brothers swore the protection and plotted the harm, and the believer who leans on the oath of the creature is the believer whom the oath betrays, and the story of Joseph is the teaching that the guard of the Lord is the only guard that the believer must trust. The verse is the record of the assurance, and the teaching of the oath that the plot broke.",
  "15": "“And so, when they took him away and decided to throw him into the bottom of the well, We inspired him: “˹One day˺ you will remind them of this deed of theirs while they are unaware ˹of who you are˺.”” records the crime and the first comfort, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the crime: “when they took him away and decided to throw him into the bottom of the well,” or “fa-lamma thahaboo bihi wa ajma‘oo an yaj‘aloohu fee ghayabati al-jubbi,” and the commentators describe the scene, for the brothers took Joseph and agreed to cast him into the depths of the well, and the agreement is the execution of the plot, the deed that the envy had planned, and the commentators note the detail from the reports, for Joseph clung to the garments of the brothers and was stripped and cast down, and the casting into the well is the first of the trials, the darkness of the pit that the Lord would turn to the first step of the journey. Ibn Kathir relates the detail, for Joseph, when cast, was inspired with the comfort, and the inspiration is the second part: “We inspired him: ‘One day you will remind them of this deed of theirs while they are unaware of who you are,’” or “wa awhayna ilayhi la-tunabbi’annahum bi-amrihim hatha wa hum la yash‘uroon,” and the commentators note the inspiration, for the Lord comforted the boy in the well with the prophecy, the promise that he would one day remind the brothers of the deed while they did not know him, and the promise is the first light in the darkness, the assurance that the Lord had not abandoned the cast-away, and the prophecy is the thread that runs through the whole story, the promise that the end of the chapter fulfills when the brothers stand before Joseph and do not know him.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the comfort, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own darkness. The believer is taught that the darkness of the well is the place of the inspiration, and the one who is cast into the pit by the envy of the world is the one whom the Lord comforts with the promise, and the promise of the future is the light that the believer carries in the darkness, and the Lord who inspired Joseph in the well is the Lord who inspires the believer in the trial, and the prophecy of the reminder is the assurance that the wrong will be righted and the truth will be known. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the nearness of the Lord in the trial: Joseph was cast and inspired, and the casting and the inspiration are the two sides of the single event, and the believer who is cast by the world is the believer whom the Lord inspires, and the promise of the Lord is the comfort that the pit cannot extinguish. The verse is the record of the crime and the comfort, and the teaching of the inspiration that the darkness carries.",
  "16": "“Then they returned to their father in the evening, weeping.”” records the return and the tears, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse states the return and the weeping: “Then they returned to their father in the evening, weeping,” or “wa ja’oo abahum ‘isha’an yabkoon,” and the commentators explain the scene, for the brothers returned to Jacob in the evening, and the evening is the time of the return, the darkness that matched the deed, and the weeping is the tears that the brothers shed, and the commentators note the two possibilities, for the weeping was either the false tears of the deceivers, the performance of the grief that the plot required, or the tears of the remorse, the first stirring of the conscience that the deed had wounded, and the reports differ, and the verse preserves the weeping as the ambiguity, the tears that the father could not read, and the ambiguity is the teaching, for the tears of the deceiver and the tears of the remorseful are the same to the eye, and the heart alone distinguishes them. Ibn Kathir notes the detail, for the weeping is the first act of the deception, the tears that the brothers brought to the father, and the tears are the mask of the lie, and the verse records them as the prelude of the false report that the next verses record.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the tears, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own sincerity. The believer is taught that the tears are not the proof of the truth, and the deceiver can weep and the remorseful can weep, and the one who reads the tears without the heart is the one whom the tears deceive, and the believer who seeks the truth must look beyond the tears to the deeds, for the tears of the brothers were the tears of the lie, and the weeping of the evening was the mask of the morning’s crime. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the discernment of the tears: the brothers wept and lied, and the believer who trusts the tears is the believer whom the lie deceives, and the sincerity is the matter of the heart that the tears cannot prove. The verse is the record of the return, and the teaching of the tears that the falsehood wears.",
  "17": "“They cried, “Our father! We went racing and left Joseph with our belongings, and a wolf devoured him! But you will not believe us, no matter how truthful we are.”” records the lie, and the commentators note the three parts of the false report. The first is the excuse: “We went racing and left Joseph with our belongings,” or “ya abana inna thahabna nastabiq wa tarakna Yoosufa ‘inda mata‘ina,” and the commentators explain the excuse, for the brothers claimed that they had gone to race and left Joseph with the belongings, and the claim is the preparation of the lie, the explanation of how the boy came to be alone, and the excuse is the first thread of the fabrication. Ibn Kathir notes the detail, for the brothers had not raced, and the claim of the racing was the invention, and the invention is the measure of the lie. The second part is the lie itself: “and a wolf devoured him!” or “fa-akalahu ath-thi’bu,” and the commentators note the irony, for the wolf that the father had feared was the lie that the brothers now told, and the fear of the father became the excuse of the brothers, and the wolf is the symbol of the falsehood, the beast that the lie invented, and the commentators note the precision of the lie, for the brothers used the very fear that the father had expressed, and the use of the fear is the cunning of the lie. The third part is the complaint: “But you will not believe us, no matter how truthful we are,” or “wa ma anta bi-mu’minin lana wa law kunna sadiqeen,” and the commentators note the complaint, for the brothers preempted the doubt of the father, and the preemption is the strategy of the liar, the accusation of the distrust that the lie anticipated, and the complaint is the seal of the fabrication, the declaration of the truthfulness that the lie had abandoned.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the lie, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own discernment. The believer is taught that the lie is the work of the details, and the falsehood constructs the excuse and the report and the complaint, and the believer who examines the details is the believer whom the lie cannot take, and the wolf of the lie is the symbol of the falsehood that the believer must not believe, and the preemption of the doubt is the mark of the liar, the accusation that the liar hurls at the truth. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the anatomy of the lie: the excuse, the report, and the complaint, and the believer who sees the three is the believer who sees through the lie, and the story of Joseph is the teaching that the falsehood, however constructed, is the falsehood that the truth exposes. The verse is the record of the lie, and the teaching of the discernment that the falsehood demands.",
  "18": "“And they brought his shirt, stained with false blood. He responded, “No! Your souls must have tempted you to do something ˹evil˺. So ˹I can only endure with˺ beautiful patience! It is Allah’s help that I seek to bear your claims.”” records the proof and the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the evidence: “And they brought his shirt, stained with false blood,” or “wa ja’oo ‘ala qameesihi bi-damin kathib,” and the commentators explain the detail, for the brothers brought the shirt of Joseph stained with the blood, and the word “kathib,” the false, the lying, is the divine verdict upon the blood, for the blood was not the blood of Joseph but the blood of the animal that the brothers had slaughtered, and the commentators note the flaw of the lie, for the shirt was stained but not torn, and the father’s insight, which the reply records, saw through the evidence, and the verse calls the blood “false” from the first, so that the reader knows the lie before the father’s reply. Ibn Kathir notes the detail, for the shirt was brought with the blood but not the tear, and the absence of the tear was the flaw that the lie had missed, and the flaw is the teaching that the falsehood always leaves the gap that the truth finds. The second part is the reply: “No! Your souls must have tempted you to do something evil. So I can only endure with beautiful patience! It is Allah’s help that I seek to bear your claims,” or “qala bal sawwalat lakum anfusukum amran fa-sabrun jameelun wallahu al-musta‘anu ‘ala ma tasifoon,” and the commentators note the three elements of the reply, the diagnosis of the temptation, the declaration of the beautiful patience, and the seeking of the help of the Lord, and the reply is the model of the believer’s response to the calamity, the acceptance of the decree with the patience and the trust, and the “sabr jameel,” the beautiful patience, is the patience that Jacob chose, the patience without the complaint, and the phrase became the proverb of the patient.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the patience, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trial. The believer is taught that the calamity is met with the beautiful patience, and the beautiful patience is the patience that does not complain to the creation, and the seeking of the help of the Lord is the companion of the patience, and the believer who loses the beloved and says “beautiful patience, and the help of Allah is sought” is the believer whose patience the Lord will reward, and the false blood of the lie is the trial that the patience of Jacob absorbed. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the summit of the patience: Jacob saw the lie and chose the patience, and the choice is the model, and the believer who endures the calamity with the beautiful patience is the believer whose end is the end of Jacob, the reunion that the patience earned. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the patience that the calamity demands.",
  "19": "“And there came some travellers, and they sent their water-boy who let down his bucket into the well. He cried out, “Oh, what a great find! Here is a boy!” And they took him secretly ˹to be sold˺ as merchandise, but Allah is All-Knowing of what they did.”” records the rescue from the well, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the rescue: “And there came some travellers, and they sent their water-boy who let down his bucket into the well,” or “wa ja’at sayyaratun fa-arsaloo waridahum fa-adla dalwahu,” and the commentators describe the scene, for a caravan of the travellers came to the well, and the water-boy let down the bucket, and Joseph seized the rope, and the boy cried out: “Oh, what a great find! Here is a boy!” or “qala ya bushra hatha ghulam,” and the commentators note the exclamation, for the finding of the boy in the well was the surprise of the rescue, and the surprise is the first turning of the trial, the hand of the Lord that had guided the caravan to the well, and the rescue is the mercy that the brothers had not intended. Ibn Kathir notes the detail, for Joseph clung to the bucket and was drawn up, and the travellers found the boy, and the finding is the deliverance from the pit, and the deliverance is the work of the Lord, the first step of the journey to Egypt. The second part is the sale: “And they took him secretly to be sold as merchandise, but Allah is All-Knowing of what they did,” or “wa asarroohu bida‘atan wallahu ‘aleemun bima ya‘maloon,” and the commentators explain the secrecy, for the travellers concealed Joseph and sold him as the merchandise, and the sale is the second trial, the reduction of the free boy to the sold slave, and the verse closes with the divine knowledge: “Allah is All-Knowing of what they did,” the assurance that the sale and the secrecy were within the knowledge of the Lord, and the knowledge is the comfort, for the Lord who knew the sale was the Lord who was arranging the destiny.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the rescue, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own deliverance. The believer is taught that the rescue comes from the unexpected, and the caravan that the Lord guided to the well is the hand of the mercy that the believer does not see, and the sale that the travellers made was the trial that the Lord had destined, and the believer who is sold by the world is the believer whose price the Lord has already set, and the knowledge of the Lord is the comfort of the one whose path the world misunderstands. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the hidden hand: the well, the caravan, the bucket, and the sale were the links of the chain that the Lord had forged, and the believer who sees the chain is the believer who trusts the hand, and the journey of Joseph is the proof that the trials of the world are the roads of the Lord. The verse is the record of the rescue and the sale, and the teaching of the hidden hand that the trials serve.",
  "20": "“They ˹later˺ sold him for a cheap price, just a few silver coins—only wanting to get rid of him.”” records the sale, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the price: “They later sold him for a cheap price, just a few silver coins,” or “wa sharawhu bi-thamanin bakhsin darahima ma‘doodah,” and the commentators explain the “thaman bakhs,” the cheap price, the “darahim ma‘doodah,” the few counted silver coins, and the commentators note the detail from the reports, for the price was twenty or twenty-two silver coins, and the smallness of the price is the measure of the indifference, the sale of the boy for the pittance that the travellers accepted, and the cheapness is the irony that the verse preserves, for the one whom the Lord had chosen was sold for the few coins, and the irony is the teaching, for the worth of the chosen is not the price of the market. Ibn Kathir notes the detail, for the price was the few coins, and the few coins are the measure of the world’s valuation, and the valuation is the contrast with the worth that the Lord had assigned, and the contrast is the lesson of the whole story. The second part is the motive: “only wanting to get rid of him,” or “wa kanoo feehi mina az-zahideen,” and the commentators explain the phrase, for the travellers sold Joseph without the desire to keep him, and the phrase “az-zahideen,” the indifferent, the uninterested, is the description of the sellers, and the indifference is the second irony, for the ones who sold the chosen were the ones who did not want him, and the verse preserves the indifference as the contrast with the destiny that the Lord had prepared.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the valuation, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own worth. The believer is taught that the worth of the soul is not the price of the world, and the one whom the Lord chooses is the one whom the world sells for the pittance, and the believer who is undervalued by the world is the believer whose worth the Lord has set, and the sale of Joseph for the few coins is the proof that the market of the world and the scale of the Lord are the two measures that never meet. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the measure of the worth: Joseph was sold for the few coins and was the chosen of the Lord, and the believer who is sold cheaply by the world is the believer whose price the Lord has written in the unseen, and the cheapness of the world is the prelude of the honour of the Lord. The verse is the record of the sale, and the teaching of the worth that the world cannot measure.",
  "21": "“The man from Egypt who bought him said to his wife, “Take good care of him, perhaps he may be useful to us or we may adopt him as a son.” This is how We established Joseph in the land, so that We might teach him the interpretation of dreams. Allah’s Will always prevails, but most people do not know.”” records the arrival in Egypt and the divine purpose, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the purchase: “The man from Egypt who bought him said to his wife, ‘Take good care of him, perhaps he may be useful to us or we may adopt him as a son,’” or “wa qala allathee ishtarahu min Misra li-imra’atihi akrimee mathwahu ‘asa an yanfa‘ana aw nattakhithahu waladan,” and the commentators identify the buyer as the ‘Aziz of Egypt, the minister, and the verse records his words to his wife, the command to honour the lodging of Joseph and the hope of the usefulness or the adoption, and the commentators note the providence, for the man who bought the boy as the slave was inspired to treat him as the son, and the words of the ‘Aziz are the first kindness that the Lord arranged for Joseph, and the commentators note that the “we established Joseph in the land” is the second part: “This is how We established Joseph in the land, so that We might teach him the interpretation of dreams,” or “wa kathalika makkanna li-Yoosufa fi al-ardi wa li-nu‘allimahu min ta’weeli al-ahadeethi,” and the commentators explain the purpose, for the establishment of Joseph in Egypt was the divine arrangement for the teaching of the interpretation, and the teaching is the gift that the story will display, and the verse closes with the principle: “Allah’s Will always prevails, but most people do not know,” or “wallahu ghalibun ‘ala amrihi wa lakinna akthara an-nasi la ya‘lamoon,” and the commentators note the declaration, for the will of the Lord prevails over the plots of the creatures, and the brothers had plotted the removal and the Lord had arranged the establishment, and the majority of the people do not know the hidden will, and the declaration is the theme of the whole story.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the establishment, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own journey. The believer is taught that the trials of the journey are the roads of the establishment, and the Lord who established Joseph in Egypt through the well and the sale is the Lord who establishes the believer through the trials that the believer does not understand, and the teaching of the interpretation is the wisdom that the patience earns, and the will of the Lord prevails over the plots, and the believer who trusts the will is the believer whose journey the Lord completes. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the hidden purpose: the purchase of the ‘Aziz was the establishment, and the establishment was for the teaching, and the believer who sees the purpose behind the trial is the believer who is patient with the trial, and the will of the Lord is the comfort that the plots cannot cancel. The verse is the record of the establishment, and the teaching of the will that prevails.",
  "22": "“And when he reached maturity, We gave him wisdom and knowledge. This is how We reward the good-doers.”” records the gifts of the maturity, and the commentators note the two gifts and the principle. The verse states the two gifts: “And when he reached maturity, We gave him wisdom and knowledge,” or “wa lamma balagha ashuddahu ataynahu hukman wa ‘ilman,” and the commentators explain the “hukm,” the wisdom, the judgment, the discernment, and the “‘ilm,” the knowledge, the science of the interpretation, and the two gifts are the reward of the patience, the endowment that the Lord granted to Joseph at the maturity, and the commentators note the timing, for the gifts came at the “ashudd,” the full strength, the maturity, and the timing is the teaching that the wisdom and the knowledge are the fruits of the growth, and the growth is the passage of the trials. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the “hukm” is the prophethood or the wisdom, and the “‘ilm” is the knowledge of the interpretation, and the two together are the equipment of the mission, and the verse closes with the principle: “This is how We reward the good-doers,” or “wa kathalika najzi al-muhsineen,” and the commentators note the declaration, for the reward of the “muhsineen,” the good-doers, is the wisdom and the knowledge, and the principle is the teaching that the patience of the good-doer is the seed of the reward, and the reward of Joseph is the model of the reward that the good-doers inherit.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the maturity, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own growth. The believer is taught that the wisdom and the knowledge are the gifts of the maturity, and the maturity is the passage of the trials, and the believer who endures the trials with the patience is the believer whom the Lord endows with the wisdom and the knowledge, and the reward of the good-doers is the principle that the believer trusts, for the Lord does not lose the reward of the one who does the good, and the patience of Joseph is the proof that the reward follows the patience. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the fruit of the patience: the wisdom and the knowledge came at the maturity, and the believer who is patient through the youth is the believer whom the Lord endows in the strength, and the reward of the good-doers is the promise that the patience never loses. The verse is the record of the gifts, and the teaching of the maturity that the trials produce.",
  "23": "“And the lady, in whose house he lived, tried to seduce him. She locked the doors ˹firmly˺ and said, “Come to me!” He replied, “Allah is my refuge! It is ˹not right to betray˺ my master, who has taken good care of me. Indeed, the wrongdoers never succeed.”” records the trial of the seduction, and the commentators note the three parts of the verse. The first is the seduction: “And the lady, in whose house he lived, tried to seduce him. She locked the doors firmly and said, ‘Come to me!’” or “wa rawadathu allatee huwa fee baytiha ‘an nafsihi wa ghallaqati al-abwaba wa qalat hayta lak,” and the commentators explain the scene, for the wife of the ‘Aziz, in whose house Joseph lived, sought to seduce him, and she locked the doors and invited him, and the commentators note the detail, for the locking of the doors is the measure of the determination, and the invitation is the temptation, and the scene is the trial of the chastity, the test that the beauty of Joseph and the favour of the household had prepared. Ibn Kathir notes the identity, for the lady was the wife of the ‘Aziz, the Zulaykha of the later traditions, and the seduction is the great trial of the story, the temptation that the next verses will describe. The second part is the refusal: “He replied, ‘Allah is my refuge! It is not right to betray my master, who has taken good care of me,’” or “qala ma‘atha Allahi innahu rabbee ahsana mathwaya,” and the commentators note the refuge, for Joseph sought the refuge of the Lord, and the appeal to the master’s kindness, and the two together are the shield of the chastity, the fear of the Lord and the gratitude to the master, and the word “ma‘ath Allah” — the refuge of Allah — is the first of the shields, the seeking of the protection that the temptation could not pierce. The third part is the verdict: “Indeed, the wrongdoers never succeed,” or “innahu la yuflihu az-zalimoon,” and the commentators note the principle, for the wrongdoers, the “zalimoon,” the ones who betray the trust, never succeed, and the declaration is the reason of the refusal, the certainty that the sin of the betrayal is the sin that the success abandons.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the chastity, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own temptation. The believer is taught that the temptation is the trial of the chastity, and the refuge of the Lord and the gratitude to the benefactor are the two shields, and the certainty that the wrongdoers never succeed is the third, and the believer who is tempted in the locked room is the believer whom the three shields protect, and the chastity of Joseph is the model of the believer’s resistance, the refusal that the fear of the Lord and the gratitude and the certainty together produce. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the weapons of the chastity: the refuge of the Lord, the memory of the benefactor, and the certainty of the failure of the wrongdoers, and the believer who arms himself with the three is the believer whom the temptation cannot take, and the locked doors are the measure of the trial that the three shields withstood. The verse is the record of the trial and the refusal, and the teaching of the chastity that the temptation tests.",
  "24": "“She advanced towards him, and he would have done likewise, had he not seen a sign from his Lord. This is how We kept evil and indecency away from him, for he was truly one of Our chosen servants.”” records the peak of the trial and the divine protection, and the commentators note the three parts of the verse. The first is the advance: “She advanced towards him, and he would have done likewise, had he not seen a sign from his Lord,” or “wa laqad hammat bihi wa hamma biha law la an ra’a burhana rabbihi,” and the commentators explain the delicate phrase, for the lady advanced upon Joseph with the intention, and Joseph would have inclined toward her, the “hamma biha,” had he not seen the proof of his Lord, and the commentators differ on the meaning of the “hamma,” and the dominant understanding is that Joseph’s inclination was the natural response of the man, the first movement of the nature that the proof of the Lord checked, and the proof, the “burhan,” is the sign that the Lord showed him, and the sign is the divine intervention that saved the servant, and the commentators relate that the proof was the vision of the father Jacob, or the reminder of the Lord, and the verse is careful to preserve the innocence of Joseph, for the “hamma” that was checked is not the sin but the inclination that the sign extinguished. Ibn Kathir notes the interpretation, for the verse teaches that Joseph was protected by the sign, and the protection is the declaration of the verse: “This is how We kept evil and indecency away from him, for he was truly one of Our chosen servants,” or “kathalika li-nasrifa ‘anhu as-soo’a wal-fahsha’a innahu min ‘ibadina al-mukhlaseen,” and the commentators note the purpose, for the sign was the means of the protection, and the protection is the reward of the sincerity, for Joseph was of the “mukhlaseen,” the purified, the chosen, the sincere, and the chosen are the ones whom the Lord guards from the evil and the indecency.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the protection, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own weakness. The believer is taught that the nature of the man is the inclination, and the protection of the Lord is the sign that checks it, and the believer who is tempted is the believer whose safety is the sign of the Lord, and the sincerity is the condition of the protection, and the chosen are the ones whom the Lord turns from the evil, and the believer who seeks the protection must seek the sincerity that the protection follows. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reality of the weakness and the shield: Joseph was the man with the nature, and the sign was the shield, and the believer who admits the weakness and seeks the sign is the believer whom the Lord protects, and the sincerity of the chosen is the key of the protection. The verse is the record of the peak and the protection, and the teaching of the sign that the sincerity attracts.",
  "25": "“They raced for the door and she tore his shirt from the back, only to find her husband at the door. She cried, “What is the penalty for someone who tried to violate your wife, except imprisonment or a painful punishment?”” records the flight and the accusation, and the commentators note the three parts of the verse. The first is the race: “They raced for the door and she tore his shirt from the back, only to find her husband at the door,” or “wastabaqa al-baba wa qaddat qameesahu min duburin wa alfaya sayyidaha lada al-bab,” and the commentators describe the scene, for Joseph fled toward the door and the lady pursued him, and she tore his shirt from the back, and the husband was found at the door, and the tear from the back is the proof of the innocence, for the one who flees is torn from the back, and the one who is attacked is torn from the front, and the detail is the evidence that the next verse will use, and the verse preserves the detail as the thread of the justice. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the tear of the shirt from the back is the physical witness of the flight, and the witness is the proof that the following verse will bring. The second part is the accusation: “She cried, ‘What is the penalty for someone who tried to violate your wife, except imprisonment or a painful punishment?’” or “qalat ma jaza’u man arada bi-ahlika soo’an illa an yusjana aw ‘athabun aleem,” and the commentators note the accusation, for the lady, caught at the door, reversed the roles and accused Joseph of the violation, and the accusation is the strategy of the guilty, the preemption of the blame by the charge, and the demand of the punishment is the sealing of the lie, and the verse preserves the accusation as the measure of the shamelessness, the false charge that the justice of the following verses would expose.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the accusation, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own vindication. The believer is taught that the innocent is sometimes accused, and the flight of the innocent is the proof of the innocence, and the believer who is falsely charged is the believer whose truth the Lord will expose, and the tear of the shirt is the symbol of the evidence that the falsehood cannot hide, and the believer who trusts the Lord in the false accusation is the believer whose innocence the Lord will establish. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the accused: Joseph fled and was accused, and the flight and the accusation are the two sides of the trial, and the believer who is falsely charged is the believer whose vindication the Lord prepares, and the tear from the back is the witness that the falsehood could not erase. The verse is the record of the flight and the accusation, and the teaching of the evidence that the truth preserves.",
  "26": "“Joseph responded, “It was she who tried to seduce me.” And a witness from her own family testified: “If his shirt is torn from the front, then she has told the truth and he is a liar.”” records the defence and the testimony, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the defence: “Joseph responded, ‘It was she who tried to seduce me,’” or “qala hiya rawadatnee ‘an nafsee,” and the commentators note the calm of the reply, for Joseph declared the truth, the accusation of the seduction, and the declaration is the defence of the innocent, the statement of the fact that the lady had sought him, and the reply is the courage of the truthful, the refusal to accept the false charge, and the commentators note that the verse records the word of Joseph without the anger, the calm of the one whose truth the Lord would establish. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the defence of Joseph is the first word of the vindication, and the vindication is completed by the witness of the following clause. The second part is the testimony: “And a witness from her own family testified: ‘If his shirt is torn from the front, then she has told the truth and he is a liar,’” or “wa shahida shahidun min ahliha in kana qameesuhu qudda min qubulin fa-sadaqat wa huwa mina al-kathibeen,” and the commentators note the witness, for the witness was from the family of the lady, and the commentators differ on the identity, and the testimony is the reasoning of the shirt, the proof of the tear, and the reasoning is the justice of the evidence, the physical witness that the following verse completes, and the witness is the divine provision of the proof, the means by which the truth of Joseph was established, and the commentators note that the witness was the infant in the cradle, or a man of the family, and the difference is the richness of the reports, and the meaning is the proof of the tear.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the defence, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own truth. The believer is taught that the defence of the innocent is the declaration of the truth, and the proof of the Lord is the witness that the believer does not expect, and the one who is falsely accused is the one whose witness the Lord raises, and the reasoning of the shirt is the teaching that the truth is the matter of the evidence, and the believer who trusts the Lord in the accusation is the believer whose evidence the Lord supplies. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the certainty of the vindication: Joseph declared the truth and the witness testified, and the believer who is accused is the believer whose vindication the Lord arranges, and the proof of the shirt is the symbol of the evidence that the truth always leaves. The verse is the record of the defence and the testimony, and the teaching of the vindication that the Lord supplies.",
  "27": "“But if his shirt is torn from the back, then she has lied and he is truthful.”” completes the reasoning of the witness, and the commentators note the logic and its outcome. The verse is the second half of the testimony: “But if his shirt is torn from the back, then she has lied and he is truthful,” or “wa in kana qameesuhu qudda min duburin fa-kathabat wa huwa mina as-sadiqeen,” and the commentators explain the logic, for the tear of the shirt is the direction of the truth, and the one who flees is torn from the back and the one who is attacked is torn from the front, and the reasoning of the witness is the application of the logic, the judgment of the tear, and the verse completes the proof, for the shirt of Joseph was torn from the back, and the tear was the witness of the flight, and the flight was the proof of the innocence, and the verse states the two possibilities and their verdicts, the front for the truth of the lady and the back for the truth of Joseph, and the commentators note the elegance of the reasoning, the physical evidence that the falsehood could not answer. Ibn Kathir notes the completion, for the two verses together are the full testimony of the witness, and the reasoning of the shirt is the model of the Quran’s method of the proof, the demonstration of the truth by the evidence that the lie cannot counterfeit, and the verse is the second half of the proof that the following verse applies.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the evidence, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own discernment. The believer is taught that the truth is the matter of the evidence, and the direction of the tear is the direction of the truth, and the one who examines the evidence is the one whom the lie cannot deceive, and the believer who is judged by the falsehood is the believer whose evidence the Lord preserves, and the reasoning of the shirt is the teaching that the truth always leaves the sign that the falsehood cannot erase. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the value of the evidence: the tear was the witness, and the witness was the vindication, and the believer who trusts the evidence of the truth is the believer whose innocence the Lord establishes, and the story of Joseph is the proof that the falsehood is the lie that the evidence exposes. The verse is the completion of the testimony, and the teaching of the evidence that the truth preserves.",
  "28": "“So when her husband saw that Joseph’s shirt was torn from the back, he said ˹to her˺, “This must be ˹an example˺ of the cunning of you ˹women˺! Indeed, your cunning is so shrewd!”” records the verdict of the husband, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The first is the sight: “when her husband saw that Joseph’s shirt was torn from the back,” or “fa-lamma ra’a qameesahu qudda min duburin,” and the commentators note the detail, for the husband saw the tear from the back, and the sight was the application of the reasoning that the witness had proposed, and the tear was the proof of the flight, and the flight was the proof of the innocence, and the husband, seeing the proof, pronounced the verdict. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the tear from the back was the physical witness that the lie of the lady could not answer, and the sight of the tear was the moment of the vindication. The second part is the verdict: “This must be an example of the cunning of you women! Indeed, your cunning is so shrewd!” or “qala innahu min kaydikunna inna kaydakunna ‘azeem,” and the commentators note the words, for the husband attributed the matter to the “kayd,” the cunning, the scheme of the women, and the attribution is the rebuke of the wife, and the exclamation “your cunning is so shrewd” is the verdict upon the deception, and the commentators note that the husband did not punish Joseph, and the word of the verdict is the first vindication, and the verse preserves the verdict as the measure of the falsehood that the shirt exposed. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the evidence: the tear of the shirt was the proof, and the proof exposed the cunning, and the believer who is falsely accused is the believer whose evidence the Lord will raise, and the cunning of the falsehood, however shrewd, is the cunning that the truth exposes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the verdict, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own vindication. The believer is taught that the truth is the matter of the proof, and the proof is the witness that the falsehood cannot counterfeit, and the one who is accused by the cunning is the one whose innocence the evidence establishes, and the verdict of the husband is the first step of the vindication that the following verses complete, and the believer who trusts the Lord in the false accusation is the believer whose proof the Lord preserves. The verse is the record of the verdict, and the teaching of the evidence that the cunning cannot hide.",
  "29": "“O Joseph! Forget about this. And you ˹O wife˺! Seek forgiveness for your sin. It certainly has been your fault.”” records the instruction of the husband, and the commentators note the two addresses and their meaning. The first is to Joseph: “O Joseph! Forget about this,” or “Yoosufu a‘rid ‘an hatha,” and the commentators explain the instruction, for the husband commanded Joseph to turn away from the matter and to conceal it, and the instruction is the protection of the household, the decision to settle the affair without the scandal, and the commentators note that the instruction is not the doubt of the innocence but the wisdom of the concealment, the choice of the privacy over the exposure. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the husband knew the innocence of Joseph and chose to cover the matter, and the covering is the wisdom of the head of the house. The second is to the wife: “And you O wife! Seek forgiveness for your sin. It certainly has been your fault,” or “wastaghfiree li-thanbiki innaki kunti mina al-khati’een,” and the commentators note the instruction, for the husband commanded the wife to seek the forgiveness for her sin, and the declaration “it certainly has been your fault” is the verdict of the guilt, the confirmation that the fault was the wife’s, and the instruction is the rebuke, and the verse preserves the two addresses as the closing of the affair, the concealment of the innocent and the rebuke of the guilty.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the concealment, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own wisdom. The believer is taught that the settlement of the affair is sometimes the concealment, and the protection of the household is the wisdom that the believer exercises, and the rebuke of the guilty is the justice that the concealment does not cancel, and the believer who is wronged and then concealed is the believer whose dignity the Lord preserves, and the instruction of the husband is the model of the wise head who covers the fault and corrects the guilty. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the balance of the concealment and the correction: the affair was covered and the fault was named, and the believer who is wronged can accept the concealment without the surrender of the truth, and the verdict “it has been your fault” is the justice that the concealment retained. The verse is the record of the instruction, and the teaching of the wisdom that covers and corrects.",
  "30": "“Some women of the city gossiped, “The Chief Minister’s wife is trying to seduce her slave-boy. Love for him has plagued her heart. Indeed, we see that she is clearly mistaken.”” records the gossip of the city, and the commentators note the two parts of the talk. The first is the report: “The Chief Minister’s wife is trying to seduce her slave-boy,” or “imra’atu al-‘azeezi turawidu fataha ‘an nafsihi,” and the commentators note the word, for the matter of the house had leaked to the city, and the women gossiped about the wife of the ‘Aziz and her seduction of the slave-boy, and the gossip is the spread of the scandal that the husband had tried to conceal, and the spread is the teaching that the secret of the sin is the secret that the walls cannot keep. Ibn Kathir notes the detail, for the women of the city were the wives of the nobles, and their gossip is the prelude of the banquet that the following verse records, and the gossip is the means by which the story of the seduction reached the women. The second part is the verdict: “Love for him has plagued her heart. Indeed, we see that she is clearly mistaken,” or “qad shaghafaha hubban inna la-naraha fee dalalin mubeen,” and the commentators note the words, for the women declared that the love had “shaghafa,” plagued, pierced, the heart of the lady, and the verdict “she is clearly mistaken” is the judgment of the women, and the judgment is the irony, for the women who judged the lady would themselves, at the banquet, be stunned by the beauty of Joseph and cut their hands, and the verse preserves the judgment as the prelude of the scene that the next verse records, and the commentators note that the gossip of the women was the occasion that the lady would use to display Joseph, and the display would be the proof of the beauty that the gossip had doubted.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the gossip, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own tongue. The believer is taught that the gossip of the sin is the spread of the sin, and the one who talks of the fault of another is the one who spreads the fault, and the judgment of the women is the warning that the one who judges may fall, and the believer who guards the tongue from the gossip is the believer whom the Lord guards from the fall. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the danger of the gossip: the women gossiped and judged, and the banquet that followed exposed their own weakness, and the believer who speaks of the fault of another is the believer whose own fault the Lord may expose, and the guard of the tongue is the guard of the self. The verse is the record of the gossip, and the teaching of the tongue that the believer must guard.",
  "31": "“When she heard about their gossip, she invited them and set a banquet for them. She gave each one a knife, then said ˹to Joseph˺, “Come out before them.” When they saw him, they were so stunned ˹by his beauty˺ that they cut their hands, and exclaimed, “Good God! This cannot be human; this must be a noble angel!”” records the banquet and the stunning, and the commentators note the three parts of the scene. The first is the invitation: “When she heard about their gossip, she invited them and set a banquet for them. She gave each one a knife, then said to Joseph, ‘Come out before them,’” or “fa-lamma sami‘at bi-makrihinna arsalat ilayhinna wa a‘tadat lahunna muttaka’an wa atat kulla wahidatin minhunna sikkeenan wa qalati ukhruj ‘alayhinna,” and the commentators describe the scene, for the lady, hearing the gossip, invited the women to the banquet and prepared the couches and gave each a knife, and then commanded Joseph to come out before them, and the banquet is the trap of the display, the plan of the lady to show the beauty that had plagued her heart, and the knives are the instruments of the scene, the means by which the stunning would be witnessed. Ibn Kathir notes the detail, for the banquet was the lady’s answer to the gossip, and the answer was the display, and the display is the proof of the beauty that the gossip had doubted. The second part is the stunning: “When they saw him, they were so stunned by his beauty that they cut their hands,” or “fa-lamma ra’aynahu akbarnahu wa qatta‘na aydiyahunna,” and the commentators note the detail, for the women, stunned by the beauty of Joseph, cut their hands with the knives, and the cutting is the witness of the stunning, the physical proof that the beauty of Joseph was the wonder that the gossip had not imagined. The third part is the exclamation: “Good God! This cannot be human; this must be a noble angel!” or “qulna hasha lillahi ma hatha basharan in hatha illa malakun kareem,” and the commentators note the words, for the women declared that Joseph could not be the human but must be the noble angel, and the declaration is the confession of the beauty, and the confession is the vindication of the lady’s love, and the verse preserves the scene as the proof of the beauty that the story of Joseph carries.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the beauty, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trial. The believer is taught that the beauty is the trial, and the one whom the Lord has given the beauty is the one whom the beauty tests, and the stunning of the women is the measure of the trial that Joseph bore, and the chastity of Joseph in the midst of the beauty is the summit of the virtue, and the believer who is given the beauty is the believer whose gratitude is the guard, and the one who is tested by the beauty is the one whose refuge is the Lord. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the weight of the beauty: the women cut their hands at the sight, and Joseph bore the sight with the chastity, and the believer who is given the gift is the believer whose gift is the test, and the refuge of the Lord is the shield that the beauty cannot pierce. The verse is the record of the banquet, and the teaching of the beauty that the chastity withstood.",
  "32": "“She said, “This is the one for whose love you criticized me! I did try to seduce him but he ˹firmly˺ refused. And if he does not do what I order him to, he will certainly be imprisoned and ˹fully˺ disgraced.”” records the confession and the threat, and the commentators note the two parts of the speech. The first is the confession: “This is the one for whose love you criticized me! I did try to seduce him but he firmly refused,” or “qalat fa-thalikunna allathee lumtunnanee feehi wa laqad rawadtuhu ‘an nafsihi fast‘asam,” and the commentators note the words, for the lady confessed to the women that Joseph was the one for whose love they had criticized her, and the confession is the vindication, the declaration that the beauty of Joseph was the cause of the love that the gossip had mocked, and the second confession: “I did try to seduce him but he firmly refused,” the admission of the seduction and the praise of the refusal, and the word “fast‘asam” — he firmly refused, he sought the protection — is the testimony of the lady herself to the chastity of Joseph, and the testimony is the irony, for the accuser became the witness of the innocence. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the confession of the lady is the second vindication of Joseph, and the confession is the proof that the chastity of Joseph was the truth, and the proof is the irony that the verse preserves. The second part is the threat: “And if he does not do what I order him to, he will certainly be imprisoned and fully disgraced,” or “wa la-in lam yaf‘al ma amuruhu la-yusjananna wa la-yakoonan mina as-saghireen,” and the commentators note the threat, for the lady threatened Joseph with the prison and the disgrace if he did not obey, and the threat is the last resort of the passion, the turning of the love into the oppression, and the threat is the prelude of the imprisonment that the story records, and the verse preserves the threat as the measure of the trial, the choice that Joseph faced between the sin and the prison.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the threat, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own choice. The believer is taught that the trial of the passion ends in the threat, and the one who refuses the sin is the one whom the passion threatens, and the believer who is threatened for the chastity is the believer whose choice is the prison over the sin, and the choice of Joseph is the model, the preference of the prison to the disobedience, and the believer who faces the same threat is the believer whose refuge is the Lord. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the cost of the chastity: the lady confessed and threatened, and the confession is the vindication and the threat is the trial, and the believer who refuses the sin and bears the threat is the believer whose choice the Lord will honour, and the prison that the threat promised is the door that the Lord would open. The verse is the record of the confession and the threat, and the teaching of the choice that the chastity demands.",
  "33": "“Joseph prayed, “My Lord! I would rather be in jail than do what they invite me to. And if You do not turn their cunning away from me, I might yield to them and fall into ignorance.”” records the prayer of Joseph, and the commentators note the two parts of the prayer. The first is the preference: “My Lord! I would rather be in jail than do what they invite me to,” or “rabbi as-sijnu ahabbu ilayya mimma yad‘oonanee ilayhi,” and the commentators note the preference, for Joseph declared that the prison was more beloved to him than the sin, and the preference is the summit of the chastity, the declaration that the darkness of the prison is the light beside the darkness of the disobedience, and the word “ahabb,” more beloved, is the measure of the choice, the heart that preferred the cell to the sin. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the prayer is the confession of the weakness and the seeking of the help, and the preference of the prison is the proof of the faith, the choice of the hardship over the disobedience. The second part is the plea: “And if You do not turn their cunning away from me, I might yield to them and fall into ignorance,” or “wa illa tasrif ‘annee kaydahunna asbu ilayhinna wa akun mina al-jahileen,” and the commentators note the plea, for Joseph confessed his own weakness, the possibility of the yielding, and the confession is the humility of the servant, and the plea is the seeking of the divine protection, the “tasrif,” the turning away of the cunning, and the verse teaches that the protection of the chastity is the gift of the Lord, not the strength of the servant, and the prayer is the model of the believer’s reliance in the face of the temptation, and the commentators note the word “jahileen,” the ignorant, for the one who yields to the sin is the one who has fallen into the ignorance, and the prayer is the seeking of the protection from the ignorance.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the prayer, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own temptation. The believer is taught that the preference of the hardship over the sin is the summit of the chastity, and the confession of the weakness is the humility that the protection follows, and the plea for the turning of the cunning is the reliance that the believer must hold, and the one who is tempted is the one who prays the prayer of Joseph, the preference, the confession, and the plea, and the believer who prays the three is the believer whom the Lord protects. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the complete prayer of the tempted: the preference of the prison, the confession of the weakness, and the plea for the protection, and the believer who prays the three is the believer whose chastity the Lord guards, and the prison that Joseph preferred is the witness of the faith that the temptation could not shake. The verse is the record of the prayer, and the teaching of the reliance that the temptation demands.",
  "34": "“So his Lord responded to him, turning their cunning away from him. Surely He is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing.”” records the answer to the prayer, and the commentators note the response and the two names. The verse states the response: “So his Lord responded to him, turning their cunning away from him,” or “fastajaba lahu rabbuhu fa-sarafa ‘anhu kaydahunna,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the Lord answered the prayer of Joseph and turned away the cunning of the women, and the answer is the fulfillment of the plea, the divine protection that the prayer had sought, and the word “sarafa,” the turning, is the same word that Joseph had used in the plea, so that the answer echoes the prayer, and the commentators note the immediacy of the response, for the Lord answered the sincere plea of the servant, and the answer is the assurance that the protection of the chastity is the gift that the prayer receives. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the verse teaches that the prayer of the tempted is the key of the protection, and the Lord who hears the prayer is the Lord who turns the cunning, and the response is the proof that the reliance of Joseph was not in vain. The verse closes with the two names: “Surely He is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing,” or “innahu huwa as-samee‘u al-‘aleem,” and the commentators note the pairing, for the Lord heard the prayer and knew the cunning, and the two names are the ground of the response, the hearing of the plea and the knowledge of the scheme, and the believer who knows the two names is the believer whose prayer is the confident, the plea to the One who hears and knows.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the response, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own prayer. The believer is taught that the prayer of the tempted is the prayer that the Lord answers, and the turning of the cunning is the response that the reliance receives, and the believer who prays with the sincerity of Joseph is the believer whose protection the Lord grants, and the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing is the Lord who hears the plea and knows the scheme, and the believer who trusts the two names is the believer whose prayer is the confident. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the certainty of the response: Joseph prayed and was answered, and the believer who prays the same prayer is the believer whose prayer the Lord hears, and the turning of the cunning is the protection that the believer seeks, and the two names are the comfort of the one who prays. The verse is the record of the response, and the teaching of the prayer that the Lord answers.",
  "35": "“And so it occurred to those in charge, despite seeing all the proofs ˹of his innocence˺, that he should be imprisoned for a while.”” records the decision of the imprisonment, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the decision: “And so it occurred to those in charge that he should be imprisoned for a while,” or “thumma bada lahum min ba‘di ma ra’aw al-ayati la-yasjununnahu hatta heen,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the household of the ‘Aziz, after seeing the proofs of the innocence of Joseph, decided to imprison him for a time, and the decision is the strange turn, for the innocent was imprisoned and the guilty was left, and the commentators note the word “bada,” the occurring, the appearing, for the idea of the imprisonment occurred to them, and the occurrence is the divine arrangement, the step that the Lord had destined, for the prison was the next stage of the journey. Ibn Kathir notes the irony, for the imprisonment of the innocent was the scandal that the household chose to contain the scandal, and the choice is the measure of the world’s justice, and the word “al-ayati,” the proofs, is the key, for the proofs of the innocence were seen and the imprisonment was still decreed, and the verse preserves the irony as the teaching that the world’s judgment is not the measure of the truth. The second part is the duration: “for a while,” or “hatta heen,” and the commentators note the indefiniteness, for the imprisonment was not the fixed term but the indefinite, and the indefiniteness is the trial, the prison without the known end, and the trial is the preparation of the elevation that the prison would bring.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the world’s judgment, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own patience. The believer is taught that the world sometimes imprisons the innocent, and the proofs of the innocence do not always move the world, and the believer who is wronged by the world is the believer whose patience the Lord will reward, and the prison of Joseph is the proof that the injustice of the world is the road of the Lord, and the elevation that the prison led to is the witness that the patience in the cell is the seed of the throne. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the trust in the hidden: the innocent was imprisoned, and the prison was the step, and the believer who is wronged is the believer whose wrong the Lord will turn, and the “for a while” is the promise that the trial has its term, and the term is the wisdom that the believer awaits. The verse is the record of the decision, and the teaching of the patience that the injustice demands.",
  "36": "“And two other servants went to jail with Joseph. One of them said, “I dreamt I was pressing wine.” The other said, “I dreamt I was carrying ˹some˺ bread on my head, from which birds were eating.” ˹Then both said,˺ “Tell us their interpretation, for we surely see you as one of the good-doers.”” records the meeting with the two prisoners, and the commentators note the two dreams and the request. The verse introduces the two: “And two other servants went to jail with Joseph,” or “wa dakhala ma‘ahu as-sijna fatayan,” and the commentators explain the identity, for the two were the servants of the king, the cupbearer and the baker, and they entered the prison with Joseph, and the prison is the meeting place that the Lord had arranged, and the meeting is the step of the elevation, for the dreams of the two are the keys of the palace. Ibn Kathir notes the identity, for the two were the cupbearer and the baker of the king, and their imprisonment was the occasion of the meeting, and the meeting is the divine arrangement that the story records. The verse then records the dreams: “One of them said, ‘I dreamt I was pressing wine.’ The other said, ‘I dreamt I was carrying some bread on my head, from which birds were eating,’” or “qala ahaduhuma innee aranee a‘siru khamran wa qala al-akharu innee aranee ahmilu fawqa ra’see khubzan ta’kulu at-tayru minhu,” and the commentators note the two dreams, the pressing of the wine and the carrying of the bread, and the two dreams are the two destinies, and the verse records the request: “Tell us their interpretation, for we surely see you as one of the good-doers,” or “nabbi’na bi-ta’weelihi inna naraka mina al-muhsineen,” and the commentators note the request, for the two prisoners asked Joseph for the interpretation, and the request is the recognition of the wisdom, the “muhsineen,” the good-doers, and the recognition is the first step of the elevation, the acknowledgment of the gift that the prison had not hidden.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the meeting, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own providence. The believer is taught that the meetings of the trial are the arrangements of the Lord, and the prison that gathered Joseph and the two servants was the place of the destiny, and the believer who is placed in the trial is the believer whose companions in the trial are the instruments of the deliverance, and the recognition of the wisdom is the door that the believer’s character opens, and the good-doing of Joseph in the prison is the cause of the request that led to the palace. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the value of the character in the trial: Joseph was known as the good-doer in the prison, and the knowledge opened the door, and the believer who does the good in the trial is the believer whose good the Lord will use, and the dreams of the two are the keys that the character of Joseph had earned. The verse is the record of the meeting, and the teaching of the providence that the trial arranges.",
  "37": "“Joseph replied, “I can even tell you what kind of meal you will be served before you receive it. This ˹knowledge˺ is from what my Lord has taught me. I have shunned the faith of a people who disbelieve in Allah and deny the Hereafter.”” records the first part of the reply, and the commentators note the two declarations that the verse joins. The first is the claim of the knowledge: “I can even tell you what kind of meal you will be served before you receive it,” or “qala la ya’teekuma ta‘amun turzaqanihi illa nabba’tukuma bi-ta’weelihi qabla an ya’tiyakuma,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for Joseph declared that he could tell them the interpretation of the dreams, and that he could even tell them the food that they would receive before it came, and the declaration is the display of the gift, the proof of the knowledge that the Lord had taught him, and the commentators note that the display was the preparation of the da‘wah, the introduction of the gift before the call. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the verse is the first part of the reply, and the reply is the model of the messenger, the one who uses the opportunity to call to the Lord, and the display of the gift is the opening of the call. The second part is the declaration of the faith: “This knowledge is from what my Lord has taught me. I have shunned the faith of a people who disbelieve in Allah and deny the Hereafter,” or “thalikuma mimma ‘allamanee rabbee innee taraktu millata qawmin la yu’minoona billahi wa hum bil-akhirati hum kafiroon,” and the commentators note the declaration, for Joseph attributed the knowledge to the Lord, and declared the shunning of the faith of the disbelievers, and the two together are the first step of the call, the attribution of the gift and the severing of the falsehood, and the verse is the opening of the great da‘wah that Joseph delivered in the prison, the call to the oneness that the following verses complete.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the opportunity, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own calling. The believer is taught that the opportunity is the door of the call, and the one who is asked about the world is the one who can answer with the truth, and the believer who is asked for the help is the believer who leads the asker to the Lord, and the attribution of the gift to the Lord is the humility that the believer must hold, and the severing of the falsehood is the first step of the call. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the use of the opportunity: Joseph was asked about the dreams and he called to the Lord, and the believer who is asked for the knowledge is the believer who opens the door of the truth, and the prison of Joseph became the pulpit of the da‘wah. The verse is the opening of the call, and the teaching of the opportunity that the believer must use.",
  "38": "“I follow the faith of my fathers: Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob. It is not ˹right˺ for us to associate anything with Allah ˹in worship˺. This is part of Allah’s grace upon us and humanity, but most people are not grateful.”” continues the declaration of the faith, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the lineage: “I follow the faith of my fathers: Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob,” or “wattaba‘tu millata aba’ee Ibraheema wa Ishaqa wa Ya‘qoob,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for Joseph declared that he followed the faith of the fathers, the “millah” of Abraham and Isaac and Jacob, and the declaration is the connection of the faith to the line of the prophets, and the connection is the answer to the accusation of the innovation, for the faith of Joseph was not the new but the ancient, the faith of the fathers, and the verse is the teaching that the true religion is the religion of the fathers, the millah of Abraham, and the believers are the heirs of the line. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the declaration of the lineage is the declaration of the truth, and the faith of the fathers is the faith that the verse upholds. The second part is the monotheism: “It is not right for us to associate anything with Allah in worship. This is part of Allah’s grace upon us and humanity, but most people are not grateful,” or “ma kana lana an nushrika billahi min shay’in thalika min fadli Allahi ‘alayna wa ‘ala an-nasi wa lakinna akthara an-nasi la yashkuroon,” and the commentators note the two declarations, the impossibility of the association, and the grace of the monotheism, and the closing lament, the ingratitude of the majority, and the verse is the completion of the declaration, the statement of the faith that Joseph held and called to, and the grace of the Lord is the theme, the gift of the faith that the majority neglects.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the lineage, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own faith. The believer is taught that the faith is the inheritance of the fathers, and the believer who holds the millah of Abraham is the believer who stands in the line of the prophets, and the association of the partners is the impossibility that the faith rejects, and the grace of the monotheism is the gift that the believer must not neglect, and the gratitude of the believer is the answer to the grace, and the majority who are not grateful are the warning against the neglect. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the dignity of the faith: Joseph declared the lineage and the monotheism, and the believer who declares the same is the believer whose faith the Lord honours, and the grace of the faith is the gift that the gratitude receives. The verse is the completion of the declaration, and the teaching of the faith that the fathers bequeathed.",
  "39": "“O my fellow-prisoners! Which is far better: many different lords or Allah—the One, the Supreme?”” opens the argument of the oneness, and the commentators note the two parts of the question. The first is the address: “O my fellow-prisoners!” or “ya sahibayi as-sijni,” and the commentators note the address, for Joseph addressed the two prisoners as his companions in the prison, and the address is the brotherhood of the trial, the bond of the cell that the call used, and the address is the gentleness of the caller, the approach of the hearts through the shared condition. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the address is the beginning of the argument, and the argument is the model of the da‘wah, the question that leads the reason to the truth. The second part is the question: “Which is far better: many different lords or Allah—the One, the Supreme?” or “a-arbabun mutafarriqoona khayrun ami Allahu al-wahidu al-qahhar,” and the commentators explain the question, for Joseph asked the two prisoners which was better, the many divided lords or the One God, the “wahid,” the One, and the “qahhar,” the Supreme, the Subduer, and the question is the argument of the reason, the comparison of the many and the One, and the comparison is the demonstration of the absurdity of the many, for the divided lords would dispute and conflict, while the One is the order and the peace, and the question is the invitation to the reason, the argument that the falsehood cannot answer.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the argument, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own da‘wah. The believer is taught that the argument of the oneness is the question, and the question “which is better, the many or the One” is the argument that the reason cannot resist, and the believer who calls to the truth with the question is the believer who leads the reason, and the comparison of the many and the One is the demonstration that the believer must offer, and the prison of Joseph is the model of the da‘wah that the believer delivers in the midst of the trial. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the simplicity of the argument: the many lords are the absurdity, and the One is the truth, and the believer who asks the question is the believer who has delivered the argument, and the question of Joseph is the question that the believer repeats in every age. The verse is the opening of the argument, and the teaching of the question that the reason must answer.",
  "40": "“Whatever ˹idols˺ you worship instead of Him are mere names which you and your forefathers have made up—a practice Allah has never authorized. It is only Allah Who decides. He has commanded that you worship none but Him. That is the upright faith, but most people do not know.”” completes the argument, and the commentators note the four declarations that the verse joins. The first is the exposure of the idols: “Whatever idols you worship instead of Him are mere names which you and your forefathers have made up—a practice Allah has never authorized,” or “ma ta‘budoona min doonihi illa asma’an sammaytumooha antum wa aba’ukum ma anzala Allahu biha min sultan,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the idols are the names, the labels without the reality, and the naming is the invention of the fathers, and the authority is the thing that the Lord never sent, and the exposure is the argument that the falsehood cannot answer, the reduction of the gods to the words. Ibn Kathir notes the force, for the verse is the complete refutation of the idolatry, the declaration that the gods are the names and the authority is absent. The second is the decision: “It is only Allah Who decides,” or “ini al-hukmu illa lillah,” and the commentators note the declaration, for the judgment belongs to the Lord alone, and the declaration is the affirmation of the sovereignty. The third is the command: “He has commanded that you worship none but Him,” or “amara alla ta‘budoo illa iyyahu,” and the commentators note the command, for the worship of the One is the command of the Lord. The fourth is the verdict: “That is the upright faith, but most people do not know,” or “thalika ad-deenu al-qayyimu wa lakinna akthara an-nasi la ya‘lamoon,” and the commentators note the closing, for the upright faith, the “deen al-qayyim,” is the monotheism, and the ignorance of the majority is the lament, and the verse is the complete statement of the faith that Joseph called to.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the refutation, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own argument. The believer is taught that the falsehood is the names, and the truth is the command, and the believer who refutes the falsehood with the argument of the names is the believer who has delivered the proof, and the command of the worship of the One is the command that the believer delivers, and the upright faith is the faith that the believer holds, and the ignorance of the majority is the warning that the believer must not join. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the completeness of the da‘wah: the exposure, the decision, the command, and the verdict, and the believer who delivers the four is the believer whose call is the complete, and the call of Joseph in the prison is the model of the call that the believer repeats. The verse is the completion of the argument, and the teaching of the faith that the reason establishes.",
  "41": "““O my fellow-prisoners! ˹The first˺ one of you will serve wine to his master, and the other will be crucified and the birds will eat from his head. The matter about which you inquired has been decided.”” records the interpretation, and the commentators note the two destinies and the closing. The verse records the interpretation: “The first one of you will serve wine to his master, and the other will be crucified and the birds will eat from his head,” or “ya sahibayi as-sijni amma ahadukuma fa-yasqee rabbahu khamran wa amma al-akharu fa-yuslabu fa-ta’kulu at-tayru min ra’sihi,” and the commentators note the two destinies, for the cupbearer would return to the service of the king, and the baker would be crucified, and the interpretation is the fulfillment of the two dreams, and the commentators note the accuracy, for the interpretation of Joseph was the exact, and the accuracy is the proof of the gift, and the verse closes with the declaration: “The matter about which you inquired has been decided,” or “qudiya al-amru allathee feehi tastaftiyan,” and the commentators note the closing, for the matter was decided, the decree was issued, and the interpretation was the statement of the decree, and the verse is the completion of the scene of the prison, the declaration of the destinies that the following verses fulfill.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the truth, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own honesty. The believer is taught that the interpretation of the truth is the duty of the one who knows, and the truth is not softened by the fear, and Joseph told the baker the truth of the crucifixion, and the telling of the truth is the integrity that the believer must hold, and the interpretation of the dreams is the gift that the believer uses for the truth, and the believer who knows the truth must speak it, whatever the truth may be. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the courage of the truth: Joseph told the two destinies without the softening, and the believer who is asked for the truth is the believer who speaks it, and the accuracy of the interpretation is the witness of the gift, and the gift is the trust that the believer must use for the truth. The verse is the record of the interpretation, and the teaching of the truth that the knowledge demands.",
  "42": "“Then he said to the one he knew would survive, “Mention me in the presence of your master.” But Satan made him forget to mention Joseph to his master, so he remained in prison for several years.”” records the request and the forgetting, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the request: “Then he said to the one he knew would survive, ‘Mention me in the presence of your master,’” or “wa qala lillathee zanna annahu najin minhuma udhkurnee ‘inda rabbika,” and the commentators note the request, for Joseph asked the cupbearer, whom he knew would survive, to mention him to the king, and the request is the human step, the seeking of the means, and the commentators note that the request is not the lack of the trust, for the believer seeks the means and trusts the Lord, and the request of Joseph is the model of the seeking of the means, and the commentators differ on the significance, and the dominant understanding is that the request was the permissible, the use of the means, and the verse records it without the censure. Ibn Kathir notes the context, for the request is the seeking of the deliverance through the cupbearer, and the seeking is the sunnah of the believer, the use of the means with the trust. The second part is the forgetting: “But Satan made him forget to mention Joseph to his master, so he remained in prison for several years,” or “fa-ansahu ash-shaytanu thikra rabbihi fa-labitha fi as-sijni bid‘a sineen,” and the commentators note the forgetting, for the cupbearer forgot the request, and the forgetting is attributed to Satan, and the result is the several years of the prison, and the commentators note the number, for the “bid‘a sineen” is the several years, the reports say seven or more, and the years are the second trial, the extension of the prison that the forgetting caused, and the verse teaches the believer that the means sometimes fails, and the failure is the trial that the Lord extends, and the patience in the extension is the seed of the elevation that the dream of the king would bring.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the means, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the means is sought and the Lord is trusted, and the failure of the means is not the failure of the Lord, and the believer who is forgotten by the creation is the believer whom the Lord remembers, and the years of the prison are the trial that the believer endures, and the endurance is the preparation of the deliverance that the Lord has appointed, and the forgetting of the cupbearer is the teaching that the reliance upon the creature is the reliance that fails, and the reliance upon the Lord is the reliance that holds. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the balance of the means and the trust: Joseph sought the cupbearer and the cupbearer forgot, and the believer who seeks the means and is disappointed is the believer whose trust the Lord will test, and the several years are the span of the patience that the Lord rewards. The verse is the record of the request and the forgetting, and the teaching of the means that the trust must not replace.",
  "43": "“And ˹one day˺ the King said, “I dreamt of seven fat cows eaten up by seven skinny ones; and seven green ears of grain and ˹seven˺ others dry. O chiefs! Tell me the meaning of my dream if you can interpret dreams.”” records the dream of the king, and the commentators note the dream and the request. The verse introduces the dream: “And one day the King said, ‘I dreamt of seven fat cows eaten up by seven skinny ones; and seven green ears of grain and seven others dry,’” or “wa qala al-maliku innee ara sab‘a baqaratin simanin ya’kuluhunna sab‘un ‘ijafun wa sab‘a sunbulatin khudrin wa ukhara yabisat,” and the commentators explain the dream, for the king saw the seven fat cows devoured by the seven skinny ones, and the seven green ears and the seven dry ones, and the dream is the vision of the years of the plenty and the famine, and the dream is the key of the deliverance, the divine arrangement that would bring Joseph from the prison to the palace, and the commentators note the detail, for the dream of the king is the second of the dreams that the chapter records, and the dream is the means by which the Lord opened the door. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the dream of the king is the occasion of the release of Joseph, and the dream is the thread that the Lord had prepared. The verse then records the request: “O chiefs! Tell me the meaning of my dream if you can interpret dreams,” or “ya ayyuha al-mala’u aftoonee fee ru’yaya in kuntum lir-ru’ya ta‘buroon,” and the commentators note the request, for the king asked the chiefs for the interpretation, and the request is the test of the wisdom of the court, and the test would fail, and the failure is the occasion of the remembrance of Joseph, and the verse is the opening of the scene that the following verses complete.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the dream, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own providence. The believer is taught that the dreams of the kings and the events of the world are the threads that the Lord weaves, and the dream of the king is the means of the deliverance, and the believer who trusts the Lord sees the thread in the event, and the deliverance that the believer awaits is the deliverance that the Lord has already arranged, and the failure of the chiefs is the occasion of the success of the truth, and the believer who is patient in the cell is the believer whose door the dream will open. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the providence of the events: the dream came and the door opened, and the believer who sees the hand of the Lord in the events is the believer whose patience is the confident, and the dream of the king is the proof that the trial of Joseph had its appointed end. The verse is the record of the dream, and the teaching of the providence that the events serve.",
  "44": "“They replied, “These are confused visions and we do not know the interpretation of such dreams.”” records the failure of the chiefs, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The first is the dismissal: “These are confused visions,” or “qaloo adghathu ahlam,” and the commentators explain the “adghath,” the confused, the jumbled, the false dreams, for the chiefs dismissed the dream of the king as the confused visions, the “adghath ahlam,” the tangled dreams, and the dismissal is the confession of the ignorance, the failure of the wisdom of the court, and the word is the measure of the poverty, the declaration that the dream was the chaos that the chiefs could not read. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the “adghath” are the dreams of the mixed, the visions that the interpreter cannot untangle, and the dismissal is the last word of the court that had no answer. The second part is the confession: “and we do not know the interpretation of such dreams,” or “wa ma nahnu bi-ta’weeli al-ahlami bi-‘alimeen,” and the commentators note the confession, for the chiefs declared their ignorance of the interpretation, and the confession is the honesty of the failure, and the failure is the occasion of the remembrance of Joseph, for the cupbearer, hearing the failure, would remember the man of the prison, and the confession of the chiefs is the door of the truth, and the verse preserves the failure as the step of the elevation, the confession of the court that the truth would answer.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the failure, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own occasion. The believer is taught that the failure of the falsehood is the occasion of the truth, and the ignorance of the chiefs is the door of the knowledge of Joseph, and the believer who trusts the Lord sees the failure of the false as the preparation of the true, and the confession of the ignorance is the step that the believer must not despise, for the confession of the false is the opening of the true. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the providence of the failure: the chiefs failed and confessed, and the confession opened the door, and the believer who is patient while the falsehood fails is the believer whose time the Lord has prepared, and the failure of the court is the witness that the wisdom of the world is not the wisdom of the truth. The verse is the record of the failure, and the teaching of the occasion that the falsehood’s ignorance opens.",
  "45": "“˹Finally,˺ the surviving ex-prisoner remembered ˹Joseph˺ after a long time and said, “I will tell you its interpretation, so send me forth ˹to Joseph˺.”” records the remembrance, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the remembrance: “Finally, the surviving ex-prisoner remembered Joseph after a long time,” or “wa qala allathee naja minhuma waddakara ba‘da ummatin,” and the commentators note the word “waddakara,” the remembrance, and the “ummah,” the long time, for the cupbearer remembered Joseph after the long years, and the remembrance is the awakening, the return of the memory that the years had buried, and the awakening is the divine arrangement, the timing of the remembrance at the moment of the need, and the commentators note the detail, for the forgetting of the cupbearer was the trial, and the remembrance is the end of the trial, and the two together are the span of the patience. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the remembrance came at the moment of the dream, and the timing is the providence, the awakening of the memory when the deliverance was due. The second part is the offer: “I will tell you its interpretation, so send me forth to Joseph,” or “ana unabbi’ukum bi-ta’weelihi fa-arsiloon,” and the commentators note the offer, for the cupbearer offered the interpretation and asked to be sent to Joseph, and the offer is the recognition of the gift, the confession that the interpretation was with the man of the prison, and the request is the means of the meeting, and the verse is the record of the remembrance and the offer, the step that the following verses complete.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the remembrance, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own timing. The believer is taught that the forgetting of the creation is not the forgetting of the Lord, and the remembrance that the creature delays is the remembrance that the Lord appoints, and the believer who is forgotten by the world is the believer whom the Lord remembers at the hour, and the timing of the remembrance is the proof that the deliverance has its appointed time, and the believer who is patient in the waiting is the believer whose time the Lord has set. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the providence of the timing: the cupbearer remembered after the long time, and the remembrance was at the hour, and the believer who is forgotten is the believer whose remembrance the Lord arranges, and the offer of the cupbearer is the door that the patience of Joseph had earned. The verse is the record of the remembrance, and the teaching of the timing that the Lord appoints.",
  "46": "“˹He said,˺ “Joseph, O man of truth! Interpret for us ˹the dream of˺ seven fat cows eaten up by seven skinny ones; and seven green ears of grain and ˹seven˺ others dry, so that I may return to the people and let them know.”” records the request to Joseph, and the commentators note the address and the dream. The first is the address: “Joseph, O man of truth!” or “Yoosufu ayyuha as-siddeequ,” and the commentators note the title, for the cupbearer addressed Joseph as the “siddeeq,” the man of the truth, the truthful, and the title is the recognition of the virtue, the acknowledgment of the truthfulness that the prison had proven, and the title is the honour that the cupbearer gave to the one he had forgotten, and the verse preserves the title as the witness of the character, the truth that the years had not erased. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the “siddeeq” is the title of the truthful, and the title is the recognition that the cupbearer had known and forgotten, and the recognition is the return of the memory with the honour. The second part is the request: “Interpret for us the dream of seven fat cows eaten up by seven skinny ones; and seven green ears of grain and seven others dry, so that I may return to the people and let them know,” or “aftina fee sab‘i baqaratin simanin ya’kuluhunna sab‘un ‘ijafun wa sab‘i sunbulatin khudrin wa ukhara yabisatin la‘allee arji‘u ila an-nasi la‘allahum ya‘lamoon,” and the commentators note the request, for the cupbearer repeated the dream and asked for the interpretation, and the request is the fulfillment of the earlier offer, and the repetition of the dream is the accuracy of the transmission, and the verse is the record of the request, the step that the interpretation of the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the title, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reputation. The believer is taught that the truth is the reputation that endures, and the one who is known as the truthful is the one whose word the world seeks, and the believer who lives the truth is the believer whom the years cannot forget, and the title “siddeeq” is the honour that the truthfulness earns, and the believer who seeks the honour of the title must live the truth of the title. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the value of the truth: Joseph was remembered as the man of the truth, and the believer who lives the truth is the believer whom the world remembers, and the request of the cupbearer is the proof that the character of Joseph had survived the years of the prison. The verse is the record of the request, and the teaching of the truth that the years preserve.",
  "47": "“Joseph replied, “You will plant ˹grain˺ for seven consecutive years, leaving in the ear whatever you will harvest, except for the little you will eat.”” records the first part of the interpretation, and the commentators note the plan and its wisdom. The verse records the interpretation: “You will plant grain for seven consecutive years, leaving in the ear whatever you will harvest, except for the little you will eat,” or “qala tazra‘oona sab‘a sineenin da’aban fa-ma hasadtum fa-tharoohu fee sunbulihi illa qaleelan mimma ta’kuloon,” and the commentators explain the interpretation, for Joseph declared that the people would plant for seven years, and the command was to leave the harvest in the ear, the storing of the grain in the ear for the preservation, and the wisdom of the storing in the ear is the detail that the commentators note, for the grain in the ear is the grain that the years cannot spoil, and the exception of the little that they would eat is the provision of the seven years, and the interpretation is the plan, the economic wisdom that the dream had hidden, and the verse is the first of the three verses of the interpretation, the seven years of the plenty and the preparation. Ibn Kathir notes the wisdom, for the storing in the ear is the method of the preservation, and the interpretation is the proof of the gift, the wisdom that the court had failed to find.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the wisdom, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own prudence. The believer is taught that the wisdom of the interpretation is the wisdom of the provision, and the one who knows the years of the plenty is the one who stores for the years of the famine, and the believer who is given the knowledge is the believer whose knowledge the Lord uses for the benefit of the people, and the storing in the ear is the teaching of the prudence, the preparation that the believer must make, and the wisdom of Joseph is the model of the knowledge that serves the community. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the value of the wisdom: Joseph interpreted the dream with the plan, and the believer who is given the knowledge is the believer whose knowledge is the plan, and the prudence of the storing is the duty that the knowledge imposes, and the wisdom of Joseph is the proof that the gift of the Lord is the gift that serves. The verse is the first part of the interpretation, and the teaching of the wisdom that the knowledge carries.",
  "48": "“Then after that will come seven years of great hardship which will consume whatever you have saved, except the little you will store ˹for seed˺.”” records the second part of the interpretation, and the commentators note the famine and the provision. The verse continues the interpretation: “Then after that will come seven years of great hardship which will consume whatever you have saved, except the little you will store for seed,” or “thumma ya’tee min ba‘di thalika sab‘un shidadun ya’kulna ma qaddamtum lahunna illa qaleelan mimma tuhsinoon,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the seven years of the plenty would be followed by the seven years of the hardship, the “shidad,” the severe, the famine that would consume the stored grain, and the exception of the little that they would keep for the seed, and the interpretation is the complete plan, the plenty and the famine and the preservation, and the commentators note the accuracy, for the dream of the seven fat cows and the seven skinny ones was the vision of the two periods, and the interpretation of Joseph was the exact, and the verse is the second of the three verses, the warning of the famine that the plan would meet.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the foresight, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own preparation. The believer is taught that the plenty is the preparation of the famine, and the one who stores in the plenty is the one who survives the hardship, and the believer who is given the warning of the future is the believer whose warning the Lord will vindicate, and the foresight of Joseph is the model of the prudence, the knowledge of the future that the Lord grants to the one whom He chooses, and the believer who plans with the wisdom of the Lord is the believer whose plan the years confirm. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the duty of the foresight: the seven years of the plenty were the seed of the seven years of the famine, and the believer who uses the plenty for the famine is the believer whose wisdom the Lord rewards, and the warning of Joseph is the proof that the knowledge of the future is the gift that the believer must use. The verse is the second part of the interpretation, and the teaching of the foresight that the plenty demands.",
  "49": "“Then after that will come a year in which people will receive abundant rain and they will press ˹oil and wine˺.”” completes the interpretation, and the commentators note the year of the relief. The verse completes the interpretation: “Then after that will come a year in which people will receive abundant rain and they will press oil and wine,” or “thumma ya’tee min ba‘di thalika ‘amun feehi yughathu an-nasu wa feehi ya‘siroon,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the year after the famine would be the year of the relief, the “yughathu,” the rain that would come to the people, and the pressing of the oil and the wine, the return of the abundance, and the interpretation is the complete, the plenty and the famine and the relief, and the commentators note the detail, for Joseph added the year of the relief to the interpretation, and the addition is the proof of the gift, the knowledge that the dream had not mentioned, and the verse is the completion of the interpretation, the plan that the king would accept.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the relief, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the famine is followed by the relief, and the one who endures the years of the hardship is the one whom the year of the abundance finds, and the believer who trusts the Lord in the trial is the believer whose relief the Lord appoints, and the year of the rain is the promise that the hardship has its term, and the believer who is patient in the famine is the believer whose patience the relief rewards. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the hope of the relief: the interpretation ended with the year of the abundance, and the believer who reads the end of the trial in the promise of the Lord is the believer whose patience is the confident, and the rain that Joseph promised is the symbol of the mercy that follows the hardship. The verse is the completion of the interpretation, and the teaching of the relief that the hardship precedes.",
  "50": "“The King ˹then˺ said, “Bring him to me.” When the messenger came to him, Joseph said, “Go back to your master and ask him about the case of the women who cut their hands. Surely my Lord has ˹full˺ knowledge of their cunning.”” records the summons and the condition, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the summons: “The King then said, ‘Bring him to me,’” or “wa qala al-maliku i’toonee bihi,” and the commentators note the summons, for the king, having heard the interpretation, commanded the bringing of Joseph, and the summons is the first step of the release, the recognition of the wisdom, and the verse records the command. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the summons is the door of the elevation, and the door was opened by the interpretation that the court had failed to find. The second part is the condition: “When the messenger came to him, Joseph said, ‘Go back to your master and ask him about the case of the women who cut their hands. Surely my Lord has full knowledge of their cunning,’” or “fa-lamma ja’ahu ar-rasoolu qala irji‘ ila rabbika fas’alhu ma balu an-niswati allatee qatta‘na aydiyahunna inna rabbee bi-kaydihinna ‘aleem,” and the commentators note the condition, for Joseph refused to leave the prison until the case of the women was examined, and the refusal is the demand of the vindication, the clearing of the name before the release, and the demand is the dignity of the innocent, the refusal to leave the prison as the guilty, and the verse preserves the condition as the teaching, for the innocent does not accept the release until the truth is cleared, and the knowledge of the Lord of the cunning is the ground of the demand, the certainty that the truth would be known.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the vindication, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own dignity. The believer is taught that the release is not the end until the truth is cleared, and the innocent who is imprisoned must demand the clearing of the name, and the believer who is wronged is the believer whose vindication the Lord will establish, and the condition of Joseph is the model of the dignity, the refusal to leave the cell as the guilty, and the knowledge of the Lord of the cunning is the comfort of the one whose innocence the falsehood concealed. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the value of the honour: Joseph refused the release until the truth was known, and the believer who is wronged is the believer whose honour the Lord will restore, and the case of the women is the witness that the truth of the innocent is the truth that the Lord exposes. The verse is the record of the summons and the condition, and the teaching of the dignity that the innocence demands.",
  "51": "“The King asked ˹the women˺, “What did you get when you tried to seduce Joseph?” They replied, “Allah forbid! We know nothing indecent about him.” Then the Chief Minister’s wife admitted, “Now the truth has come to light. It was I who tried to seduce him, and he is surely truthful.”” records the examination and the confession, and the commentators note the three parts of the scene. The first is the question: “The King asked the women, ‘What did you get when you tried to seduce Joseph?’” or “qala ma khatbukunna ith rawadtunna Yoosufa ‘an nafsihi,” and the commentators note the question, for the king summoned the women and asked them of the seduction, and the question is the examination that Joseph had demanded, and the verse records the question as the fulfillment of the condition. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the question of the king is the vindication, and the vindication is the work of the Lord, the exposure of the truth that the falsehood had concealed. The second part is the reply: “They replied, ‘Allah forbid! We know nothing indecent about him,’” or “qulna hasha lillahi ma ‘alimna ‘alayhi min soo’,” and the commentators note the reply, for the women declared the innocence of Joseph, and the declaration is the second vindication, the testimony of the women, and the word “hasha lillah” — God forbid — is the exclamation of the innocence, the declaration that no evil was known of Joseph, and the testimony is the exposure of the truth. The third part is the confession: “Then the Chief Minister’s wife admitted, ‘Now the truth has come to light. It was I who tried to seduce him, and he is surely truthful,’” or “qalati imra’atu al-‘azeezi al-ana hashasa al-haqqu ana rawadtuhu ‘an nafsihi wa innahu la-mina as-sadiqeen,” and the commentators note the confession, for the lady confessed the truth, and the confession is the complete vindication, the admission of the guilt and the declaration of the innocence, and the verse preserves the confession as the proof that the truth of Joseph was the truth, and the three parts together are the full examination, the question, the testimony, and the confession, and the three are the vindication that the patience of Joseph had earned.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the vindication, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own truth. The believer is taught that the truth is the thing that the time exposes, and the innocent who is patient is the innocent whom the Lord vindicates, and the confession of the guilty is the witness of the innocent, and the believer who is wronged is the believer whose wrong the Lord will clear, and the patience of Joseph in the prison is the patience that the confession rewarded, and the believer who endures the false accusation is the believer whose truth the Lord will establish. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the certainty of the vindication: the women testified and the lady confessed, and the believer who is falsely accused is the believer whose accuser the Lord will turn into the witness, and the confession of the lady is the proof that the truth is the thing that the falsehood cannot bury. The verse is the record of the examination, and the teaching of the vindication that the truth earns.",
  "52": "“From this, Joseph should know that I did not speak dishonestly about him in his absence, for Allah certainly does not guide the scheming of the dishonest.”” records the continuation of the confession, and the commentators note the speaker and the meaning. The verse is the continuation of the speech of the lady: “From this, Joseph should know that I did not speak dishonestly about him in his absence,” or “thalika li-ya‘lama annee lam akhunhu bil-ghaybi,” and the commentators explain the meaning, for the lady declared that her confession was made so that Joseph would know that she had not betrayed him in his absence, and the declaration is the completion of the honesty, the clearing of the conscience, and the commentators note the significance, for the lady, having confessed the seduction, now declared that she had not slandered Joseph in his absence, and the declaration is the full confession, the admission of the one fault and the denial of the other, and the honesty of the confession is the fruit of the truth that had come to light. Ibn Kathir notes the interpretation, for some of the commentators hold that the words are the speech of Joseph, the declaration that he had not betrayed the ‘Aziz in his absence, and the difference is the richness of the reports, and the meaning of the honesty is the same, the declaration of the innocence of the one who was accused. The verse closes with the principle: “for Allah certainly does not guide the scheming of the dishonest,” or “wa anna Allaha la yahdee kayda al-kha’ineen,” and the commentators note the principle, for the scheming of the dishonest is the scheme that the Lord does not guide, and the principle is the teaching that the falsehood is the thing that the Lord frustrates, and the confession of the lady is the proof that the scheme of the falsehood had failed, and the truth had prevailed.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the honesty, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own sincerity. The believer is taught that the honesty is the fruit of the truth, and the one who confesses the fault is the one whom the truth has reached, and the believer who is wronged is the believer whose innocence the honesty of the truth will declare, and the principle that the Lord does not guide the scheme of the dishonest is the assurance that the falsehood is the thing that the Lord frustrates, and the believer who trusts the truth is the believer whose truth the Lord will establish. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the certainty of the failure of the scheme: the confession came and the scheme fell, and the believer who is wronged by the scheme is the believer whose vindication the Lord arranges, and the principle of the verse is the comfort of the one whose innocence the falsehood attacked. The verse is the continuation of the confession, and the teaching of the honesty that the truth produces.",
  "53": "“And I do not seek to free myself from blame, for indeed the soul is ever inclined to evil, except those shown mercy by my Lord. Surely my Lord is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.”” completes the confession with the humility, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the disclaimer: “And I do not seek to free myself from blame, for indeed the soul is ever inclined to evil, except those shown mercy by my Lord,” or “wa ma ubarri’u nafsee inna an-nafsa la-ammaratun bis-soo’i illa ma rahima rabbee,” and the commentators note the declaration, for the lady declared that she did not absolve herself, and the declaration is the humility, the acknowledgment that the soul is the “ammarah bis-su’,” the inciter of the evil, the commander of the wrong, and the exception is the mercy of the Lord, the “illa ma rahima rabbee,” the ones whom the mercy of the Lord protects, and the verse is the Quran’s great statement of the nature of the soul, the declaration that the soul commands the evil unless the mercy of the Lord intervenes, and the commentators note the depth of the verse, for it is the diagnosis of the human condition, and the diagnosis is the key of the reliance, the knowledge that the safety of the soul is the mercy, not the strength. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the verse is the confession of the lady and the teaching of the soul, and the teaching is the foundation of the reliance, the knowledge that the believer’s protection is the mercy of the Lord. The second part is the closing: “Surely my Lord is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful,” or “inna rabbee ghafoorun raheem,” and the commentators note the closing, for the forgiveness and the mercy are the two names that the confession seeks, and the closing is the hope, the trust that the Lord of the forgiveness and the mercy receives the one who confesses.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the soul, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reliance. The believer is taught that the soul is the commander of the evil, and the safety of the believer is the mercy of the Lord, and the one who knows the nature of the soul is the one who does not trust his own strength, and the reliance upon the Lord is the fruit of the knowledge, and the believer who confesses the weakness of the soul is the believer whose refuge is the mercy, and the forgiveness and the mercy are the two names that the believer seeks. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the foundation of the reliance: the soul commands the evil, and the mercy protects, and the believer who knows the two is the believer whose trust is the Lord, and the confession of the lady is the model of the humility that the knowledge of the soul produces. The verse is the completion of the confession, and the teaching of the soul that the mercy alone restrains.",
  "54": "“The King said, “Bring him to me. I will employ him exclusively in my service.” And when Joseph spoke to him, the King said, “Today you are highly esteemed and fully trusted by us.”” records the release and the honour, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the release: “The King said, ‘Bring him to me. I will employ him exclusively in my service,’” or “wa qala al-maliku i’toonee bihi astakhlishu li-nafsee,” and the commentators note the command, for the king commanded the bringing of Joseph, and the word “astakhlishu” — I will take him exclusively for myself — is the declaration of the honour, the selection of Joseph for the private service, and the command is the elevation, the reversal of the fortune, the slave of the well becoming the chosen of the king, and the verse records the command as the fulfillment of the dream, the first step of the prostration that the stars had foretold. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the release is the turning point, and the turning point is the work of the Lord, the elevation of the patient, and the selection of the king is the honour that the prison had prepared. The second part is the reception: “And when Joseph spoke to him, the King said, ‘Today you are highly esteemed and fully trusted by us,’” or “fa-lamma kallamahu qala innaka al-yawma ladayna makeenun ameen,” and the commentators note the reception, for the king, after speaking with Joseph, declared him the “makeen,” the esteemed, the established, and the “ameen,” the trusted, and the two words are the two honours, the station and the trust, and the reception is the proof of the wisdom, the recognition of the character that the prison had not broken, and the verse is the record of the release and the honour, the elevation that the patience earned.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the elevation, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own patience. The believer is taught that the patience in the trial is the seed of the elevation, and the prisoner of the well became the trusted of the king, and the believer who endures the trial with the patience is the believer whom the Lord elevates, and the honour of Joseph is the proof that the station is the gift of the Lord, not the accident of the fortune, and the believer who is lowly in the eyes of the world is the believer whose elevation the Lord has written. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reward of the patience: the prison ended and the honour began, and the believer who is patient in the cell is the believer whose cell the Lord will turn to the station, and the trust of the king is the witness of the character that the trials had proven. The verse is the record of the release and the honour, and the teaching of the elevation that the patience earns.",
  "55": "“Joseph proposed, “Put me in charge of the store-houses of the land, for I am truly reliable and adept.”” records the proposal of Joseph, and the commentators note the two qualities and their significance. The verse records the proposal: “Put me in charge of the store-houses of the land, for I am truly reliable and adept,” or “qala ij‘alnee ‘ala khaza’ini al-ardi innee hafeezun ‘aleem,” and the commentators note the proposal, for Joseph asked the king to place him over the storehouses of the land, and the request is the seeking of the office, and the commentators discuss the permissibility, for the seeking of the position is the permissible when the seeker is the qualified, and the verse records the two qualifications that Joseph cited: the “hafeez,” the reliable, the keeper, and the “‘aleem,” the adept, the knowledgeable, and the two are the two pillars of the office, the trust and the knowledge, and the commentators note the significance, for Joseph declared his qualifications, and the declaration is the seeking of the duty, the offering of the service, and the proposal is the model of the one who seeks the office for the service, not for the honour, and the commentators note that the proposal was made in the face of the famine, and the office was the means of the saving of the people, and the seeking of the office was the seeking of the service. Ibn Kathir notes the two qualities, for the “hafeez” is the one who guards the trust, and the “‘aleem” is the one who knows the management, and the two together are the complete qualification, and the proposal is the proof of the wisdom, the offer of the qualified to the king.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the qualification, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own service. The believer is taught that the office is the trust, and the one who seeks the office must be the qualified, and the two qualifications of the trust and the knowledge are the two conditions of the service, and the believer who is given the office is the believer whose office is the worship, and the seeking of the office for the service is the permissible, and the seeking of it for the honour is the forbidden, and the proposal of Joseph is the model of the seeker who offers the service, and the believer who seeks the duty with the qualification is the believer whose duty the Lord blesses. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the manner of the office: Joseph asked for the office and cited the qualifications, and the believer who seeks the office must seek it with the qualification and for the service, and the storehouses of Joseph are the symbol of the trust that the believer must guard. The verse is the record of the proposal, and the teaching of the qualification that the office demands.",
  "56": "“This is how We established Joseph in the land to settle wherever he pleased. We shower Our mercy on whoever We will, and We never discount the reward of the good-doers.”” records the establishment and the principle, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the establishment: “This is how We established Joseph in the land to settle wherever he pleased,” or “wa kathalika makkanna li-Yoosufa fi al-ardi yatabawwa’u minha haythu yashao,” and the commentators note the phrase, for the establishment of Joseph is the completion of the promise, the fulfillment of the word that the chapter had repeated, and the establishment is the elevation of the patient, the grant of the dominion, and the word “yatabawwa’u” — he settles wherever he pleases — is the description of the freedom, the station of the one whom the Lord had raised from the well, and the establishment is the proof that the trials were the roads, and the roads ended in the dominion. Ibn Kathir notes the repetition, for the phrase “kathalika makkanna” is the refrain of the chapter, and the refrain is the teaching that the establishment is the work of the Lord, and the work is the reward of the patience. The second part is the principle: “We shower Our mercy on whoever We will, and We never discount the reward of the good-doers,” or “nuseebu bi-rahmatina man nashao wa la nudee‘u ajra al-muhsineen,” and the commentators note the two clauses, the mercy that the Lord showers upon whom He wills, and the reward of the good-doers that the Lord never discounts, and the two together are the teaching, for the mercy is the gift and the reward is the due, and the good-doers are the ones whose reward the Lord preserves, and the principle is the assurance that the patience of Joseph was not lost, and the reward of the good-doer is the reward that the Lord never wastes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the establishment, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the establishment is the work of the Lord, and the one whom the Lord establishes is the one whom the trials have tested, and the believer who endures the trials with the patience is the believer whom the Lord establishes, and the mercy is the gift that the Lord showers, and the reward of the good-doer is the reward that the Lord preserves, and the believer who does the good is the believer whose good the Lord will not discount. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the certainty of the reward: Joseph was established and the reward was not lost, and the believer who is patient in the trial is the believer whose establishment the Lord arranges, and the principle of the verse is the comfort of the good-doer, the assurance that the good is the seed that the Lord harvests. The verse is the record of the establishment, and the teaching of the reward that the patience earns.",
  "57": "“And the reward of the Hereafter is far better for those who are faithful and are mindful ˹of Allah˺.”” completes the account with the comparison, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse states the comparison: “And the reward of the Hereafter is far better for those who are faithful and are mindful of Allah,” or “wa la-ajru al-akhirati khayrun lillatheena amanoo wa kanoo yattaqoon,” and the commentators note the comparison, for the reward of the Hereafter is the better, the “khayr,” than the establishment of the world, and the comparison is the teaching, for the honour of Joseph in the world was the small, and the reward of the Hereafter is the great, and the believer who is given the world is the believer whose eye must remain on the Hereafter, and the two conditions are named: the “amanu,” the faith, and the “ittaqaw,” the mindfulness, and the two are the keys of the reward, the faith and the taqwa, and the verse is the correction of the perspective, the reminder that the elevation of Joseph was the sign and the Hereafter is the substance. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the verse is the closing of the establishment, and the closing is the teaching that the world is the small and the Hereafter is the great, and the believer who measures the two aright is the believer whose heart the world cannot capture.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the comparison, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own perspective. The believer is taught that the reward of the Hereafter is the better, and the establishment of the world is the small, and the believer who is given the world is the believer whose gratitude must not become the attachment, and the faith and the mindfulness are the two conditions of the better reward, and the believer who holds the two is the believer whose eye is on the Hereafter, and the story of Joseph is the proof that the world is the test and the Hereafter is the reward. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the scale of the reward: the establishment of Joseph was the small, and the Hereafter is the great, and the believer who reads the story is the believer who keeps the scale, and the faith and the mindfulness are the two keys of the greater reward. The verse is the completion of the account, and the teaching of the scale that the believer must keep.",
  "58": "“And Joseph’s brothers came and entered his presence. He recognized them but they were unaware of who he really was.”” records the arrival of the brothers, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the arrival: “And Joseph’s brothers came and entered his presence,” or “wa ja’a ikhwatu Yoosufa fa-dakhaloo ‘alayhi,” and the commentators explain the scene, for the brothers of Joseph, driven by the famine, came to Egypt to seek the grain, and the arrival is the fulfillment of the dream, the first step of the prostration of the stars, and the commentators note the detail, for the brothers came to the one they had cast into the well, and they did not know him, and the ignorance is the key of the scene. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the arrival is the beginning of the reunion, and the reunion is the fulfillment of the prophecy that the chapter had opened with, and the arrival of the brothers is the hand of the Lord, the bringing of the family to the one whom they had wronged. The second part is the recognition: “He recognized them but they were unaware of who he really was,” or “fa-‘arafahum wa hum lahu munkiroon,” and the commentators note the two sides, for Joseph recognized the brothers, and the brothers did not recognize Joseph, and the two sides are the two states, the knowledge of the wronged and the ignorance of the wrongdoers, and the verse preserves the two states as the teaching, for the years and the station had changed Joseph, and the brothers saw the minister and not the brother, and the ignorance is the occasion of the test that the following verses record, the trial of the brothers before the recognition, and the commentators note the word “munkiroon,” the unaware, the deniers, for the brothers did not know the one before them, and the ignorance is the irony, the wrongdoers standing before the wronged without the recognition.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the recognition, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own providence. The believer is taught that the reunion is the work of the Lord, and the wrongdoers are brought to the wronged without the recognition, and the believer who is wronged is the believer whose wrongdoers the Lord will bring before him, and the recognition of Joseph is the knowledge of the one who trusted the Lord, and the ignorance of the brothers is the blindness of the one who had wronged, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose reunion the Lord arranges. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the providence: the brothers came and did not know, and the believer who is wronged is the believer whose wrongdoers the Lord will bring, and the recognition of Joseph is the proof that the wronged is not forgotten, and the test of the brothers is the mercy that the wronged will show. The verse is the record of the arrival, and the teaching of the providence that the reunion serves.",
  "59": "“When he had provided them with their supplies, he demanded, “Bring me your brother on your father’s side. Do you not see that I give full measure and I am the best of hosts?”” records the first demand of Joseph, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the provision: “When he had provided them with their supplies,” or “wa lamma jahhazahum bi-jahazihim,” and the commentators note the detail, for Joseph gave the brothers their supplies of the grain, and the giving is the generosity of the minister, the full measure that the verse mentions, and the provision is the first act of the meeting, the kindness that the brothers received from the one they did not know. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the provision is the beginning of the plan, and the plan is the test that Joseph had arranged, and the generosity is the mask of the plan. The second part is the demand: “Bring me your brother on your father’s side. Do you not see that I give full measure and I am the best of hosts?” or “qala i’toonee bi-akhin lakum min abeekum a-la tarawna annee oofee al-kayla wa ana khayru al-munzileen,” and the commentators note the demand, for Joseph asked the brothers to bring their brother on the father’s side, the Benjamin, and the demand is the first step of the reunion, the means by which Joseph would bring his full brother, and the argument of the full measure and the best of the hosts is the reason, the proof that the brothers should return, and the verse is the opening of the test, the plan that the following verses unfold.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the plan, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own wisdom. The believer is taught that the plan of the wise is the means of the good, and the demand of Joseph was the plan of the reunion, and the believer who is given the wisdom is the believer whose wisdom the Lord uses for the good, and the generosity of the provision is the mask that the plan wore, and the believer who plans with the wisdom of the Lord is the believer whose plan the Lord guides. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the wisdom of the means: Joseph demanded the brother and cited the generosity, and the believer who seeks the good through the means is the believer whose means the Lord blesses, and the demand of Joseph is the first step of the reunion that the dream had foretold. The verse is the record of the demand, and the teaching of the wisdom that the reunion required.",
  "60": "““But if you do not bring him to me ˹next time˺, I will have no grain for you, nor will you ever come close to me again.”” records the condition and the threat, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the condition: “But if you do not bring him to me next time, I will have no grain for you,” or “fa-in lam ta’toonee bihi fa-la kayla lakum ‘indee,” and the commentators note the condition, for Joseph made the supply of the grain conditional upon the bringing of the brother, and the condition is the pressure of the plan, the means by which the brothers would be compelled to bring Benjamin, and the condition is the wisdom of the test, the arrangement that the reunion required. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the condition is the second step of the plan, and the plan is the bringing of the full brother, and the condition is the pressure that the plan used. The second part is the threat: “nor will you ever come close to me again,” or “wa la taqraboon,” and the commentators note the threat, for Joseph declared that the brothers would not approach him again without the brother, and the threat is the completion of the condition, the sealing of the demand, and the verse is the record of the condition and the threat, the pressure that the following verse shows the brothers accepting, and the commentators note that the plan of Joseph was the wisdom, not the cruelty, for the end of the plan was the reunion and the provision of the family, and the condition was the means of the good.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the means, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own prudence. The believer is taught that the means of the good is sometimes the pressure, and the condition that Joseph imposed was the pressure of the reunion, and the believer who seeks the good of the family is the believer whose plan the Lord guides, and the threat of the withholding is the instrument of the plan, and the believer who plans with the wisdom is the believer whose instruments the Lord blesses. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the legitimacy of the wise means: Joseph set the condition and the threat, and the condition and the threat were the means of the reunion, and the believer who seeks the good through the wise means is the believer whose means the Lord uses, and the plan of Joseph is the proof that the wisdom is the gift that the believer must use. The verse is the record of the condition, and the teaching of the means that the wisdom employs.",
  "61": "“They promised, “We will try to convince his father to let him come. We will certainly do ˹our best˺.”” records the promise of the brothers, and the commentators note the two parts of the promise. The verse records the promise: “We will try to convince his father to let him come. We will certainly do our best,” or “qaloo sa-nurawidu ‘anhu abahu wa inna la-fa‘iloon,” and the commentators note the promise, for the brothers pledged to persuade their father to send Benjamin, and the word “sa-nurawidu” — we will try to convince, we will seek — is the same root as the seduction of the lady, and the commentators note the irony, for the word that the story used for the seduction is now used for the persuasion of the father, and the irony is the teaching that the words of the brothers were the persuasion, and the persuasion is the means of the promise, and the second part: “We will certainly do our best,” or “wa inna la-fa‘iloon,” and the commentators note the emphasis, for the brothers stressed the doing, and the stress is the earnestness, the pledge that the verse records, and the promise is the acceptance of the condition, the step that the plan had sought, and the verse is the record of the promise, the bridge between the demand and the return.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the promise, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own pledge. The believer is taught that the promise is the bond, and the one who pledges the doing is the one whom the pledge binds, and the brothers who promised the persuasion were the brothers whose promise the following verses test, and the believer who makes the pledge is the believer whose pledge the Lord witnesses, and the earnestness of the brothers is the measure of the pledge that the trial would weigh. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the weight of the promise: the brothers pledged the doing, and the pledge is the bond, and the believer who pledges the good is the believer whose pledge the Lord records, and the promise of the brothers is the step that the plan of Joseph had arranged. The verse is the record of the promise, and the teaching of the pledge that the believer must honour.",
  "62": "“Joseph ordered his servants to put his brothers’ money back into their saddlebags so that they would find it when they returned to their family and perhaps they would come back.”” records the secret of the money, and the commentators note the plan and its purpose. The verse records the order: “Joseph ordered his servants to put his brothers’ money back into their saddlebags,” or “wa qala li-fityanihi ij‘aloo bida‘atahum fee rihalihim,” and the commentators note the detail, for Joseph commanded his servants to return the money of the brothers into their saddlebags, and the return of the money is the secret kindness, the gift that the brothers would find, and the purpose is stated: “so that they would find it when they returned to their family and perhaps they would come back,” or “la‘allahum ya‘rifoonaha itha inqalaboo ila ahlihim la‘allahum yarji‘oon,” and the commentators note the two purposes, the recognition of the money and the hope of the return, and the return of the money is the generosity that the plan used, the means by which the brothers would be drawn back, and the verse is the record of the secret, the kindness that the wrongdoers received from the wronged, and the commentators note the character of Joseph, for the wronged brother returned the money to the wrongdoers, and the return is the proof of the generosity, the kindness that the station had not corrupted. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the return of the money is the mark of the noble character, and the character of Joseph is the thread of the whole story, the goodness that the trials had not diminished.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the generosity, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own kindness. The believer is taught that the generosity to the wrongdoer is the mark of the noble, and the one who is wronged is the one whose generosity the Lord values, and the return of the money is the kindness that the believer must model, and the believer who is given the station is the believer whose station must not corrupt the character, and the secret of Joseph is the teaching that the good is the thing that the trials and the stations alike cannot erase. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the nobility of the wronged: Joseph returned the money and sought the return, and the believer who is wronged is the believer whose generosity the Lord rewards, and the secret of the money is the proof that the character of Joseph was the character that the well and the prison had purified. The verse is the record of the secret, and the teaching of the generosity that the station must not corrupt.",
  "63": "“When Joseph’s brothers returned to their father, they pleaded, “O our father! We have been denied ˹further˺ supplies. So send our brother with us so that we may receive our measure, and we will definitely watch over him.”” records the plea of the brothers to Jacob, and the commentators note the two parts of the plea. The first is the report: “We have been denied further supplies,” or “ya abana muni‘a minna al-kaylu,” and the commentators note the report, for the brothers told the father that the supply had been withheld, and the report is the pressure, the means by which the brothers sought to convince the father, and the report is the truth, for Joseph had made the supply conditional, and the verse preserves the report as the step of the plan. Ibn Kathir notes the detail, for the brothers reported the condition and the demand, and the report is the persuasion, and the persuasion is the second stage of the plan that Joseph had arranged. The second part is the request: “So send our brother with us so that we may receive our measure, and we will definitely watch over him,” or “fa-arsil ma‘ana akhana naktal wa inna lahu la-hafizoon,” and the commentators note the request, for the brothers asked the father to send Benjamin, and the promise of the guarding is the same promise that they had made for Joseph, and the irony is the key, for the ones who had betrayed the first trust now pledged the second, and the verse preserves the promise as the echo of the first, the repetition of the oath that the father would weigh against the memory.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the trust, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own caution. The believer is taught that the trust that was betrayed is the trust that the heart weighs, and the father who had lost Joseph would weigh the promise of the brothers against the memory of the loss, and the believer who has been betrayed is the believer whose caution the Lord honours, and the promise of the guarding is the promise that the betrayed must examine, and the echo of the first promise is the warning that the believer must remember. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the memory of the betrayal: the brothers pledged the guarding again, and the pledge was the echo of the first, and the believer who has been wronged is the believer whose memory the Lord has not erased, and the caution of Jacob is the wisdom that the memory of the betrayal supplied. The verse is the record of the plea, and the teaching of the caution that the betrayal teaches.",
  "64": "“He responded, “Should I trust you with him as I once trusted you with his brother ˹Joseph˺? But ˹only˺ Allah is the best Protector, and He is the Most Merciful of the merciful.”” records the reply of Jacob, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The first is the question: “Should I trust you with him as I once trusted you with his brother Joseph?” or “qala hal amanukum ‘alayhi illa kama amintukum ‘ala akheehi min qabl,” and the commentators note the question, for Jacob asked the brothers whether he should trust them with Benjamin as he had trusted them with Joseph, and the question is the rebuke, the reminder of the first betrayal, and the question is the answer of the caution, the declaration that the trust that was broken is not the trust that is easily given again, and the verse preserves the question as the wisdom of the wounded heart. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the question is the father’s memory of the loss, and the memory is the ground of the caution, and the caution is the wisdom that the betrayal had taught. The second part is the reliance: “But only Allah is the best Protector, and He is the Most Merciful of the merciful,” or “fallahu khayrun hafizan wa huwa arhamu ar-rahimeen,” and the commentators note the declaration, for Jacob declared that the Lord is the best of the protectors and the most merciful of the merciful, and the declaration is the reliance, the turning of the trust from the creatures to the Creator, and the two names are the comfort, the assurance that the protection of the Lord is the protection that the betrayal of the creatures cannot touch, and the verse is the record of the reply, the caution and the reliance that the faith of Jacob combined.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reliance, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the caution of the betrayed is the wisdom, and the reliance upon the Lord is the comfort, and the one who has been wronged by the creatures is the one who turns to the Creator, and the declaration that the Lord is the best of the protectors is the trust that the believer must hold, and the most merciful of the merciful is the comfort of the one whose heart the betrayal has wounded, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose protection the betrayal cannot breach. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the balance of the caution and the trust: Jacob remembered the betrayal and trusted the Lord, and the believer who is wronged is the believer whose caution is the wisdom and whose trust is the refuge, and the protection of the Lord is the protection that the creatures cannot give. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the reliance that the betrayal drives the believer toward.",
  "65": "“When they opened their bags, they discovered that their money had been returned to them. They argued, “O our father! What more can we ask for? Here is our money, fully returned to us. Now we can buy more food for our family. We will watch over our brother, and obtain an extra camel-load of grain. That load can be easily secured.”” records the discovery of the money and the renewed plea, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the discovery: “When they opened their bags, they discovered that their money had been returned to them,” or “wa lamma fatahoo mata‘ahum wajadoo bida‘atahum ruddat ilayhim,” and the commentators note the detail, for the brothers found the money returned in the bags, and the discovery is the effect of the secret of Joseph, and the discovery is the surprise, the finding of the kindness that the plan had hidden, and the verse records the discovery as the proof of the generosity, the return of the money that the brothers had paid. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the discovery is the turning point, and the turning point is the work of the generosity, and the generosity is the means of the persuasion. The second part is the plea: “O our father! What more can we ask for? Here is our money, fully returned to us. Now we can buy more food for our family. We will watch over our brother, and obtain an extra camel-load of grain,” or “qaloo ya abana ma nabghee hathihi bida‘atuna ruddat ilayna wa nameeru ahlana wa nahfazu akhana wa nazdadu kayla ba‘eerin thalika kaylun yaseer,” and the commentators note the plea, for the brothers used the discovery of the money as the argument, and the argument is the persuasion, the claim that the return of the money was the proof of the generosity of the minister, and the promise of the watching and the extra load, and the verse is the record of the plea, the pressure that the following verse answers with the condition of the oath.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the kindness, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own generosity. The believer is taught that the kindness is the means of the good, and the return of the money was the kindness that persuaded, and the believer who does the good is the believer whose good the Lord uses, and the generosity of Joseph is the proof that the good is the seed that the Lord harvests, and the believer who is generous to the wrongdoer is the believer whose generosity the Lord will turn to the reunion. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the kindness: the money was returned and the hearts were softened, and the believer who is kind is the believer whose kindness the Lord will use, and the discovery of the brothers is the witness that the secret of Joseph was the wisdom that the plan required. The verse is the record of the discovery and the plea, and the teaching of the kindness that the generosity spreads.",
  "66": "“Jacob insisted, “I will not send him with you until you give me a solemn oath by Allah that you will certainly bring him back to me, unless you are totally overpowered.” Then after they had given him their oaths, he concluded, “Allah is a Witness to what we have said.”” records the condition of the oath, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the condition: “I will not send him with you until you give me a solemn oath by Allah that you will certainly bring him back to me, unless you are totally overpowered,” or “qala lan ursilahu ma‘akum hatta tu’tooni mawthiqan mina Allahi la-ta’tunnanee bihi illa an yuhata bikum,” and the commentators note the condition, for Jacob demanded the solemn oath, the “mawthiq min Allah,” the covenant by the Lord, and the exception of the overpowering, and the demand is the caution, the seeking of the binding that the first trust had lacked, and the exception is the realism, the acknowledgment that the overpowering is the limit that the oath cannot cover. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the oath is the precaution, and the precaution is the wisdom of the father, and the exception is the justice, the acknowledgment of the limit of the human power. The second part is the acceptance: “Then after they had given him their oaths, he concluded, ‘Allah is a Witness to what we have said,’” or “fa-lamma atawhu mawthiqahum qala Allahu ‘ala ma naqoolu wakeel,” and the commentators note the conclusion, for Jacob, after the oaths, declared that the Lord is the witness, and the declaration is the reliance, the entrusting of the matter to the witness of the Lord, and the verse is the record of the condition and the acceptance, the caution and the trust that the faith of Jacob combined.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the oath, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own precaution. The believer is taught that the precaution of the oath is the wisdom, and the exception of the overpowering is the realism, and the witness of the Lord is the reliance, and the one who takes the oath and entrusts the matter to the Lord is the one whose caution and trust are the complete, and the believer who is betrayed is the believer whose caution the Lord honours, and the witness of the Lord is the comfort that the believer seeks. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the balance of the precaution and the reliance: Jacob took the oath and trusted the Lord, and the believer who takes the precaution and trusts the Lord is the believer whose matter the Lord guards, and the oath of the brothers is the bond that the following verses test. The verse is the record of the condition, and the teaching of the oath that the trust requires.",
  "67": "“He then instructed ˹them˺, “O my sons! Do not enter ˹the city˺ all through one gate, but through separate gates. I cannot help you against ˹what is destined by˺ Allah in the least. It is only Allah Who decides. In Him I put my trust. And in Him let the faithful put their trust.”” records the instruction of Jacob, and the commentators note the two parts of the instruction. The first is the precaution: “Do not enter the city all through one gate, but through separate gates,” or “ya baniyya la tadkhuloo min babin wahidin wadkhuloo min abwabin mutafarriqah,” and the commentators note the instruction, for Jacob commanded his sons to enter through the separate gates, and the commentators explain the wisdom, for the entry through the separate gates was the precaution against the evil eye, the fear that the sight of the many sons together would attract the envy, and the precaution is the father’s care, the use of the means against the harm, and the instruction is the teaching of the taking of the means. Ibn Kathir notes the detail, for the sons of Jacob were the eleven of the beauty and the strength, and the entry together would attract the eye, and the separate gates were the means against the envy, and the instruction is the wisdom of the precaution. The second part is the reliance: “I cannot help you against what is destined by Allah in the least. It is only Allah Who decides. In Him I put my trust. And in Him let the faithful put their trust,” or “wa ma ughnee ‘ankum mina Allahi min shay’in ini al-hukmu illa lillahi ‘alayhi tawakkaltu wa ‘alayhi fal-yatawakkali al-mutawakkiloon,” and the commentators note the declaration, for Jacob declared that the precaution does not avail against the decree, and the decision belongs to the Lord, and the trust is in the Lord, and the declaration is the balance, the taking of the means with the acknowledgment of the limit, and the verse is the model of the believer’s reliance, the precaution and the trust together, and the commentators note the depth of the verse, for it teaches that the means is taken and the Lord is trusted, and the decree is the Lord’s, and the trust is the believer’s.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the balance, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reliance. The believer is taught that the means is taken and the Lord is trusted, and the precaution is the duty and the decree is the Lord’s, and the one who takes the means and trusts the Lord is the one whose reliance is the complete, and the believer who says “the means avails nothing against the decree” is the believer whose trust is the sound, and the instruction of Jacob is the model of the reliance that the believer must hold. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the completeness of the trust: the separate gates and the trust, and the believer who takes the means and trusts the Lord is the believer whose affair the Lord decides, and the declaration “in Him let the faithful put their trust” is the summons that the believer answers. The verse is the record of the instruction, and the teaching of the balance that the reliance requires.",
  "68": "“Then when they entered as their father had instructed them, this did not help them against ˹the Will of˺ Allah whatsoever. It was just a desire in Jacob’s heart which he satisfied. He was truly blessed with ˹great˺ knowledge because of what We had taught him, but most people have no knowledge.”” records the entry and the assessment, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the entry: “when they entered as their father had instructed them, this did not help them against the Will of Allah whatsoever,” or “wa lamma dakhaloo min haythu amarahum aboohum ma kana yughnee ‘anhum mina Allahi min shay’in,” and the commentators note the meaning, for the sons entered through the separate gates as the father had commanded, and the entry did not avail against the decree, and the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the means is the duty and the decree is the Lord’s, and the means did not change the decree, and the verse records the truth that the previous verse had declared. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the verse is the confirmation of the doctrine, the teaching that the precaution is the human duty and the decree is the divine will, and the two are not the rivals but the companions. The second part is the assessment: “It was just a desire in Jacob’s heart which he satisfied. He was truly blessed with great knowledge because of what We had taught him, but most people have no knowledge,” or “illa hajatan fee nafsi Ya‘qooba qadaha wa innahu la-thoo ‘ilmin lima ‘allamnahu wa lakinna akthara an-nasi la ya‘lamoon,” and the commentators note the two clauses, the desire of the heart that Jacob satisfied, and the knowledge that the Lord had taught him, and the closing lament, the ignorance of the majority, and the verse is the teaching, for the desire of Jacob was the father’s care, and the knowledge was the gift of the Lord, and the two together are the portrait of the prophet, the heart and the knowledge, and the verse declares that most people do not know the balance, and the declaration is the warning against the two extremes, the neglect of the means and the trust in the means.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the balance, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own understanding. The believer is taught that the means is the duty and the decree is the Lord’s, and the desire of the heart is the human and the knowledge is the divine, and the believer who takes the means and trusts the Lord is the believer whose knowledge is the sound, and the two extremes are the two errors, the one who neglects the means and the one who trusts the means, and the believer who holds the balance is the believer whose knowledge the Lord has taught. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the sound doctrine: the entry availed nothing against the decree, and the desire was satisfied, and the believer who takes the means and trusts the Lord is the believer whose knowledge is the complete, and the ignorance of the majority is the warning against the extremes. The verse is the record of the entry, and the teaching of the balance that the knowledge requires.",
  "69": "“When they entered Joseph’s presence, he called his brother ˹Benjamin˺ aside, and confided ˹to him˺, “I am indeed your brother ˹Joseph˺! So do not feel distressed about what they have been doing.”” records the reunion of the two brothers, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the aside: “When they entered Joseph’s presence, he called his brother Benjamin aside,” or “wa lamma dakhaloo ‘ala Yoosufa awa ilayhi akhahu,” and the commentators note the detail, for Joseph drew his full brother Benjamin aside, and the aside is the private reunion, the first meeting of the two brothers after the long years, and the commentators note the tenderness of the scene, for the two full brothers were reunited before the rest, and the aside is the care of Joseph for the brother whom the others had wronged. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the aside is the first step of the full reunion, and the private meeting is the comfort that the brother needed. The second part is the confidence: “I am indeed your brother Joseph! So do not feel distressed about what they have been doing,” or “qala innee ana akhooka fa-la tabta’is bima kanoo ya‘maloon,” and the commentators note the confidence, for Joseph revealed himself to Benjamin, and the revelation is the comfort, the removal of the distress, and the words “do not feel distressed about what they have been doing” are the reassurance, the declaration that the past was the past and the reunion was the present, and the verse is the record of the reunion, the first thread of the full gathering that the following verses complete.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reunion, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own comfort. The believer is taught that the reunion of the loved is the mercy of the Lord, and the comfort of the brother is the care that the believer must give, and the revelation of the truth is the removal of the distress, and the believer who is reunited is the believer whose gratitude the Lord expects, and the words of Joseph to Benjamin are the model of the comfort, the assurance that the past is not the present, and the believer who comforts the distressed is the believer whose comfort the Lord records. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the tenderness of the reunion: Joseph drew Benjamin aside and comforted him, and the believer who reunites with the loved is the believer whose comfort the Lord values, and the distress that Joseph removed is the distress that the believer must not leave upon the other. The verse is the record of the reunion, and the teaching of the comfort that the reunion brings.",
  "70": "“When Joseph had provided them with supplies, he slipped the royal cup into his brother’s bag. Then a herald cried, “O people of the caravan! You must be thieves!”” records the plan of the cup, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the plan: “When Joseph had provided them with supplies, he slipped the royal cup into his brother’s bag,” or “fa-lamma jahhazahum bi-jahazihim ja‘ala as-siqayata fee rahli akheehi,” and the commentators note the detail, for Joseph placed the cup, the “siqayah,” the measuring cup of the king, into the bag of Benjamin, and the placement is the plan, the divine inspiration that the following verse names, and the plan is the means of the keeping of Benjamin, the test that the plan required, and the commentators note that the plan was not the accusation but the means, for the cup was the instrument by which Benjamin would be retained, and the retention was the step of the reunion. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the placement of the cup is the wisdom that the Lord inspired, and the wisdom is the teaching that the means of the good is sometimes the hidden, and the plan of Joseph is the proof that the wisdom of the Lord guides the one whom He chooses. The second part is the cry: “Then a herald cried, ‘O people of the caravan! You must be thieves!’” or “thumma aththana mu’aththinun ayyatuha al-‘eeru innakum la-sariqoon,” and the commentators note the cry, for the herald announced the theft, and the announcement is the public accusation, the stage of the plan, and the verse is the record of the plan and the cry, the opening of the test that the following verses unfold.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the plan, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own wisdom. The believer is taught that the plan of the wise is the means of the good, and the cup of Joseph was the instrument of the reunion, and the believer who is guided by the wisdom of the Lord is the believer whose plans the Lord blesses, and the accusation of the herald was the stage, and the believer who plans with the wisdom must know that the means of the good is sometimes the hidden, and the wisdom of the Lord is the guide that the believer trusts. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the legitimacy of the wise plan: Joseph placed the cup and the herald cried, and the plan was the means of the reunion, and the believer who seeks the good through the wise means is the believer whose means the Lord guides, and the cup of Joseph is the proof that the plan of the wise is the plan that the Lord inspires. The verse is the record of the plan, and the teaching of the wisdom that the means employs.",
  "71": "“They asked, turning back, “What have you lost?”” records the question of the brothers, and the commentators note the question and the scene. The verse records the question: “They asked, turning back, ‘What have you lost?’” or “qaloo wa aqbaloo ‘alayhim matha tafqidoon,” and the commentators note the scene, for the brothers, hearing the accusation of the herald, turned back and asked what was lost, and the question is the innocence, the natural reaction of the ones who did not know the plan, and the question is the opening of the dialogue that the following verses record, and the commentators note the detail, for the brothers turned to the accusers and asked the question, and the question is the first word of the innocent, the seeking of the knowledge of the accusation, and the verse preserves the question as the step of the test, the innocence that the accusation would test. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the question is the first exchange of the scene, and the exchange is the unfolding of the plan, and the question of the brothers is the innocence that the cup would seem to contradict, and the innocence is the irony, for the brothers who had once cast Joseph into the well now stood accused of the theft, and the irony is the teaching, the reversal that the plan had arranged.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the innocence, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trial. The believer is taught that the innocent is sometimes accused, and the question of the accused is the seeking of the truth, and the believer who is falsely accused is the believer whose question the Lord hears, and the irony of the brothers is the teaching, for the ones who had wronged now tasted the accusation, and the believer who trusts the Lord in the accusation is the believer whose innocence the Lord will establish, and the question of the brothers is the first step of the trial that the plan had arranged. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the accused: the brothers asked the question and the accusation followed, and the believer who is accused is the believer whose question is the seeking of the truth, and the irony of the scene is the measure of the reversal that the Lord had arranged. The verse is the record of the question, and the teaching of the innocence that the accusation tests.",
  "72": "“The herald ˹along with the guards˺ replied, “We have lost the King’s measuring cup. And whoever brings it will be awarded a camel-load ˹of grain˺. I guarantee it.”” records the reply and the reward, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The first is the loss: “We have lost the King’s measuring cup,” or “qaloo nafqidu suwa‘a al-maliki,” and the commentators note the detail, for the herald declared the loss of the “suwa‘,” the measuring cup of the king, and the cup is the instrument of the plan, the object that the following verses search for, and the declaration is the public announcement, the statement of the loss that the search would seek. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the cup is the “suwa‘ al-malik,” the royal measure, and the loss is the accusation that the plan had staged, and the announcement is the step of the search. The second part is the reward: “And whoever brings it will be awarded a camel-load of grain. I guarantee it,” or “wa liman ja’a bihi himlu ba‘eerin wa ana bihi za‘eem,” and the commentators note the reward, for the herald promised the camel-load of the grain to the one who brought the cup, and the promise is the offer, the inducement of the search, and the word “za‘eem” — the guarantor — is the pledge of the herald, and the verse is the record of the reply, the announcement of the loss and the reward, the opening of the search that the following verses record.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the plan, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own wisdom. The believer is taught that the plan of the wise has its stages, and the announcement of the loss and the reward is the stage of the plan, and the believer who is guided by the wisdom is the believer whose stages the Lord arranges, and the cup of the king is the instrument, and the reward is the inducement, and the believer who plans with the wisdom must know that the means of the good is the chain that the Lord links. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the detail of the plan: the loss was announced and the reward was offered, and the believer who seeks the good through the plan is the believer whose plan the Lord completes, and the cup of Joseph is the proof that the wisdom of the plan is the wisdom that the Lord inspires. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the stages that the plan employs.",
  "73": "“Joseph’s brothers replied, “By Allah! You know well that we did not come to cause trouble in the land, nor are we thieves.”” records the defence of the brothers, and the commentators note the two parts of the oath. The verse records the defence: “By Allah! You know well that we did not come to cause trouble in the land, nor are we thieves,” or “qaloo tallahi laqad ‘alimtum ma ji’na li-nufsida fi al-ardi wa ma kunna sariqeen,” and the commentators note the oath, for the brothers swore by the Lord that they had not come to corrupt the land and were not the thieves, and the oath is the defence, the appeal to the knowledge of the people of Egypt, and the claim is the truth, for the brothers had not come to corrupt and had not stolen, and the verse preserves the oath as the innocence, the declaration of the brothers that the accusation had not shaken, and the commentators note the irony, for the brothers who had once committed the great wrong now swore the innocence of the small, and the irony is the teaching, the measure of the reversal that the plan had arranged. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the oath of the brothers is the truth of the particular, and the innocence of the theft is the truth that the cup would seem to contradict, and the truth of the particular is the irony that the plan had staged.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the innocence, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own defence. The believer is taught that the defence of the innocent is the oath and the appeal, and the one who is falsely accused is the one whose oath the truth will vindicate, and the believer who swears the innocence is the believer whose innocence the Lord knows, and the irony of the brothers is the warning, for the ones who had wronged now tasted the accusation, and the believer who has wronged is the believer whose past the Lord may return upon him, and the truth of the particular is the thing that the falsehood of the general cannot erase. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the defence of the innocent: the brothers swore and appealed, and the believer who is accused is the believer whose defence the Lord hears, and the oath of the brothers is the step of the trial that the plan had arranged. The verse is the record of the defence, and the teaching of the innocence that the accusation tests.",
  "74": "“Joseph’s men asked, “What should be the price for theft, if you are lying?”” records the question of the men, and the commentators note the question and the law. The verse records the question: “What should be the price for theft, if you are lying?” or “qaloo fa-ma jaza’uhu in kuntum kathibeen,” and the commentators note the question, for the men of Joseph asked the brothers what the penalty of the theft would be if they were the liars, and the question is the step of the plan, the invitation of the brothers to pronounce the law, and the question is the cunning of the plan, the means by which the brothers would condemn themselves, and the commentators note the wisdom, for the question led the brothers to state the law of their own religion, the enslavement of the thief, and the law is the instrument that the plan used to keep Benjamin. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the question is the trap, and the trap is the wisdom of the plan, and the question is the step that the following verse answers with the law, and the law is the means by which the plan was completed, and the verse preserves the question as the stage of the test.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the question, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own wisdom. The believer is taught that the question of the wise is the means of the truth, and the question that led the brothers to pronounce the law was the wisdom of the plan, and the believer who seeks the truth through the question is the believer whose question the Lord guides, and the trap of the question is the cunning that the wisdom uses, and the believer who plans with the wisdom must know that the question is the key that the truth turns. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the question: the men asked and the brothers answered, and the answer was the law that the plan needed, and the believer who asks the wise question is the believer whose question the Lord uses, and the question of the men is the proof that the wisdom of Joseph was the wisdom that the Lord inspired. The verse is the record of the question, and the teaching of the question that the plan employs.",
  "75": "“Joseph’s brothers responded, “The price will be ˹the enslavement of˺ the one in whose bag the cup is found. That is how we punish the wrongdoers.”” records the self-condemnation, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The first is the law: “The price will be the enslavement of the one in whose bag the cup is found,” or “qaloo jaza’uhu man wujida fee rahlihi fa-huwa jaza’uhu,” and the commentators note the law, for the brothers pronounced the penalty of the enslavement, the “jaza’,” the recompense, of the one in whose bag the cup was found, and the law is the law of the religion of Jacob, the enslavement of the thief, and the pronouncement is the self-condemnation, the law that the brothers themselves would be bound by, and the commentators note the wisdom of the plan, for Joseph did not impose the law but let the brothers pronounce it, and the pronouncement is the means by which Benjamin would be kept, and the law is the instrument that the plan used. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the brothers pronounced the law of their own religion, and the law is the enslavement, and the pronouncement is the step of the plan, and the verse preserves the pronouncement as the self-condemnation, the law that the brothers would taste. The second part is the justification: “That is how we punish the wrongdoers,” or “kathalika najzi az-zalimeen,” and the commentators note the justification, for the brothers declared that the law was their punishment of the wrongdoers, and the declaration is the irony, for the brothers who had wronged Joseph now spoke of the punishment of the wrongdoers, and the irony is the measure of the reversal, and the verse is the record of the self-condemnation, the law that the plan had sought.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the law, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own justice. The believer is taught that the law that one pronounces is the law that one will be judged by, and the brothers who pronounced the enslavement were the ones whom the enslavement would bind, and the believer who judges the other is the believer whose judgment the Lord will return, and the justice of the law is the thing that the believer must hold, and the irony of the brothers is the warning, for the ones who spoke of the punishment of the wrongdoers were the ones whose own wrong was the greater. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the weight of the word: the brothers pronounced the law and were bound by it, and the believer who pronounces the judgment is the believer whose judgment the Lord will weigh, and the self-condemnation of the brothers is the teaching that the law is the mirror that the speaker holds. The verse is the record of the self-condemnation, and the teaching of the law that the tongue pronounces.",
  "76": "“Joseph began searching their bags before that of his brother ˹Benjamin˺, then brought it out of Benjamin’s bag. This is how We inspired Joseph to plan. He could not have taken his brother under the King’s law, but Allah had so willed. We elevate in rank whoever We will. But above those ranking in knowledge is the One All-Knowing.”” records the search and the inspiration, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the search: “Joseph began searching their bags before that of his brother Benjamin, then brought it out of Benjamin’s bag,” or “fa-bada’a bi-aw‘iyatihim qabla wi‘a’i akheehi thumma astakhrajaha min wi‘a’i akheehi,” and the commentators note the detail, for Joseph searched the bags of the brothers before the bag of Benjamin, and the order is the wisdom, the concealment of the plan, and the bringing out of the cup from the bag of Benjamin is the completion of the plan, and the verse then states the inspiration: “This is how We inspired Joseph to plan,” or “kathalika kidna li-Yoosufa,” and the commentators note the declaration, for the plan of Joseph was the “kayd,” the inspired plan, the divine guidance, and the declaration is the key, for the verse teaches that the wisdom of Joseph was the gift of the Lord, and the plan was the means that the Lord had arranged, and the verse explains the necessity: “He could not have taken his brother under the King’s law, but Allah had so willed,” or “ma kana li-ya’khuza akhahu fee deeni al-maliki illa an yasha’a Allahu,” and the commentators note the meaning, for the law of the king did not permit the retention of Benjamin, and the plan of the cup was the means that the divine will had arranged, and the verse closes with the principle: “We elevate in rank whoever We will. But above those ranking in knowledge is the One All-Knowing,” or “narfa‘u darajatin man nashao wa fawqa kulli thee ‘ilmin ‘aleem,” and the commentators note the declaration, for the elevation is the gift of the Lord, and the knowledge is the rank, and above every rank of the knowledge is the All-Knowing, and the verse is the teaching, for the wisdom of Joseph was the degree that the Lord had granted, and the Lord is the One whose knowledge is above all.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the inspiration, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own guidance. The believer is taught that the wisdom is the gift of the Lord, and the plan of the believer is the inspiration that the Lord grants, and the elevation of the rank is the will of the Lord, and the believer who seeks the wisdom must seek it from the All-Knowing, and the knowledge of the believer is the degree, and above every degree is the knowledge of the Lord, and the believer who knows this is the believer whose humility the knowledge produces. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the source of the wisdom: the plan of Joseph was the inspiration, and the believer who plans with the wisdom is the believer whose wisdom the Lord granted, and the elevation is the gift, and the knowledge is the trust that the believer must use for the truth. The verse is the record of the search and the inspiration, and the teaching of the wisdom that the Lord grants.",
  "77": "“˹To distance themselves,˺ Joseph’s brothers argued, “If he has stolen, so did his ˹full˺ brother before.” But Joseph suppressed his outrage—revealing nothing to them—and said ˹to himself˺, “You are in such an evil position, and Allah knows best ˹the truth of˺ what you claim.”” records the accusation of the brothers and the patience of Joseph, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the accusation: “Joseph’s brothers argued, ‘If he has stolen, so did his full brother before,’” or “qaloo in yasriq fa-qad saraqa akhun lahu min qabl,” and the commentators note the accusation, for the brothers, to distance themselves, accused Benjamin and Joseph, the two full brothers, of the theft, and the accusation is the slander, the falsehood that the brothers had once used against Joseph, and the verse preserves the accusation as the measure of the character, the return of the old slander, and the commentators note the irony, for the brothers accused Joseph of the theft, and Joseph was the innocent, and the accusation is the old falsehood that the plan had revived. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the accusation is the slander of the innocent, and the slander is the thing that Joseph suppressed, and the suppression is the second part: “But Joseph suppressed his outrage—revealing nothing to them—and said to himself, ‘You are in such an evil position, and Allah knows best the truth of what you claim,’” or “fa-asarraha Yoosufu fee nafsihi wa lam yubdiha lahum qala antum sharrun makana wallahu a‘lamu bima tasifoon,” and the commentators note the suppression, for Joseph concealed his outrage and revealed nothing, and the word to himself is the judgment, the declaration that the brothers were in the evil position and the Lord knew the truth of the claim, and the suppression is the patience, the endurance of the slander, and the verse is the model of the believer who bears the false accusation with the patience and leaves the truth to the Lord.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the patience, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own endurance. The believer is taught that the false accusation is borne with the patience, and the suppression of the outrage is the strength, and the word to oneself is the comfort, the declaration that the Lord knows the truth, and the believer who is slandered is the believer whose truth the Lord knows, and the patience of Joseph is the model, the endurance of the slander without the reply, and the believer who leaves the truth to the Lord is the believer whose truth the Lord will expose. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the strength of the patience: Joseph suppressed and judged in the heart, and the believer who is slandered is the believer whose patience the Lord rewards, and the declaration “Allah knows best” is the comfort of the one whose truth the falsehood attacked. The verse is the record of the accusation and the patience, and the teaching of the endurance that the slander demands.",
  "78": "“They appealed, “O Chief Minister! He has a very old father, so take one of us instead. We surely see you as one of the good-doers.”” records the appeal of the brothers, and the commentators note the two parts of the appeal. The first is the plea: “O Chief Minister! He has a very old father, so take one of us instead,” or “qaloo ya ayyuha al-‘azeezu inna lahu aban shaykhan kabeeran fa-khuth ahadana makanahu,” and the commentators note the plea, for the brothers appealed to the minister, and the appeal is the mention of the old father, the appeal to the mercy, and the offer of the exchange, the taking of one of them instead of Benjamin, and the plea is the first sign of the change, the care of the brothers for the father, and the commentators note the irony, for the brothers who had once caused the grief of the father now pleaded for the sparing of the grief, and the change is the fruit of the trial. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the appeal is the first softening of the brothers, and the softening is the effect of the plan, and the care for the father is the first step of the repentance. The second part is the praise: “We surely see you as one of the good-doers,” or “inna naraka mina al-muhsineen,” and the commentators note the praise, for the brothers praised Joseph as the good-doer, and the praise is the appeal to the character, the hope that the good-doer would show the mercy, and the verse is the record of the appeal, the plea that the following verse answers with the refusal.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the change, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own repentance. The believer is taught that the trial is the school of the change, and the brothers who had wronged the father now cared for the father, and the change is the fruit of the test, and the believer who is tested is the believer whose heart the test may soften, and the care for the father is the first step of the return, and the appeal of the brothers is the proof that the plan of Joseph was the mercy, the means of the repentance that the story records. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the trial: the brothers appealed and cared, and the believer who is tested is the believer whose heart the test softens, and the appeal of the brothers is the first fruit of the plan that the wisdom of Joseph had arranged. The verse is the record of the appeal, and the teaching of the change that the trial produces.",
  "79": "“Joseph responded, “Allah forbid that we should take other than the one with whom we found our property. Otherwise, we would surely be unjust.”” records the refusal of Joseph, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The first is the refusal: “Allah forbid that we should take other than the one with whom we found our property,” or “qala ma‘atha Allahi an na’khuza illa man wajadna mata‘ana ‘indahu,” and the commentators note the refusal, for Joseph refused to take the innocent in place of the one with whom the cup was found, and the refusal is the justice, the declaration that the innocent is not punished for the guilty, and the word “ma‘ath Allah” — the refuge of Allah — is the exclamation of the justice, and the refusal is the principle, the law that the exchange of the innocent for the guilty is the injustice. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the refusal is the justice of Joseph, and the justice is the principle that the believer must hold, and the refusal is the answer to the appeal, the declaration that the law does not bend to the mercy. The second part is the principle: “Otherwise, we would surely be unjust,” or “inna ithan la-zalimoon,” and the commentators note the principle, for the taking of the innocent would be the “zulm,” the injustice, and the verse is the teaching, for the justice is the thing that the believer must hold even against the appeal of the mercy, and the refusal of Joseph is the model of the justice, the holding of the principle against the plea, and the verse preserves the refusal as the proof of the character, the justice that the station had not corrupted.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the justice, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own principle. The believer is taught that the justice is the principle that the believer must hold, and the innocent is not the ransom for the guilty, and the exchange of the innocent for the guilty is the injustice, and the believer who holds the justice against the appeal is the believer whose principle the Lord rewards, and the refusal of Joseph is the model, the justice that the mercy cannot bend, and the believer who is given the station is the believer whose station must serve the justice. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the firmness of the justice: Joseph refused the exchange, and the believer who holds the principle is the believer whose justice the Lord values, and the injustice of the exchange is the thing that the believer must not commit, and the refusal of Joseph is the proof that the good-doer is the just. The verse is the record of the refusal, and the teaching of the justice that the principle demands.",
  "80": "“When they lost all hope in him, they spoke privately. The eldest of them said, “Do you not know that your father had taken a solemn oath by Allah from you, nor how you failed him regarding Joseph before? So I am not leaving this land until my father allows me to, or Allah decides for me. For He is the Best of Judges.”” records the private council, and the commentators note the two parts of the speech. The first is the reminder: “Do you not know that your father had taken a solemn oath by Allah from you, nor how you failed him regarding Joseph before?” or “a-lam ta‘lamoo anna abakum qad akhatha ‘alaykum mawthiqan mina Allahi wa min qablu ma farrattum fee Yoosufa,” and the commentators note the reminder, for the eldest brother, whom the commentators identify as Judah or Reuben, reminded the brothers of the oath and the first failure, and the reminder is the conscience, the memory of the two sins, and the memory is the weight that the brother laid upon the council, and the verse preserves the reminder as the turning, the first open acknowledgment of the failure. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the reminder is the confession of the conscience, and the confession is the first step of the repentance, and the memory of Joseph is the thread that the plan had revived. The second part is the vow: “So I am not leaving this land until my father allows me to, or Allah decides for me. For He is the Best of Judges,” or “fa-lan abraha al-arda hatta ya’thana lee abee aw yahkuma Allahu lee wa huwa khayru al-hakimeen,” and the commentators note the vow, for the eldest declared that he would not leave the land until the father permitted or the Lord decided, and the vow is the resolve, the taking of the responsibility, and the closing praise of the Best of Judges is the reliance, the entrusting of the matter to the Lord, and the verse is the record of the council, the conscience and the resolve that the trial had produced.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the conscience, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own repentance. The believer is taught that the memory of the sin is the conscience, and the reminder of the oath and the failure is the first step of the return, and the believer who remembers the sin is the believer whose repentance the Lord accepts, and the vow of the eldest is the resolve that the repentance requires, and the reliance upon the Best of Judges is the trust that the believer holds, and the council of the brothers is the proof that the plan of Joseph had reached the hearts. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the path of the repentance: the reminder, the memory, the vow, and the trust, and the believer who walks the path is the believer whose return the Lord completes, and the conscience of the brothers is the fruit that the trial had ripened. The verse is the record of the council, and the teaching of the conscience that the trial awakens.",
  "81": "“Return to your father and say, ‘O our father! Your son committed theft. We testify only to what we know. We could not guard against the unforeseen.”” records the instruction of the eldest, and the commentators note the two parts of the instruction. The first is the report: “O our father! Your son committed theft. We testify only to what we know,” or “irji‘oo ila abeekum fa-qooloo ya abana inna ibnaka saraqa wa ma shahidna illa bima ‘alimna,” and the commentators note the report, for the eldest instructed the brothers to tell the father of the theft, and the report is the truth of the appearance, the testimony of what they knew, and the qualification “we testify only to what we know” is the honesty, the limitation of the testimony to the seen, and the report is the step of the return, and the verse preserves the instruction as the teaching of the honesty, the testimony that the believer limits to the knowledge. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the instruction is the honesty of the brothers, and the honesty is the change, and the change is the fruit of the trial. The second part is the admission: “We could not guard against the unforeseen,” or “wa ma kunna lil-ghaybi hafizeen,” and the commentators note the admission, for the brothers confessed that they could not guard against the unseen, and the admission is the humility, the acknowledgment of the limit, and the admission is the echo of the first failure, the recognition that the guarding that they had promised was not in their power, and the verse is the record of the instruction, the honesty and the humility that the trial had produced.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the honesty, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own testimony. The believer is taught that the testimony is the truth of the knowledge, and the limitation of the testimony is the honesty, and the admission of the limit is the humility, and the believer who testifies with the honesty and the humility is the believer whose word the Lord accepts, and the change of the brothers is the proof that the trial had worked, and the believer who is tested is the believer whose honesty the trial reveals. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the discipline of the testimony: the brothers testified to the known and admitted the unknown, and the believer who testifies with the same discipline is the believer whose honesty the Lord values, and the admission of the limit is the humility that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the instruction, and the teaching of the honesty that the trial produces.",
  "82": "“Ask ˹the people of˺ the land where we were and the caravan we travelled with. We are certainly telling the truth.”” completes the instruction, and the commentators note the two parts of the appeal. The first is the appeal to the witness: “Ask the people of the land where we were and the caravan we travelled with,” or “was’ali al-qaryata allatee kunna feeha wal-‘eera allatee aqbalna feeha,” and the commentators note the appeal, for the brothers instructed the father to ask the people of the land and the caravan, and the appeal is the honesty, the offering of the witnesses, and the appeal is the proof of the truth, the confidence of the ones whose account the witnesses would confirm, and the verse preserves the appeal as the teaching, for the honest account is the account that offers the witnesses. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the appeal is the confidence of the honest, and the confidence is the fruit of the change, and the change is the proof that the trial had worked. The second part is the affirmation: “We are certainly telling the truth,” or “wa inna la-sadiqoon,” and the commentators note the affirmation, for the brothers affirmed the truth of the account, and the affirmation is the seal of the honesty, and the verse is the record of the appeal, the confidence of the ones whose account the witnesses would confirm, and the commentators note the irony, for the same brothers who had once brought the false blood now offered the witnesses, and the irony is the measure of the change, the return of the honesty that the trial had restored.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the witness, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own honesty. The believer is taught that the honest account offers the witnesses, and the confidence of the truthful is the proof of the truth, and the believer who speaks the truth is the believer whose account the witnesses confirm, and the change of the brothers is the teaching, for the ones who had lied now offered the proof, and the believer who is tested is the believer whose honesty the trial reveals. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the confidence of the truthful: the brothers offered the witnesses and affirmed the truth, and the believer who speaks the truth is the believer whose confidence the Lord honours, and the appeal of the brothers is the proof that the trial had restored the honesty. The verse is the completion of the instruction, and the teaching of the witness that the honesty offers.",
  "83": "“He cried, “No! Your souls must have tempted you to do something ˹evil˺. So ˹I am left with nothing but˺ beautiful patience! I trust Allah will return them all to me. Surely He ˹alone˺ is the All-Knowing, All-Wise.”” records the reply of Jacob, and the commentators note the three parts of the reply. The first is the diagnosis: “No! Your souls must have tempted you to do something evil,” or “qala bal sawwalat lakum anfusukum amran,” and the commentators note the echo, for the words are the same that Jacob had spoken at the loss of Joseph, and the echo is the wisdom of the father, the knowledge that the account of the theft was the product of the same souls that had produced the first lie, and the diagnosis is the discernment of the prophet, the insight that the truth was hidden behind the report. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the repetition of the phrase is the mark of the father’s wisdom, and the wisdom is the knowledge that the Lord had taught him, and the diagnosis is the first step of the hope. The second part is the patience: “So I am left with nothing but beautiful patience!” or “fa-sabrun jameel,” and the commentators note the phrase, for the beautiful patience is the same that Jacob had declared at the first loss, and the repetition is the constancy of the patience, the endurance that the second loss could not break, and the phrase is the proverb of the patient. The third part is the hope: “I trust Allah will return them all to me. Surely He alone is the All-Knowing, All-Wise,” or “‘asa Allahu an ya’tiyanee bihim jamee‘an innahu huwa al-‘aleemu al-hakeem,” and the commentators note the hope, for Jacob hoped that the Lord would return all of them, the Joseph and the Benjamin and the brother who had stayed, and the hope is the trust, the confidence that the Lord would gather the family, and the closing names, the All-Knowing and the All-Wise, are the ground of the hope, and the verse is the record of the reply, the patience and the hope that the faith of Jacob combined.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the hope, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the patience and the hope are the two companions of the trial, and the one who says the beautiful patience and hopes in the Lord is the one whose trial the Lord will turn to the reunion, and the believer who trusts the Lord with the return of the lost is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the names of the All-Knowing and the All-Wise are the comfort of the one whose loss the Lord has measured. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the completeness of the patience: Jacob diagnosed, endured, and hoped, and the believer who loses and hopes is the believer whose patience the Lord rewards, and the hope of Jacob is the prophecy that the following verses fulfill. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the hope that the patience carries.",
  "84": "“He turned away from them, lamenting, “Alas, poor Joseph!” And his eyes turned white out of the grief he suppressed.”” records the grief of Jacob, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the lament: “He turned away from them, lamenting, ‘Alas, poor Joseph!’” or “wa tawalla ‘anhum wa qala ya asafa ‘ala Yoosufa,” and the commentators note the lament, for Jacob turned away from the sons and lamented Joseph, and the lament is the grief of the father, the sorrow that the second loss had revived, and the word “ya asafa” — alas, the sorrow — is the cry of the heart, and the turning away is the solitude, the withdrawal of the grief from the eyes of the sons, and the verse preserves the lament as the measure of the love, the father whose heart the loss of the son had never healed. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the lament is the grief that the years had not extinguished, and the grief is the love, and the love is the measure of the patience that Jacob bore. The second part is the blindness: “And his eyes turned white out of the grief he suppressed,” or “wabyaddat ‘aynahu mina al-huzni fa-huwa kazeem,” and the commentators note the detail, for the eyes of Jacob turned white, the blindness that the grief caused, and the word “kazeem” — the one who suppresses, the one who chokes the sorrow — is the description of the grief that was held within, and the verse is the record of the grief, the blindness of the weeping and the suppression of the sorrow, and the commentators note that the weeping of Jacob was the weeping of the prophet, the grief that the Lord would reward with the return of the sight, and the verse is the teaching, for the grief of the believer is the thing that the Lord sees, and the sight that the grief took is the sight that the mercy would restore.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the grief, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own sorrow. The believer is taught that the grief of the loss is the human, and the weeping of the father is the love, and the one who weeps for the lost is the one whose tears the Lord counts, and the believer who grieves is the believer whose grief the Lord will turn to the joy, and the blindness of Jacob is the witness of the depth, and the restoration of the sight is the mercy that the following verses record, and the believer who trusts the Lord in the grief is the believer whose grief the Lord heals. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the legitimacy of the grief: Jacob wept and was blind, and the believer who grieves is the believer whose sorrow the Lord understands, and the suppression of the grief is the patience, and the patience is the thing that the Lord rewards with the healing. The verse is the record of the grief, and the teaching of the sorrow that the Lord sees.",
  "85": "“They said, “By Allah! You will not cease to remember Joseph until you lose your health or ˹even˺ your life.”” records the protest of the sons, and the commentators note the two parts of the protest. The verse records the protest: “By Allah! You will not cease to remember Joseph until you lose your health or even your life,” or “qaloo tallahi tafta’u tathkuru Yoosufa hatta takoona haradan aw takoona mina al-halikeen,” and the commentators note the protest, for the sons said that Jacob would not cease the remembrance of Joseph until he wasted away or died, and the protest is the concern, the fear of the sons for the health of the father, and the concern is the first sign of the care, and the protest is the measure of the grief, the sons who saw the father consumed by the memory, and the verse preserves the protest as the witness of the love, the sons whose hearts the grief of the father had touched, and the commentators note the irony, for the sons who had caused the grief now protested its depth, and the irony is the measure of the change, the care that the trial had planted. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the protest is the confession of the depth of the grief, and the confession is the witness, and the witness is the step of the repentance.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the care, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own heart. The believer is taught that the care for the grieving is the duty of the family, and the sons who protested the grief of the father were the sons whose care the trial had awakened, and the believer who sees the grief of another is the believer whose care the Lord values, and the protest of the sons is the first fruit of the change, the concern that the plan of Joseph had planted, and the believer who is tested is the believer whose heart the test softens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the awakening of the care: the sons protested the grief, and the believer who is tested is the believer whose care the trial awakens, and the protest of the sons is the proof that the plan had reached the hearts, and the care is the step of the return. The verse is the record of the protest, and the teaching of the care that the trial plants.",
  "86": "“He replied, “I complain of my anguish and sorrow only to Allah, and I know from Allah what you do not know.”” records the reply of Jacob, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The first is the complaint: “I complain of my anguish and sorrow only to Allah,” or “qala innama ashkoo baththee wa huznee ila Allahi,” and the commentators note the declaration, for Jacob declared that he complained of his anguish and sorrow only to the Lord, and the declaration is the teaching, for the complaint of the believer is directed to the Lord, not to the creation, and the word “innama” — only — is the key, the exclusivity of the complaint, and the verse is the model of the believer’s grief, the sorrow that is poured to the Lord alone, and the commentators note the depth, for the complaint to the Lord is the worship, the turning of the grief into the prayer, and the believer who complains to the Lord is the believer whose complaint the Lord hears. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the verse is the teaching of the etiquette of the grief, and the etiquette is the complaint to the Lord, and the exclusivity is the sincerity, and the sincerity is the key of the answer. The second part is the knowledge: “and I know from Allah what you do not know,” or “wa a‘lamu mina Allahi ma la ta‘lamoon,” and the commentators note the declaration, for Jacob declared that he knew from the Lord what the sons did not know, and the knowledge is the hope, the certainty that the Lord would return Joseph, and the declaration is the prophecy, the knowledge that the sons lacked, and the verse is the record of the reply, the complaint and the knowledge that the faith of Jacob combined.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the complaint, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own grief. The believer is taught that the complaint is directed to the Lord alone, and the exclusivity is the sincerity, and the one who pours the grief to the Lord is the one whose grief the Lord turns to the relief, and the knowledge from the Lord is the hope, the certainty that the believer holds, and the believer who complains to the Lord and hopes in the Lord is the believer whose trial the Lord resolves. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the etiquette of the sorrow: Jacob complained to the Lord and declared the knowledge, and the believer who grieves is the believer whose complaint the Lord hears, and the knowledge from the Lord is the hope that the believer must hold, and the complaint of Jacob is the model of the grief that the worship transforms. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the complaint that the Lord alone receives.",
  "87": "“O my sons! Go and search ˹diligently˺ for Joseph and his brother. And do not lose hope in the mercy of Allah, for no one loses hope in Allah’s mercy except those with no faith.”” records the command of Jacob, and the commentators note the two parts of the command. The first is the command: “Go and search diligently for Joseph and his brother,” or “ya baniyya ithhaboo fa-tahassasoo min Yoosufa wa akheehi,” and the commentators note the command, for Jacob sent the sons to search for Joseph and Benjamin, and the command is the hope, the certainty that the search would find, and the word “tahassasoo” — search diligently, seek — is the instruction, the command of the hope, and the verse preserves the command as the proof of the knowledge that Jacob had declared, the knowledge that Joseph was alive, and the search is the means of the hope. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the command to search is the prophecy, and the prophecy is the knowledge that the Lord had given to Jacob, and the search is the step of the reunion. The second part is the warning: “And do not lose hope in the mercy of Allah, for no one loses hope in Allah’s mercy except those with no faith,” or “wa la tay’asoo min rawhi Allahi innahu la yay’asu min rawhi Allahi illa al-qawmu al-kafiroon,” and the commentators note the warning, for Jacob commanded the sons not to despair of the mercy of the Lord, and the warning is the principle, the declaration that the despair of the mercy is the mark of the disbelievers, and the verse is the great teaching of the hope, the prohibition of the despair, and the commentators note the word “rawh” — the relief, the mercy, the ease — and the declaration that the despair of the relief is the despair of the faithless, and the verse is the model of the hope that the believer must hold, the certainty that the mercy of the Lord is the thing that the believer never despairs of.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the hope, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own patience. The believer is taught that the despair of the mercy is the mark of the faithless, and the hope in the relief is the mark of the believer, and the one who searches with the hope is the one whose search the Lord guides, and the believer who is in the trial is the believer whose hope the Lord will fulfill, and the command of Jacob is the summons, the call to the hope that the believer must never abandon, and the mercy of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the necessity of the hope: Jacob commanded the search and forbade the despair, and the believer who is tested is the believer whose hope the Lord rewards, and the despair of the mercy is the thing that the believer must flee, and the hope of Jacob is the prophecy that the following verses fulfill. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the hope that the believer must hold.",
  "88": "“When they entered Joseph’s presence, they pleaded, “O Chief Minister! We and our family have been touched with hardship, and we have brought only a few worthless coins, but ˹please˺ give us our supplies in full and be charitable to us. Indeed, Allah rewards the charitable.”” records the third entry and the plea, and the commentators note the two parts of the plea. The first is the hardship: “We and our family have been touched with hardship, and we have brought only a few worthless coins,” or “ya ayyuha al-‘azeezu massana wa ahlana ad-durru wa ji’na bi-bida‘atin muzjah,” and the commentators note the plea, for the brothers described the hardship that had touched them and the family, and the few worthless coins that they had brought, and the description is the appeal to the mercy, the confession of the need, and the verse preserves the plea as the humility, the brothers who had once come with the strength now came with the need, and the change is the fruit of the trial. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the plea is the humility, and the humility is the step of the return, and the description of the hardship is the confession that the pride had once concealed. The second part is the request: “but please give us our supplies in full and be charitable to us. Indeed, Allah rewards the charitable,” or “fa-awfi lana al-kayla wa tasaddaq ‘alayna inna Allaha yajzi al-mutasaddiqeen,” and the commentators note the request, for the brothers asked the full measure and the charity, and the appeal to the reward of the charitable is the argument, and the verse is the record of the plea, the need and the request that the following verse answers with the revelation of the truth.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the humility, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own need. The believer is taught that the humility of the need is the step of the return, and the one who confesses the hardship is the one whose confession the Lord hears, and the believer who is in the need is the believer whose need the Lord will meet, and the change of the brothers is the proof that the trial had humbled the pride, and the believer who is tested is the believer whose pride the test breaks. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the value of the humility: the brothers confessed the need, and the believer who is tested is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the plea of the brothers is the prelude of the reunion, and the charity that they sought is the thing that the revelation of the truth would give them. The verse is the record of the plea, and the teaching of the humility that the trial produces.",
  "89": "“He asked, “Do you remember what you did to Joseph and his brother in your ignorance?”” records the revelation of the truth, and the commentators note the question and its effect. The verse records the question: “Do you remember what you did to Joseph and his brother in your ignorance?” or “qala hal ‘alimtum ma fa‘altum bi-Yoosufa wa akheehi ith antum jahiloon,” and the commentators note the question, for Joseph, moved by the plea of the brothers, revealed himself through the question, and the question is the gentle revelation, the mention of the deed and the brothers and the ignorance, and the word “jahiloon” — the ignorant — is the softening, the attribution of the deed to the ignorance rather than the malice, and the question is the mercy of the wronged, the revelation that the brothers would hear without the harshness, and the verse is the moment of the recognition, the turning point that the dream had foretold. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the question is the revelation, and the revelation is the mercy, and the word “jahiloon” is the gentleness, the covering of the deed with the name of the ignorance, and the gentleness is the mark of the noble, and the verse preserves the question as the model of the forgiveness, the revelation that the wronged makes with the mercy.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the revelation, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own forgiveness. The believer is taught that the revelation of the truth is the mercy, and the one who reveals the wrong of the other is the one who reveals it with the gentleness, and the word “in your ignorance” is the softening that the believer must use, and the believer who is wronged is the believer whose revelation the Lord guides, and the question of Joseph is the model, the gentle revealing of the truth that the wrongdoers could bear, and the mercy of the wronged is the thing that the following verses display. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gentleness of the revelation: Joseph asked the question and named the ignorance, and the believer who reveals the truth is the believer whose gentleness the Lord values, and the question of Joseph is the first step of the forgiveness that the story records. The verse is the record of the question, and the teaching of the gentleness that the revelation requires.",
  "90": "“They replied ˹in shock˺, “Are you really Joseph?” He said, “I am Joseph, and here is my brother ˹Benjamin˺! Allah has truly been gracious to us. Surely whoever is mindful ˹of Allah˺ and patient, then certainly Allah never discounts the reward of the good-doers.”” records the recognition, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the shock: “Are you really Joseph?” or “qaloo a-innaka la-anta Yoosuf,” and the commentators note the shock, for the brothers, hearing the question, recognized Joseph, and the recognition is the shock, the astonishment of the ones who had cast the boy into the well and now stood before the minister, and the question of the brothers is the confession, the recognition that the dream had foretold, and the verse preserves the shock as the measure of the surprise, the wrongdoers face to face with the wronged. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the recognition is the fulfillment, and the fulfillment is the proof of the dream, and the shock of the brothers is the first step of the prostration that the stars had foretold. The second part is the reply: “I am Joseph, and here is my brother Benjamin! Allah has truly been gracious to us. Surely whoever is mindful of Allah and patient, then certainly Allah never discounts the reward of the good-doers,” or “qala ana Yoosufu wa hatha akhee qad manna Allahu ‘alayna innahu man yattaqi wa yasbir fa-inna Allaha la yudee‘u ajra al-muhsineen,” and the commentators note the reply, for Joseph confirmed the identity and declared the grace of the Lord, and the principle of the mindfulness and the patience, and the principle is the key of the whole story, the declaration that the reward of the good-doers is the thing that the Lord never discounts, and the verse is the record of the recognition, the reunion that the patience and the mindfulness had earned.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the patience, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reward. The believer is taught that the mindfulness and the patience are the two keys of the reward, and the reward of the good-doers is the thing that the Lord never discounts, and the one who is mindful and patient is the one whose trial the Lord turns to the honour, and the story of Joseph is the proof, the boy of the well and the prisoner becoming the minister, and the believer who holds the two keys is the believer whose reward the Lord completes. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the certainty of the reward: Joseph declared the principle, and the believer who is mindful and patient is the believer whose reward the Lord preserves, and the grace of the Lord is the gift that the patience receives, and the recognition of the brothers is the witness of the principle. The verse is the record of the recognition, and the teaching of the patience that the reward crowns.",
  "91": "“They admitted, “By Allah! Allah has truly preferred you over us, and we have surely been sinful.”” records the confession of the brothers, and the commentators note the two parts of the confession. The verse records the confession: “By Allah! Allah has truly preferred you over us, and we have surely been sinful,” or “qaloo tallahi laqad atharaka Allahu ‘alayna wa in kunna la-khati’een,” and the commentators note the confession, for the brothers admitted the preference of the Lord for Joseph and their own sin, and the confession is the repentance, the first open acknowledgment of the wrong, and the oath “by Allah” is the solemnity, the sealing of the confession with the name of the Lord, and the two parts of the confession — the preference and the sin — are the complete admission, the acknowledgment of the truth and the acknowledgment of the fault, and the verse preserves the confession as the fruit of the trial, the repentance that the plan of Joseph had cultivated, and the commentators note the significance, for the confession is the first of the many confessions that the story records, and the confession is the door of the forgiveness that the next verse opens. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the brothers admitted the preference and the sin, and the admission is the repentance, and the repentance is the thing that the forgiveness receives.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the confession, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own repentance. The believer is taught that the confession of the sin is the first step of the repentance, and the admission of the preference of the Lord is the humility, and the believer who confesses the fault is the believer whose confession the Lord accepts, and the brothers who had wronged confessed, and the confession is the proof that the trial had worked, and the believer who is tested is the believer whose confession the trial produces. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the path of the return: the brothers confessed the preference and the sin, and the believer who confesses the fault is the believer whose return the Lord completes, and the confession of the brothers is the prelude of the forgiveness that the following verse declares. The verse is the record of the confession, and the teaching of the repentance that the trial cultivates.",
  "92": "“Joseph said, “There is no blame on you today. May Allah forgive you! He is the Most Merciful of the merciful!”” records the forgiveness, and the commentators note the two parts of the forgiveness. The first is the pardon: “There is no blame on you today,” or “qala la tathreeba ‘alaykumu al-yawma,” and the commentators note the pardon, for Joseph declared that there was no blame upon the brothers, and the pardon is the forgiveness, the erasure of the wrong, and the word “tathreeb” — the blame, the reproach — is the thing that Joseph removed, and the removal is the complete, the declaration that the wrong was forgiven, and the verse preserves the pardon as the model of the forgiveness, the wronged brother forgiving the wrongdoers without the reproach. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the pardon is the summit of the noble character, and the character is the fruit of the trials, and the forgiveness is the thing that the following verse completes with the prayer. The second part is the prayer: “May Allah forgive you! He is the Most Merciful of the merciful!” or “yaghfiru Allahu lakum wa huwa arhamu ar-rahimeen,” and the commentators note the prayer, for Joseph prayed for the forgiveness of the brothers, and the prayer is the completion of the pardon, the seeking of the divine forgiveness for the wrongdoers, and the closing praise of the Most Merciful of the merciful is the trust, and the verse is the record of the forgiveness, the pardon and the prayer that the noble heart offered.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the forgiveness, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own pardon. The believer is taught that the forgiveness is the summit of the character, and the one who is wronged is the one whose forgiveness the Lord values, and the pardon without the reproach is the model, and the prayer for the forgiveness of the wrongdoer is the completion, and the believer who forgives the one who wronged him is the believer whose forgiveness the Lord will reward, and the story of Joseph is the proof, the wronged brother forgiving the brothers who had cast him into the well. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the nobility of the forgiveness: Joseph pardoned and prayed, and the believer who is wronged is the believer whose forgiveness the Lord honours, and the pardon of Joseph is the summit that the believer must seek, and the Most Merciful of the merciful is the Lord whose mercy the believer mirrors. The verse is the record of the forgiveness, and the teaching of the pardon that the noble heart offers.",
  "93": "“Go with this shirt of mine and cast it over my father’s face, and he will regain his sight. Then come back to me with your whole family.”” records the command of the shirt, and the commentators note the two parts of the command. The first is the shirt: “Go with this shirt of mine and cast it over my father’s face, and he will regain his sight,” or “ithhaboo bi-qameesee hatha fa-alqoohu ‘ala wajhi abee ya’ti baseeran,” and the commentators note the command, for Joseph gave the brothers the shirt and commanded them to cast it over the face of the father, and the promise of the return of the sight, and the commentators note the detail, for the shirt of Joseph was the shirt that the Lord had given, and the casting of the shirt is the means of the healing, and the commentators relate that the shirt was the shirt of Abraham that Gabriel had brought, and the fragrance of the shirt was the fragrance of the Paradise, and the healing is the miracle, and the verse preserves the command as the proof of the knowledge, the certainty that the shirt would restore the sight. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the shirt is the means of the healing, and the healing is the mercy, and the return of the sight is the completion of the grief, and the verse is the bridge between the reunion and the gathering. The second part is the gathering: “Then come back to me with your whole family,” or “wa’toonee bi-ahlikum ajma‘een,” and the commentators note the command, for Joseph asked the brothers to bring the whole family, and the command is the invitation, the summons of the family to Egypt, and the verse is the record of the command, the shirt and the gathering, the two steps of the full reunion.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the mercy, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own healing. The believer is taught that the mercy of the Lord is the thing that heals, and the shirt of Joseph is the means, and the means is the gift of the Lord, and the believer who trusts the Lord in the grief is the believer whose grief the Lord heals, and the return of the sight is the symbol of the return of the hope, and the believer who is in the darkness is the believer whose light the Lord restores. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the mercy: the shirt restored the sight, and the believer who trusts the mercy is the believer whose darkness the Lord turns to the light, and the gathering of the family is the promise that the trials end in the reunion. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the mercy that the shirt carried.",
  "94": "“When the caravan departed ˹from Egypt˺, their father said ˹to those around him˺, “You may think I am senile, but I certainly sense the smell of Joseph.”” records the sense of Jacob, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the sense: “You may think I am senile, but I certainly sense the smell of Joseph,” or “qala innee la-ajidu reeha Yoosufa law la an tufannidoon,” and the commentators note the declaration, for Jacob, as the caravan departed, declared that he sensed the smell of Joseph, and the sense is the knowledge, the gift that the Lord had given, and the word “reeh” — the smell, the fragrance — is the sign, and the commentators note the distance, for the smell reached Jacob from the far caravan, and the sense is the miracle, the knowledge that the grief had not extinguished, and the verse preserves the declaration as the proof of the hope, the father whose certainty the sons had mocked. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the sense of Jacob is the knowledge, and the knowledge is the gift of the Lord, and the sense is the prophecy that the following verses fulfill. The second part is the anticipation: “You may think I am senile,” or “law la an tufannidoon,” and the commentators note the anticipation, for Jacob preempted the accusation of the senility, and the preemption is the awareness of the sons’ judgment, and the verse is the record of the sense, the certainty that the sons would doubt.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the certainty, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the certainty of the believer is the gift of the Lord, and the one who hopes in the Lord is the one whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the sense of Jacob is the proof that the knowledge of the unseen is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who is mocked for the hope is the believer whose hope the Lord vindicates, and the smell of Joseph is the symbol of the approaching relief. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the confidence of the hope: Jacob sensed the smell and preempted the mockery, and the believer who hopes in the Lord is the believer whose hope the Lord confirms, and the sense of Jacob is the witness that the grief had not destroyed the certainty. The verse is the record of the sense, and the teaching of the certainty that the hope carries.",
  "95": "“They replied, “By Allah! You are definitely still in your old delusion.”” records the reply of the people, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: “By Allah! You are definitely still in your old delusion,” or “qaloo tallahi innaka la-fee dalalika al-qadeem,” and the commentators note the reply, for the people, hearing the declaration of Jacob, judged that he was in his old delusion, and the judgment is the mockery, the dismissal of the hope, and the word “dalal” — the delusion, the error — is the accusation, and the word “qadeem” — the old — is the reference to the long grief, and the verse preserves the reply as the measure of the world’s judgment, the mockery of the hope that the Lord had given, and the commentators note the irony, for the delusion that the people mocked was the certainty, and the certainty was the truth, and the truth would be confirmed by the arrival of the shirt, and the verse is the record of the mockery, the doubt of the world that the following verse dissolves. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the reply is the doubt of the people, and the doubt is the contrast with the certainty of Jacob, and the contrast is the teaching, for the believer whose hope the Lord has given is the believer whom the world will mock, and the mockery is the tribute that the falsehood pays.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the mockery, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the hope of the believer is the thing that the world mocks, and the one who hopes in the Lord is the one whom the world judges the deluded, and the believer who is mocked for the hope is the believer whose hope the Lord will vindicate, and the delusion of Jacob was the certainty, and the believer who holds the hope in the face of the mockery is the believer whose hope the Lord confirms. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the mocked: Jacob was judged the deluded and the shirt arrived, and the believer who is mocked for the hope is the believer whose hope the Lord will fulfill, and the mockery of the world is the measure of the certainty that the world lacks. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the hope that the mockery cannot shake.",
  "96": "“But when the bearer of the good news arrived, he cast the shirt over Jacob’s face, so he regained his sight. Jacob then said ˹to his children˺, “Did I not tell you that I truly know from Allah what you do not know?”” records the arrival and the healing, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the healing: “When the bearer of the good news arrived, he cast the shirt over Jacob’s face, so he regained his sight,” or “fa-lamma an ja’a al-basheeru alqahu ‘ala wajhihi fartadda baseeran,” and the commentators note the detail, for the bearer of the good news cast the shirt over the face of Jacob, and the sight returned, and the healing is the fulfillment of the promise of Joseph, and the commentators note the return, for the eyes that the grief had blinded were opened by the fragrance of the shirt, and the healing is the mercy, the completion of the grief that the Lord had measured, and the verse preserves the healing as the proof of the knowledge, the certainty that Jacob had declared. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the healing is the fulfillment, and the fulfillment is the proof of the knowledge, and the return of the sight is the symbol of the return of the hope, and the verse is the record of the mercy. The second part is the vindication: “Did I not tell you that I truly know from Allah what you do not know?” or “qala a-lam aqul lakum innee a‘lamu mina Allahi ma la ta‘lamoon,” and the commentators note the question, for Jacob asked the children whether he had not told them that he knew from the Lord what they did not know, and the question is the vindication, the confirmation of the hope that the world had mocked, and the verse is the record of the healing and the vindication, the certainty that the arrival had confirmed.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the fulfillment, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the hope of the believer is the thing that the Lord fulfills, and the one who hopes in the Lord is the one whose hope the arrival confirms, and the healing of the sight is the symbol of the healing of the grief, and the believer who is in the darkness is the believer whose light the Lord restores, and the question of Jacob is the vindication, the proof that the knowledge of the believer is the truth that the world doubts. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the certainty of the fulfillment: the shirt arrived and the sight returned, and the believer who hopes in the Lord is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the knowledge of Jacob is the thing that the believer must hold, and the mockery of the world is the thing that the fulfillment answers. The verse is the record of the healing, and the teaching of the fulfillment that the hope receives.",
  "97": "“They begged, “O our father! Pray for the forgiveness of our sins. We have certainly been sinful.”” records the plea of the sons, and the commentators note the two parts of the plea. The verse records the plea: “O our father! Pray for the forgiveness of our sins. We have certainly been sinful,” or “qaloo ya abana istaghfir lana thunoobana inna kunna khati’een,” and the commentators note the plea, for the sons asked the father to seek the forgiveness for their sins, and the plea is the repentance, the acknowledgment of the sin, and the confession “we have certainly been sinful” is the admission, the same confession that the brothers had made to Joseph, and the plea is the fruit of the trial, the return of the sons to the father and to the Lord, and the verse preserves the plea as the completion of the repentance, the asking of the forgiveness that the father would seek. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the plea is the return, and the return is the fruit of the plan, and the asking of the forgiveness is the step that the following verse answers with the promise of the prayer.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the repentance, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own return. The believer is taught that the repentance is the asking of the forgiveness, and the confession of the sin is the first step, and the believer who asks the forgiveness of the Lord is the believer whose forgiveness the Lord grants, and the plea of the sons is the proof that the trial had worked, and the believer who is tested is the believer whose repentance the trial produces, and the asking of the father is the model, the seeking of the intercession of the righteous. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the path of the return: the sons confessed and asked, and the believer who confesses the sin and asks the forgiveness is the believer whose return the Lord completes, and the plea of the sons is the fruit that the plan of Joseph had cultivated. The verse is the record of the plea, and the teaching of the repentance that the trial produces.",
  "98": "“He said, “I will pray to my Lord for your forgiveness. He ˹alone˺ is indeed the All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.”” records the promise of Jacob, and the commentators note the two parts of the promise. The first is the promise: “I will pray to my Lord for your forgiveness,” or “qala sawfa astaghfiru lakum rabbee,” and the commentators note the promise, for Jacob promised to seek the forgiveness of the Lord for the sons, and the promise is the acceptance, the response of the father to the plea, and the word “sawfa” — I will, in the future — is the note that the commentators discuss, for the promise was made for the future, and the commentators relate that Jacob delayed the prayer to the time of the dawn, the hour of the acceptance, and the delay is the wisdom, the seeking of the best hour for the prayer, and the verse preserves the promise as the mercy of the father, the intercession that the sons had sought. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the promise is the acceptance, and the acceptance is the completion of the repentance, and the delay of the prayer is the wisdom, and the wisdom is the teaching that the prayer has its hours. The second part is the praise: “He alone is indeed the All-Forgiving, Most Merciful,” or “innahu huwa al-ghafooru ar-raheem,” and the commentators note the two names, the All-Forgiving and the Most Merciful, and the two names are the hope, the trust that the Lord of the forgiveness and the mercy would accept, and the verse is the record of the promise, the intercession that the father offered.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the intercession, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own prayer. The believer is taught that the intercession of the righteous is the mercy, and the promise of the prayer is the acceptance, and the believer who is asked for the prayer is the believer whose prayer the Lord hears, and the two names of the All-Forgiving and the Most Merciful are the hope that the believer holds, and the delay of the prayer to the best hour is the wisdom that the believer must learn. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the value of the intercession: Jacob promised the prayer, and the believer who prays for the other is the believer whose prayer the Lord accepts, and the two names are the comfort of the one whose forgiveness is sought. The verse is the record of the promise, and the teaching of the intercession that the father offered.",
  "99": "“When they entered Joseph’s presence, he received his parents ˹graciously˺ and said, “Enter Egypt, Allah willing, in security.”” records the arrival of the family, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the reception: “When they entered Joseph’s presence, he received his parents graciously,” or “fa-lamma dakhaloo ‘ala Yoosufa awa ilayhi abawayhi,” and the commentators note the reception, for Joseph received the parents, and the reception is the embrace, the gathering of the family, and the commentators note the detail, for the parents are the father Jacob and the mother or the aunt, and the reception is the completion of the reunion, the meeting of the son and the parents after the long separation, and the verse preserves the reception as the tenderness, the embrace that the trials had earned. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the reception is the gathering, and the gathering is the fulfillment of the dream, and the embrace of the parents is the mercy that the following verse completes with the throne. The second part is the invitation: “Enter Egypt, Allah willing, in security,” or “qala udkhuloo Misra in sha’a Allahu amineen,” and the commentators note the invitation, for Joseph invited the family to enter Egypt in the security, and the phrase “in sha’a Allah” — Allah willing — is the submission, the attachment of the entry to the will of the Lord, and the word “amineen” — the secure — is the promise, the safety that the station of Joseph had secured, and the verse is the record of the reception, the embrace and the invitation, the gathering of the family in the security.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the gathering, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reunion. The believer is taught that the reunion of the family is the mercy of the Lord, and the one who is separated from the loved is the one whose gathering the Lord arranges, and the embrace of Joseph and the parents is the proof that the trials end in the reunion, and the believer who trusts the Lord in the separation is the believer whose gathering the Lord completes, and the security of the entry is the promise that the believer's end is the safety. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the hope of the reunion: Joseph received the parents, and the believer who is separated is the believer whose reunion the Lord prepares, and the phrase \"Allah willing\" is the submission that the believer must hold, and the security of the entry is the peace that the patience earned. The verse is the record of the reception, and the teaching of the reunion that the trials complete.",
  "100": "“Then he raised his parents to the throne, and they all fell down in prostration to Joseph, who then said, “O my dear father! This is the interpretation of my old dream. My Lord has made it come true. He was truly kind to me when He freed me from prison, and brought you all from the desert after Satan had ignited rivalry between me and my siblings. Indeed my Lord is subtle in fulfilling what He wills. Surely He ˹alone˺ is the All-Knowing, All-Wise.”” records the prostration and the interpretation, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the prostration: “Then he raised his parents to the throne, and they all fell down in prostration to Joseph,” or “wa rafa‘a abawayhi ‘ala al-‘arshi wa kharroo lahu sujjadan,” and the commentators note the scene, for Joseph raised the parents to the throne and the family prostrated to him, and the prostration is the fulfillment of the dream, the eleven stars and the sun and the moon prostrating, and the commentators note that the prostration was the greeting of the honour, not the worship, and the scene is the completion of the prophecy, the vision that the chapter had opened with, and the verse preserves the scene as the fulfillment, the gathering of the family in the prostration of the greeting. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the prostration is the fulfillment of the dream, and the fulfillment is the proof of the truth, and the scene is the crown of the whole story. The second part is the declaration: “This is the interpretation of my old dream. My Lord has made it come true,” or “qala ya abati hatha ta’weelu ru’yaya min qablu qad ja‘alaha rabbee haqqan,” and the commentators note the declaration, for Joseph declared the fulfillment of the dream, and the declaration is the gratitude, the acknowledgment of the Lord, and the verse continues with the list of the kindnesses: the freeing from the prison, the bringing from the desert, and the closing of the verse with the two names, the Subtle and the All-Knowing, and the commentators note the word “lateef” — the Subtle, the One whose kindness is the hidden — and the verse is the record of the fulfillment, the gratitude of Joseph for the kindness of the Lord, and the closing names are the praise, the acknowledgment of the subtlety and the knowledge that the Lord had displayed.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the fulfillment, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own gratitude. The believer is taught that the dream of the believer is the promise of the Lord, and the fulfillment is the mercy, and the gratitude is the response, and the one who is given the honour is the one whose gratitude the Lord expects, and the story of Joseph is the proof that the trials are the roads of the fulfillment, and the believer who is patient is the believer whose dream the Lord completes, and the subtlety of the Lord is the comfort, the knowledge that the hidden kindness is the thing that the believer trusts. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gratitude of the fulfillment: Joseph declared the interpretation and praised the Lord, and the believer who receives the fulfillment is the believer whose gratitude the Lord values, and the subtlety of the Lord is the thing that the believer must trust, for the kindness that the believer does not see is the kindness that the fulfillment reveals. The verse is the record of the prostration, and the teaching of the fulfillment that the gratitude receives.",
  "101": "““My Lord! You have surely granted me authority and taught me the interpretation of dreams. ˹O˺ Originator of the heavens and the earth! You are my Guardian in this world and the Hereafter. Allow me to die as one who submits and join me with the righteous.”” records the prayer of Joseph, and the commentators note the two parts of the prayer. The first is the acknowledgment: “You have surely granted me authority and taught me the interpretation of dreams. O Originator of the heavens and the earth! You are my Guardian in this world and the Hereafter,” or “rabbi qad ataytanee mina al-mulki wa ‘allamtanee min ta’weeli al-ahadeethi fatira as-samawati wal-ardi anta waliyyee fee ad-dunya wal-akhirati,” and the commentators note the acknowledgment, for Joseph acknowledged the two gifts, the authority and the interpretation, and the prayer is the gratitude, the recognition of the gifts, and the address to the Originator of the heavens and the earth is the praise, and the declaration of the guardianship is the reliance, the trust in the Lord of the two worlds, and the verse is the model of the gratitude, the servant who acknowledges the gifts and trusts the Giver. Ibn Kathir notes the significance, for the prayer is the closing of the story, and the closing is the gratitude, and the gratitude is the thing that the believer must hold at the end of every favour. The second part is the request: “Allow me to die as one who submits and join me with the righteous,” or “tawaffanee musliman wa alhiqnee bis-saliheen,” and the commentators note the request, for Joseph asked to die as the submitter, the Muslim, and to be joined with the righteous, and the request is the summit of the hope, the death upon the faith and the company of the righteous, and the verse is the record of the prayer, the gratitude and the hope that the story of Joseph closes with, and the commentators note that the prayer is the best of the endings, the servant who asks the good end and the good company.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the prayer, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the gratitude is the response to the favour, and the hope of the good end is the request of the believer, and the one who is given the authority and the knowledge is the one whose prayer is the gratitude, and the believer who asks the death upon the submission and the company of the righteous is the believer whose hope the Lord grants, and the story of Joseph is the proof that the trials end in the gratitude, and the believer who is patient is the believer whose end the Lord makes the good. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the closing of the favour: Joseph acknowledged and asked, and the believer who receives the favour is the believer whose gratitude and hope the Lord accepts, and the prayer of Joseph is the model of the good end, the death upon the faith and the joining with the righteous. The verse is the record of the prayer, and the teaching of the gratitude and the hope that the story closes with.",
  "102": "\"That is from the stories of the unseen which We reveal to you ˹O Prophet˺.\" steps outside the narrative now that it has ended, and speaks about the narrative itself. The story is complete, and the Quran returns to the point it made before beginning.\n\nThe Arabic term for the unseen covers what lies beyond human perception, including a past that nobody alive witnessed and no reliable record preserved.\n\n\"You were not present when they ˹all˺ made up their minds, and when they plotted ˹against Joseph˺.\" specifies the claim with precision. The Prophet was not there when the brothers reached their decision and worked out their scheme.\n\nThis is a striking choice of moment. Of everything in the chapter, the Quran singles out the private conversation among the brothers, the discussion nobody outside that group ever heard. Even Jacob never learned exactly what was said. Yet the chapter reported it in detail: their complaint about their father's favour, the proposal to kill, the alternative of the well, the plan to repent afterwards.\n\nThe argument is straightforward. Knowledge of a private conversation from centuries earlier, in another land, could only come from one source. The Prophet had no access to Jewish scriptures, could not read, and lived among people equally unfamiliar with these accounts in detail. The version preserved in the older texts differs from this one in significant ways, which makes the suggestion of borrowing difficult to sustain.\n\nThe verse also has an effect on how the whole chapter should be received. This has been a long, absorbing story with characters, dialogue, and a satisfying resolution. It would be easy to enjoy it as a story and move on. The reminder that it is revelation about real events, delivered as evidence, prevents that.\n\nFor the Meccan audience the point was immediate. A man they had known all his life was reciting the private deliberations of ancient people, and they had no explanation for where it came from.",
  "103": "\"And most people will not believe—no matter how keen you are\" turns from the story to the Prophet's situation, and it is a sobering line to place after such a complete and satisfying account.\n\nThe chapter has just delivered the finest of stories, filled with evidence of Allah's planning, forgiveness, patience, and reunion. And the verse that follows says most people will remain unconvinced.\n\nThe Arabic indicates the Prophet's eagerness, his intense desire for them to believe. This was not a man delivering a message reluctantly. Other verses describe him almost destroying himself with grief over their refusal, and Allah addressing that grief directly.\n\nThe verse does two things at once. It tells him the truth about what to expect, and it releases him from responsibility for the outcome. Both are acts of kindness.\n\nThere is a lesson here about the relationship between evidence and belief. If persuasion depended on the quality of the argument, this chapter should have converted everyone who heard it. It did not, and the Quran states plainly that it would not.\n\nThe reason lies in what the earlier chapters explained repeatedly. People whose refusal has hardened do not respond to proof, and belief involves the will as much as the mind. A person unwilling to accept the consequences of a truth will find a way past any amount of evidence.\n\nFor anyone who calls others to good today, whether a parent, a teacher, or a friend, this verse addresses a familiar disappointment. Presenting something clearly and beautifully, with genuine care for the person receiving it, does not guarantee acceptance.\n\nThat reality can produce bitterness, exhaustion, or the temptation to compromise the message to make it more appealing. The Quran offers a different response: keep delivering it honestly, expect that many will refuse, and understand that the refusal is not a verdict on the message or on you.\n\nThe following verse adds the detail that removes any excuse the refusers might have offered.",
  "104": "\"Even though you are not asking them for a reward for this ˹Quran˺\" removes the most obvious objection anyone could raise against the Prophet, and it does so at the very end of the chapter.\n\nA person hearing a religious message naturally asks what the messenger stands to gain. Payment, influence, and status are the usual answers, and any of them would weaken the message considerably. The Quran points out that none applies here.\n\nThis same declaration was made by Noah, Hud, Salih, Lut, and Shu'aib in the preceding chapter, each telling his people that his reward was only with Allah. The repetition establishes it as a mark of genuine prophethood.\n\nIn Mecca the claim was open to inspection. The Prophet had been offered wealth and leadership in exchange for stopping, and he refused. He endured boycott, insult, and the loss of his protection, and he collected nothing for any of it.\n\n\"It is only a reminder to the whole world.\" states what the Quran is and who it is for.\n\nThe word for reminder is significant. It suggests that the message is not entirely new information but a recalling of something people already have some sense of: that there is a Creator, that life has a purpose, that actions have consequences. Revelation awakens what heedlessness has buried.\n\n\"To the whole world\" defines the audience, and the Arabic covers all peoples and all times. This is a substantial claim for a message delivered in Arabic in a small trading town, to an audience of a few hundred, most of whom were hostile.\n\nPlacing it here, at the close of a chapter about a Hebrew prophet in Egypt, told to Arabs in Mecca, and read now across every continent, gives the claim a certain weight. The story of Joseph belongs to no single nation, and neither does the reminder it was sent to deliver.",
  "105": "\"How many signs in the heavens and the earth do they pass by with indifference!\" is an exclamation rather than a question, and it captures one of the strangest features of human life.\n\nThe signs are described as being everywhere, above and below, and the point of the verse is not that people cannot see them. It is that they walk past them. The Arabic indicates turning away from something while it is right there in front of you.\n\nFamiliarity is the mechanism. A person who has watched the sun rise ten thousand times stops noticing it. Rain becomes weather rather than a miracle of water lifted from the sea and dropped on dry land. A newborn child is a wonder for a few weeks and then becomes routine. Nothing about the signs has changed; the attention has faded.\n\nThis is why the Quran keeps returning to ordinary things, the alternation of night and day, the growth of plants from dead earth, the differences between people's languages and colours. It is not trying to inform anyone that these things exist. It is trying to make people look at what they have stopped seeing.\n\nPlacing this verse immediately after the story of Joseph is deliberate. That story was full of moments where Allah's arrangement was invisible to the people inside it: a caravan stopping for water, a forgotten promise, a cup in a bag. Only from the end can the pattern be seen.\n\nThe same is true of the world itself. Signs are not rare events that interrupt normal life. They are normal life, examined honestly.\n\nThere is a practical remedy implied in the complaint. Attention can be trained. A person who deliberately pauses over what he usually ignores, a plant, a hand, a night sky, is doing something the Quran repeatedly instructs. Surah Ali Imran describes people of understanding as those who remember Allah standing, sitting, and lying on their sides, and reflect on the creation of the heavens and the earth.",
  "106": "\"And most of them do not believe in Allah without associating others with Him ˹in worship˺\" makes a claim that surprises many readers when they first understand it.\n\nIt says that most believers in God do not believe purely. Their belief is mixed with something else.\n\nThis describes the situation in Mecca precisely. The Quraysh were not atheists. They acknowledged Allah as creator, provider, and controller of rain and life, and the Quran records them saying so. What they added was a layer of intermediaries: idols they said would bring them closer to Allah, deities they invoked for particular needs, and inherited practices that assigned some of His rights to others.\n\nThe Arabic word \"shirk\" means associating a partner with Allah, giving to something created what belongs only to the Creator. It does not require denying God. It requires dividing what should be undivided.\n\nUnderstanding this widens the verse considerably. Openly worshipping a statue is only the most visible form. Scholars have long discussed subtler forms, including performing worship for the sake of people's admiration, which the Prophet described as the hidden form of associating partners in a hadith recorded by Ahmad. Placing ultimate trust in wealth, in a person, or in one's own cleverness belongs to the same family of errors.\n\nThere is a useful test in this for anyone examining their own belief. It is not enough to ask whether a person believes in Allah, since most of humanity always has. The question is whether anything else has been allowed to share His place: as the source of provision, the object of real fear, the thing whose approval genuinely governs behaviour.\n\nPlacing this verse after the story of Joseph gives it context. Joseph in the prison had put the same question to two men who certainly believed in gods, asking whether many scattered lords were better than the One, the Supreme. That was the argument then, and this verse says it remains the argument now.",
  "107": "\"Do they feel secure that an overwhelming torment from Allah will not overtake them?\" asks about a particular kind of confidence, and it identifies a state of mind rather than a belief.\n\nThe people described are not necessarily denying that punishment exists. They feel safe from it. The Arabic indicates being secure, untroubled, free of any concern that something might come.\n\nThat sense of security is the target of the verse. It develops naturally when nothing has happened for a long time. Life continues, trade prospers, and the warnings people once heard begin to sound like background noise.\n\nThe Arabic word for the torment described here indicates something that covers and envelops, arriving over a whole community rather than picking off individuals.\n\n\"Or that the Hour will not take them by surprise when they least expect ˹it˺?\" adds the second possibility. Beyond any punishment within history lies the Hour itself, and its defining feature in the Quran is suddenness. It arrives while people are in the middle of ordinary business, as other verses describe, with no announcement.\n\nThe pairing of these two questions covers every timescale. Something may come to a community, and something will certainly come to everyone.\n\nThere is a state of mind the Quran consistently recommends instead, which is neither despair nor complacency. Islamic teaching describes the believer as living between hope and fear, expecting Allah's mercy while never assuming immunity. Both are necessary. Fear alone crushes a person; security alone makes him careless.\n\nThe Quran mentions this false security elsewhere in similar terms, noting that none feels safe from the plan of Allah except the people who are losers.\n\nFor the Meccan audience, the question had a particular edge. They lived beside the ruins of destroyed peoples and travelled past them regularly, and they still assumed that whatever happened to those nations had nothing to do with them.",
  "108": "\"\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"This is my way.\"\" is a declaration of position, and it comes at the point where the chapter turns from the story to the mission.\n\nThe Arabic word for way indicates a path or road, something with a direction and a destination. He is not describing an opinion but a course of life.\n\n\"I invite to Allah with insight\" defines the method, and both halves matter. The invitation is to Allah, not to a personality, a tribe, or a political programme. And it is offered with insight, using an Arabic word that indicates clear-sighted understanding, knowing what you are calling to and why.\n\nThat second element is a standard the Quran sets for anyone who speaks in the name of religion. Enthusiasm without knowledge causes damage, and history is full of well-meaning people who misrepresented what they were defending. The verse ties calling to Allah with actually understanding the matter.\n\n\"I and those who follow me\" extends the responsibility beyond the Prophet. Everyone who follows him shares this work, according to their ability and their knowledge. This is not a task reserved for scholars, though it does require that a person speak only about what he genuinely knows.\n\nThere is a balance in that. The verse does not say that only experts may speak, and it does not licence anyone to speak about anything. Each person conveys what he understands, with insight into that much.\n\n\"Glory be to Allah\" declares Him free of every fault and every partner, which is the content of the invitation in a single phrase.\n\n\"And I am not one of the polytheists.\" draws the line at the end, exactly as the preceding chapters did. The message is defined by what it affirms and by what it refuses.\n\nA believer today can read this verse as a job description. Know what you are talking about, invite people to Allah rather than to yourself, and keep your own belief clear of anything that competes with Him.",
  "109": "\"We only sent before you ˹O Prophet˺ men inspired by Us from among the people of each society\" answers an objection the Meccans raised repeatedly: that a messenger from God should not be an ordinary man from their own streets.\n\nThree points are packed into the sentence. Messengers were men, not angels. They received revelation rather than inventing their message. And they came from among the communities they addressed, so nobody could claim the messenger did not understand them.\n\nThe wording also places the Prophet in a line rather than presenting him as an innovation. Everything the Meccans found strange about him was true of every prophet before him.\n\n\"Have the deniers not travelled through the land to see what was the end of those ˹destroyed˺ before them?\" turns to evidence they could examine physically. The Quraysh were merchants whose caravans passed the ruins of earlier peoples on the routes north and south.\n\nThe invitation is to travel and look, which the Quran issues several times. This is not an appeal to accept a claim on authority. It is an appeal to observe what remains of communities that made the same choices.\n\n\"And surely the ˹eternal˺ Home of the Hereafter is far better for those mindful ˹of Allah˺.\" shifts from the ruins to the comparison that matters. Whatever those nations built, and some built impressively, none of it lasted. What is offered to those who are mindful of Allah does.\n\n\"Will you not then understand?\" closes with the same appeal to reason found throughout these chapters. The Arabic asks whether they will use their intellect.\n\nThat is a striking way to end an argument about faith. The Quran does not ask them to suspend their thinking. It asks them to apply it: look at history, notice the pattern, compare what lasts with what does not, and draw the conclusion themselves.",
  "110": "\"And when the messengers despaired and their people thought the messengers had been denied help, Our help came to them ˹at last˺\" describes the lowest point in a prophet's mission, and it is an unusually honest verse.\n\nThe messengers reached a stage of despair. Commentators have discussed this carefully, since prophets do not despair of Allah's mercy. The explanation generally given is that they despaired of their people responding, having exhausted every approach over many years, and that they had no expectation of when relief would arrive.\n\nThere is an alternative reading of the Arabic in which it is the people who assumed the messengers had been lied to, but the sense of the verse is the same either way: a point of maximum darkness, when nothing suggested that help was coming.\n\nThe chapter just concluded illustrates this precisely. Joseph in the well, Joseph in the prison after being forgotten for years, Jacob blind and mocked as senile. At no point in any of those situations was rescue visible.\n\n\"Our help came to them ˹at last˺\" is the turn, and its placement is the entire lesson. Help arrives at the point when human calculation has run out, not before.\n\nThis tells us something about how Allah's assistance works, and it is not what most people would design. Relief that comes early confirms a person's own planning. Relief that comes at the end confirms something else.\n\n\"We then saved whoever We willed\" describes the outcome for the believers. The wording preserves Allah's freedom in the matter, and the chapters preceding this one showed prophets and their followers being rescued while everyone else was removed.\n\n\"And Our punishment is never averted from the wicked people.\" completes the pair. Nothing turns it back once it comes.\n\nFor the Prophet's community in Mecca, living under boycott with no visible prospect of change, this verse was telling them exactly where they stood in the sequence: at the point before the help arrives.",
  "111": "\"In their stories there is truly a lesson for people of reason.\" closes the chapter by returning to the purpose stated near its beginning. The Arabic word for lesson indicates something a person crosses over from, moving from the surface of an event to the meaning behind it.\n\nThe word \"reason\" identifies who benefits. The Quran repeatedly attaches its evidence to those willing to think, and this verse ends a chapter that has given them a great deal to consider.\n\n\"This message cannot be a fabrication\" states the claim directly. A story of this length, detail, and coherence, delivered by an unlettered man in Mecca, including private conversations from centuries earlier in another land, does not have a human explanation.\n\n\"Rather ˹it is˺ a confirmation of previous revelation\" gives the first of four descriptions. The Quran agrees with the essential content of what was revealed before, and the story of Joseph was known in outline to the Jewish communities of Arabia, though this version differs from theirs in important ways.\n\n\"A detailed explanation of all things\" is the second. The Arabic indicates laying matters out clearly, part by part. This does not mean the Quran contains every fact about every subject; it means it explains fully what people need for guidance.\n\n\"A guide\" is the third, pointing to the road. Explanation without direction leaves a person informed and still lost.\n\n\"And a mercy for people of faith\" is the fourth and final description, and it ends the chapter where the story itself ended: with mercy.\n\nLooking back over the chapter, that word covers all of it. A boy rescued from a well, a man protected in a locked room, a prisoner remembered at exactly the right moment, a family reunited after decades, and brothers forgiven by the person they had wronged most.\n\nThe Quran calls itself a mercy, and it has just spent a hundred and eleven verses demonstrating what that looks like in a single human life."
};
